WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.820
- There are decades where nothing happens
and weeks where decades happen.

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:09.560
And we have seen decades of progress

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:13.810
happen in the last nine months.
If you're not recognizing the

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:18.640
gravity of the moment, that's
the delusion. That's the

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:22.080
psychosis. Who would not get
delirious if suddenly a genie

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.960
pops out of the bottle and says,
"You can have whatever you want.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.930
Every feature you've ever
dreamed of in an operating

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:31.520
system, I can deliver them to you,

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:36.420
most of them in five minutes, a few in
20, and if we really go hog wild, it's

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:40.330
gonna take me two hours." I want
the fastest car breaking the speed

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:44.040
limits. I want the diver watch
that can go down the Mariana

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:47.820
Trench. I want the operating system
that can install in less than

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.820
60 seconds. One of the things I always
loved about race cars was when I

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.760
would stumble out of the car
absolutely smashed and barely able to

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.380
hold my head up, and I'd lay down
on the garage floor and just think,

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.420
"Holy fuck, I'm alive." The
Overton window does not open

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:09.830
itself. It opens one nudge at a
time by people risking a little.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:13.800
A little reputation, a little pushback,
a little criticism, or maybe sometimes a

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.390
lot of reputation or a lot of criticism
or a lot of pushback. Creating

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:20.640
life with another human that you love

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.460
is literally the peak experience
of being on the planet.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:32.040
- The following is a conversation
with David Heinemeier Hansson, also

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:40.240
known as DHH, creator of Ruby on Rails, CTO
of 37signals, creator of the new Omarchy

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:43.820
Linux operating system, best-selling
author, race car driver,

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:48.000
and one of the most outspoken
and prolific programmers

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.420
in the world. For the past
20-plus years, for him,

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:55.580
programming meant meticulously
handcrafting beautiful Ruby

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:03.360
code. But recently, since late 2025, DHH
has embraced the AI revolution and has

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:07.210
openly transformed himself
into, once again, one of the

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:11.180
most outspoken and prolific
agentic engineers, even

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.440
though he hates that term. So let's say

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.120
practitioners of whatever
programming is becoming where AI is

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:23.020
doing most of the actual programming
and the human steers the ship

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.160
with high-level design,
vision, and taste. Yes, it's

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.180
true. DHH is at times
controversial, but he's

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:35.230
always fearless, brilliant,
and fun to talk to. This was,

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.860
once again, an intense,
eye-opening, and wild

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:42.820
rollercoaster ride of a
conversation. This is a Lex Fridman

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.460
podcast. To support it, please check
out our sponsors in the description

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:50.700
where you can also find links to
contact me, ask questions, give

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:55.240
feedback, and so on. And now,
dear friends, here's DHH.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.570
13 months ago, we sat down right
here to talk about programming,

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.720
and then everything changed.
At that time, you were

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:08.480
a bit skeptical about the
role of AI in the process of

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.940
programming, and then we went
through this rapid evolution

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:19.960
of agentic engineering. So, simple question
to start: How has your view on AI's

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:23.980
role in programming changed? Are you
excited? Are you terrified? Are you

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:25.880
on an emotional rollercoaster ride?

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:28.700
- I am incredibly excited.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.140
- You've been talking about fun a lot.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:38.290
- There is none of the existential threat.
That doesn't exist for me as an emotional

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.800
component. It is only there as an
intellectual component, and the

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:47.130
emotional component for me is
100% pure, unadulterated joy-

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.990
... and optimism and
amazement that we've made

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:54.980
computers do this. And I find it
so interesting that we talked

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.609
just 13 months ago because

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:02.769
it's like we talked in different
universes, different eras.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:06.620
I love this quote. I think
it's Lenin. There are

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:10.100
decades where nothing happens
and weeks where decades happen.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:17.340
And we have seen decades of progress
happen in the last nine months.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.700
I mean, imagine you're there when
the Wright brothers take flight.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:25.810
Yesterday, the New York Times would write,
"It's gonna be 10,000 years before we fly,"

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:29.960
and then the day after, we're
up in the skies, and just a

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:33.840
few years after that, there
were cross-Atlantic planes. The

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:37.760
whole world has completely
changed. What a blessing to be

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:41.720
there in that moment. If you zoom out and
look at all of human history, how many

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:49.130
humans got to live within the same epoch
that they were born in? They never saw that

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:54.320
complete change of the world
and of society. And to have

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:56.960
been blessed with two of those

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:02.039
feels just such a privilege.
I got to see the internet-

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:08.640
... from pre-internet to post-internet,
and then now pre-AI, post-AI.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:12.080
What an amazing run. How fortunate.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.010
- Yeah, but the thing is, this
feels like a thing that

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.280
happened faster than anything
else in human history.

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:18.660
- Correct.

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:22.270
- So I would say somewhere around
December, maybe late November-

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:23.740
- November 24.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:25.160
- This is...

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:26.310
- That's the exact moment.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:30.280
- You know, when they talk about when
one nation invades another in a world

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:34.300
war or something like this, it-- this is
how we talk about AI changing everything.

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:38.240
Yeah, the... And it really
just sh- shifted for many

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:42.260
great developers. It shifted
to where AI is doing some,

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:49.490
basic autocomplete, maybe writing 5, 10,
15, 20% of code to writing 80% of code.

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:50.150
- Or 100.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:54.150
- Or 100, yeah, especially if it's
not a public-facing product.

00:05:54.968 --> 00:05:58.728
- To me, that's why even when I look

00:05:58.908 --> 00:06:02.748
back upon our conversation a year
ago, I don't actually have different

00:06:02.768 --> 00:06:06.468
opinions. I have the same
opinions. A year ago, I did

00:06:06.548 --> 00:06:10.468
not like the mode of AI we were
offered. It was the autocomplete mode,

00:06:10.868 --> 00:06:14.788
or it was the AI chatbot
mode. Now, the chatbot

00:06:14.828 --> 00:06:18.748
I actually liked, as we talked about.
Great tutor right from the get-go, great

00:06:18.788 --> 00:06:20.368
way of looking things up on the internet,

00:06:21.468 --> 00:06:28.428
not what was gonna replace me chiseling
code. But- ... then we get the agents.

00:06:28.708 --> 00:06:32.408
And the agents start out being curiosities

00:06:32.468 --> 00:06:37.837
for about five minutes, and then
they get amazing, and then they get,

00:06:38.788 --> 00:06:44.517
"Oh my God, is this AGI?" And all
of that happened since last year,

00:06:45.028 --> 00:06:48.648
even just within the last
nine months. We have

00:06:48.688 --> 00:06:52.638
basically these few phases
here. We have AI in the

00:06:53.048 --> 00:06:56.868
pre-agentic era. I was
excited about that, but it

00:06:56.928 --> 00:07:00.898
was not fundamentally rewriting the
rules of the game for me. It was

00:07:01.028 --> 00:07:04.468
not completely changing how I
worked. I was still chiseling

00:07:04.648 --> 00:07:08.868
code, I just had a little
helper, a little sidekick-

00:07:08.878 --> 00:07:12.808
... who could bounce ideas
off, and I could look up this

00:07:13.828 --> 00:07:17.638
information online and so forth in a more efficient
way. It was just a more efficient way to do what I was

00:07:17.688 --> 00:07:21.588
already doing, and it didn't change
the emotional connection I had to the

00:07:21.608 --> 00:07:29.508
computer. Then we get to November
24th, 2025. Opus 4.5, to

00:07:29.568 --> 00:07:33.368
me, was the dividing line,
where suddenly... I didn't even

00:07:33.428 --> 00:07:39.388
try it on the 24th. I think I tried it on
the 26th. I give it a couple of tasks,

00:07:40.308 --> 00:07:47.768
and I realize that the quality of
the output is uncannily close to

00:07:47.808 --> 00:07:53.398
what I would've written. And I remember
just leaning back and thinking,

00:07:54.158 --> 00:07:59.408
"What just happened?" "How did we
go from this autocomplete mess that

00:07:59.468 --> 00:08:03.227
I was talking to you about
in the summer to this just a

00:08:03.287 --> 00:08:06.908
few short months later? How did
we get both the increase in

00:08:06.968 --> 00:08:09.268
intelligence and then also the increase in

00:08:10.388 --> 00:08:14.148
usability?" This agent
harness question where

00:08:15.668 --> 00:08:19.628
I don't know if Opus 4.5 was that
much smarter than Opus 4, which

00:08:19.688 --> 00:08:23.548
is what we had in the
summer, but its ability to

00:08:23.608 --> 00:08:27.328
instrument your computer, to use
tools, to check its own work,

00:08:27.868 --> 00:08:31.648
to apply its intelligence in
such a way that you could

00:08:31.708 --> 00:08:37.308
get real meaningful work out of it, was
completely different. And I think this is

00:08:38.168 --> 00:08:41.848
then the big change that happens for almost anyone
who paid attention and started playing with

00:08:41.868 --> 00:08:44.608
it over the Christmas break.
This is what I heard-

00:08:44.668 --> 00:08:47.788
... from Shopify and other places
with lots of employees who

00:08:49.108 --> 00:08:53.098
suddenly had a breather, suddenly had a couple
of weeks to lean back and just look at what

00:08:53.128 --> 00:08:57.188
was going on, gave it a
try, and had the same

00:08:58.028 --> 00:09:01.968
mind-blowing experience
that these agents were of

00:09:01.988 --> 00:09:07.028
a different genre than what we had
before. And then what we get to is,

00:09:08.668 --> 00:09:12.608
I'm already excited at this
point. Like, by December, I'm

00:09:12.668 --> 00:09:15.548
already revisiting sort of all my priors-

00:09:15.588 --> 00:09:18.628
... and going like, "Wow, if it
can do this, can it also do that?"

00:09:18.808 --> 00:09:26.668
"Oh, yeah, it can." And again, Opus
4.5 now looks like a retarded model.

00:09:26.838 --> 00:09:32.688
And this is the magic of this progress,
is you think you've reached something...

00:09:32.828 --> 00:09:34.828
I remember thinking at the moment,

00:09:35.678 --> 00:09:39.838
"If this is the last model we
get, I'll be set. I'll be happy."

00:09:39.928 --> 00:09:42.758
I could live with Opus 4.5
for the next 20 years-

00:09:42.758 --> 00:09:47.528
... and you would hear no complaints from
me because it was just so incredibly

00:09:48.488 --> 00:09:52.268
amazing to see an agent do all this
work in the way that I wanted it

00:09:52.428 --> 00:09:55.878
done. Because it was not just
about it being able to solve a

00:09:55.948 --> 00:09:59.788
task, it was also that I could look at its

00:09:59.848 --> 00:10:03.678
path there and go, "Yep.
Yep. Maybe not there,

00:10:03.768 --> 00:10:07.748
but almost," and it would give you two
notes. You'll get to where I wanted

00:10:07.788 --> 00:10:11.688
to go in the way I wanted to go
there. You could produce code I

00:10:11.788 --> 00:10:14.837
wanted to merge. You could
produce code that actually looked

00:10:15.767 --> 00:10:19.988
beautiful if it was written in
Ruby. Rust, different question. But

00:10:21.628 --> 00:10:28.868
this ability for the agents to truly
become an extension of how I wanted to

00:10:28.908 --> 00:10:35.217
work was very novel. But then we
wait just until early spring, and

00:10:35.308 --> 00:10:38.948
suddenly we get sub-agents.
We get harnesses that

00:10:39.008 --> 00:10:42.948
can subdivide the task, and
something that would take

00:10:43.008 --> 00:10:46.828
Opus quite a while
suddenly took a fifth of

00:10:47.008 --> 00:10:50.928
the time, a tenth of the time,
because it could get chopped up and

00:10:50.988 --> 00:10:53.228
suddenly you got eight
sub-agents working for you.

00:10:54.328 --> 00:10:58.068
But both of those two first
phases of the agentic age,

00:10:59.068 --> 00:11:03.058
to me, still felt like I had
to drive. I could tell it what

00:11:03.188 --> 00:11:06.948
I wanted, where to look for
it, and steer it a little bit

00:11:07.028 --> 00:11:11.108
when it went off, and then we'll get
there, and I would go much faster.

00:11:11.188 --> 00:11:15.068
But I had to be in the driver's seat.
I had to tell it what I wanted, and I

00:11:15.148 --> 00:11:21.268
had to be the reviewer, the auditor
of what was coming out. And then

00:11:21.428 --> 00:11:29.308
finally now, this summer, with Opus
5, Fable, and Sol, GPT Sol, and

00:11:29.728 --> 00:11:33.668
to a lesser extent, some of
the open weight models, we've

00:11:33.728 --> 00:11:37.628
arrived at a new era where I'm
not telling it where we're

00:11:37.668 --> 00:11:41.658
going. I'm telling it the problem
I have. I'm telling it the fuzzy,

00:11:41.968 --> 00:11:47.298
vague idea I have. It tells
me where we're going.

00:11:47.408 --> 00:11:50.628
It tells me which path to take,
and I will still look at it

00:11:50.668 --> 00:11:54.508
because I'm a curious person and I like
computers and I like the outcome of it,

00:11:55.268 --> 00:12:01.450
but I really kinda don't have to.
I've become optional in the part that

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:07.780
produces the code that picks the route.
Remember when early GPS systems came out?

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:12.740
They were amazing compared to looking
at a map, but you still wanted to pay

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:16.730
attention. Is it gonna drive you in
the harbor? I remember these newspaper

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:20.520
articles. "Ah, GPS, they're terrible because
people don't pay attention, they drive in the

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:24.460
harbor." When was the last time GPS drove anyone
in the harbor? Like, that just doesn't happen

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:28.440
anymore. In fact, the cars now just drive
themselves, right? And this is where

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:31.420
we've arrived at now, that I can trust.

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:35.800
For the domain I'm working in
right now, I can trust it,

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:40.640
and feel completely confident
that it's gonna have my

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:44.540
back. It's not gonna do something stupid, and
if it does something stupid, it's gonna be

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:45.200
able to recover.

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:48.500
- Mm-hmm. We should mention that
there's all kinds of domains that-

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:48.860
- Correct

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:50.680
- ... programmers operate in. There's...

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:54.580
I actually don't know, but
I think the most common

00:12:55.700 --> 00:13:00.530
domain in development is, like,
web dev CRUD. You have a database.

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:04.080
You have a user-facing interface, and
it does something back and forth,

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:08.610
and it could be internal to just
one person, to multiple people,

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:12.510
to a small number of people, or to
the world. And I think for that,

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:17.400
it's-- I mean, you could
really get to the 100% of

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:21.180
code written by AI and
really if you're a good

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:24.960
programmer and you have a good intuition
about what's happening behind the scenes,

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:29.420
you can legitimately not look at
the code, at least that's, that's

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:31.960
been my experience. I don't know how much,

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:36.220
prior experience with programming
you need to have to kinda

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:40.360
know that things are working correctly
behind the scenes, like intuitively

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:46.580
by observing the ripple effects,
the symptoms of the system. But I

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:52.700
think for that domain, it's close to 100%,
and then there's domains like you're

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:56.760
also operating in, which is writing a
Linux distribution. There, maybe there's

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:00.480
more because you're obsessed
with speed and all that kind of

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:04.470
stuff. There, maybe you need to look at the code
a little bit more. Then there's maybe safety

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:08.059
critical systems all the way down
to operating a nuclear power

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:12.200
plant or a self-driving car. Maybe you
need to look at the code more carefully.

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:18.510
- Yes, but that said, AI is insanely
capable at both finding- ... and fixing

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:23.440
security vulnerabilities. This was the
whole blowup about Fable. This model was

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:26.860
so capable of finding holes that a

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:30.320
an attacker could exploit that it
was simply not safe to release.

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:34.560
So the irony here is that when
you look at that field, it

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:38.900
seems like we've reached
levels of intelligence that

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:43.140
virtually no human can match because

00:14:43.660 --> 00:14:47.300
many of these security holes are
about stringing combo moves together.

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:49.020
You find one little

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:53.860
vulnerability here that by itself might not
be the worst thing in the world, but then you

00:14:53.900 --> 00:14:59.280
combine it with four others, and suddenly
you have RCE, remote command execution.

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:04.440
Humans who are able to do
that are very rare. They

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:10.540
usually work inside state-sponsored
organizations or other clandestine operations.

00:15:11.340 --> 00:15:13.880
They're not just out and
about finding things.

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:18.120
So they've gotten so good at
that. The Linux distribution

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:26.060
is why I've gotten 100% AI pilled. Because
I've been working on Omarchy for the last

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:29.620
three months, this version that just
dropped a few days ago called Quatro.

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:35.000
And almost right from the beginning
I'm working on that version,

00:15:35.420 --> 00:15:39.200
the agent acceleration
neared 100%, and in the last

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:42.560
two months it has been
100%. I have not written-

00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:43.180
- Really?

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:51.040
- ... any of the code that's shipped in
Quatro by hand. I've reviewed the shape

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:52.129
of all of it.

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:56.660
I've reviewed the individual lines of
anything that's critical in the model

00:15:56.780 --> 00:16:01.340
layer of the system, and I've not
looked at a bunch of the UI code.

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:05.980
I have not looked at a bunch
of the auxiliary code, and

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:12.640
I've not written any of the new
functionality entirely by hand. But then

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:17.500
the web part, actually
evolving Basecamp and Hey, our

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:21.340
professional products that have lots
of users and are relatively large code

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:25.220
bases, have proven surprisingly tricky

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:29.160
to fully accelerate with
agents. We just released

00:16:29.180 --> 00:16:32.290
Basecamp 5 not too long
ago. That was the first

00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:36.140
product at 37signals that was
really agent accelerated.

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:40.660
Because we were in this final
sprint phase from around

00:16:40.700 --> 00:16:43.570
February. By then, agents
were already good.

00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:49.790
And we had this early surge of,
"It's solved. We can just have the

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:53.740
designers do the programming. They know what
features they want. They know what shape

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:59.460
they want it to take. Let's just--
Let them vibe." And we let them vibe.

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:05.260
And we ended up with a lot of PRs that
individually perhaps could have been

00:17:05.320 --> 00:17:08.800
justified for a hot moment,
taken all together,

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:10.640
destroyed the architecture of the system.

00:17:10.690 --> 00:17:14.340
And we actually had to clean
up manually, mop it up by

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:17.910
hand, by human hand, to get
back to an architecture that

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:21.810
felt cohesive and coherent. So we still

00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:24.860
have a bit of that-- that was February, by
the way. Things are quite different now.

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:28.940
- Well, hold on a second. What's the lesson from that?
Is one of the lessons from that that you have to be

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:33.080
a programmer at this stage
to be able to vibe code?

00:17:33.700 --> 00:17:38.080
- To be able to vibe code
on existing substantial

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:38.150
- I see.

00:17:38.180 --> 00:17:41.760
- ... code bases, even if
they're crud, if you wanna

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:45.380
retain the element of architecture
that got that system to where it was.

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:49.400
Now, that's also a point
where I've stressed many

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:53.340
times that when people
accuse vibe coders of

00:17:53.380 --> 00:17:57.320
being slob generators, I go right
back at them and say, "Have you

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:01.304
looked at the average programmer's
output?" That is some

00:18:01.384 --> 00:18:04.844
other slob too. If you've looked
at behind the scenes of many

00:18:05.024 --> 00:18:08.904
great companies and what their
code bases look like after there's

00:18:08.944 --> 00:18:14.364
been 3,000 humans through them,
they're awful. Absolutely awful.

00:18:14.684 --> 00:18:16.384
- Can you tell me the intuition you have?

00:18:17.224 --> 00:18:21.144
I seem to just, stepping back and observing
the different apps that we all rely

00:18:21.264 --> 00:18:24.904
on, I don't know, Adobe Photoshop, all
this kind of stuff. There seems to be the

00:18:24.964 --> 00:18:28.364
progress on development
there has not accelerated.

00:18:29.164 --> 00:18:32.284
So why is it, what lessons can
you draw from Basecamp, like

00:18:32.404 --> 00:18:34.204
well-established, huge user base?

00:18:35.304 --> 00:18:39.184
Why aren't we seeing, like,
super rapid increase in, like,

00:18:39.404 --> 00:18:43.204
new updates, features, all this kind of
stuff in these well-established apps?

00:18:44.064 --> 00:18:48.044
- Multiple reasons. I'll start with the
first one that's the most critical. As

00:18:48.144 --> 00:18:51.824
soon as you're having human
teams work together on

00:18:51.884 --> 00:18:55.184
something, the bottleneck is rarely

00:18:55.224 --> 00:18:59.084
implementation. It's human
bandwidth and communication.

00:18:59.104 --> 00:19:05.224
When you have a product manager and a couple
of designers and a VP above them and a

00:19:06.184 --> 00:19:10.124
CTO above them, and
everyone wants to be part

00:19:10.184 --> 00:19:13.514
of the shaping process because we're
all justifying why we're here,

00:19:14.834 --> 00:19:22.864
that's where all the productivity goes to
die. The revelation I've had working on

00:19:22.904 --> 00:19:29.684
Omarchy the last three months is
that to get that magical 10X,

00:19:30.404 --> 00:19:37.404
100X, in a few rare cases, 1000X
productivity boost, you have

00:19:37.504 --> 00:19:43.204
to interact with the agents directly,
and you cannot intermediate that

00:19:44.084 --> 00:19:48.444
bandwidth with another human
because it's simply too slow.

00:19:49.304 --> 00:19:53.224
And on the one hand, that's a
bit of a bummer. I mean, I like

00:19:53.244 --> 00:19:57.104
humans, and it's great to work together.
But it also means we need to temper

00:19:57.124 --> 00:20:00.963
our expectations with what
these agents can do if

00:20:01.024 --> 00:20:04.964
it's humans driving it, and you have
three layers of approval and all the

00:20:05.024 --> 00:20:13.024
other machinery of a large corporation.
The implementation part is only a small

00:20:13.044 --> 00:20:20.594
segment of it. The other thing I'd say is
that most organizations don't know what

00:20:20.624 --> 00:20:24.244
they want. They don't know how
to make it better. They're not

00:20:24.284 --> 00:20:28.164
bottlenecked on implementation.
They're bottlenecked on

00:20:28.244 --> 00:20:32.064
ideas. They're bottlenecked on
vision. They're bottlenecked on

00:20:32.204 --> 00:20:36.004
taste. And if you don't have those element

00:20:36.064 --> 00:20:40.033
in excess of your
implementational capacity,

00:20:40.484 --> 00:20:44.444
it doesn't help. So you can make a lot
of shitty ideas come true, then what?

00:20:44.704 --> 00:20:45.644
Are you gonna ship that?

00:20:46.504 --> 00:20:50.503
That sounds like, I don't know, something coming
out of Microsoft. That's not what we're trying to

00:20:50.544 --> 00:20:54.384
replicate here. That process is not
actually... Because we already had

00:20:54.444 --> 00:20:57.944
this. If you step back
for a moment and think of

00:20:58.964 --> 00:21:02.924
the towering organizations
we have who have had tens of

00:21:03.084 --> 00:21:05.444
thousands of programmers
at their disposal.

00:21:06.624 --> 00:21:10.604
I mean, I'm picking on Microsoft here. I love
Microsoft some of the time, but I'll pick on

00:21:10.644 --> 00:21:14.564
them in this case because they have
had endless resources, endless

00:21:14.844 --> 00:21:19.404
programming capacity for
decades, right? This is what's

00:21:20.644 --> 00:21:23.954
showing us that just being able
to write a lot of code does not

00:21:23.984 --> 00:21:27.973
produce great compelling
software. Now, the other thing is

00:21:29.164 --> 00:21:36.144
we've had this capacity for about six
months. That's not very long in human

00:21:37.284 --> 00:21:40.844
life cycle capacity of
internalizing what's going on.

00:21:40.884 --> 00:21:44.864
And I think it's actually funny
that the critique of AI is,

00:21:45.344 --> 00:21:47.164
why isn't it going faster?

00:21:47.344 --> 00:21:51.244
What are you talking about? We've not had any
other form of progress that has moved as fast

00:21:51.284 --> 00:21:54.904
as AI, and you're impatient because
in the last three months we haven't

00:21:54.944 --> 00:21:58.844
rewritten the whole world and made
it a utopia of software goodness?

00:21:59.084 --> 00:22:03.054
- You're basically saying everybody should be
switching to Linux now. One of the arguments is,

00:22:03.144 --> 00:22:06.224
like, we can rewrite all the software
that's not available in Linux in Linux.

00:22:06.944 --> 00:22:10.924
Linux has been the love of my life for many years, but
one of the reasons I'm still attached to Windows and

00:22:10.964 --> 00:22:15.624
now, uh, Mac is because of
video editing, Premiere.

00:22:15.704 --> 00:22:16.774
- We're gonna fix that. But-

00:22:17.084 --> 00:22:20.114
- So the question is, who's
gonna build Premiere

00:22:20.984 --> 00:22:26.884
and Photoshop for Linux? And it
feels like one person can now.

00:22:27.184 --> 00:22:28.843
- 100% one person can.

00:22:28.864 --> 00:22:34.044
- And so, and I'm allowed to be
impatient. In fact, me or anyone else,

00:22:35.344 --> 00:22:39.984
impatience is the first step of
doing it yourself, right? Like-

00:22:40.024 --> 00:22:40.304
- Correct.

00:22:40.544 --> 00:22:44.024
- ... and there's a community of tens
of thousands of people that use Adobe

00:22:44.124 --> 00:22:48.114
Premiere or DaVinci, all these
non-linear video editors, that

00:22:48.184 --> 00:22:52.104
know the frustrations. Everybody shares them.
You can look at Reddit. You can look at

00:22:52.124 --> 00:22:55.904
the forums. Everybody knows it. Uh, I
don't know what it is. It may be the

00:22:55.944 --> 00:22:59.564
management. It may be the meetings. All
the things you've always discussed is the,

00:23:00.174 --> 00:23:04.064
The bureaucracy that's inside
companies that's slowing

00:23:04.124 --> 00:23:07.044
everything down in the development
of features. But it feels like

00:23:08.064 --> 00:23:11.964
there-- if you just let some of the
developers loose with a bunch of agents,

00:23:12.204 --> 00:23:16.064
you can fix all that. But again,
based on everything you're saying,

00:23:16.504 --> 00:23:21.764
maybe the way to do that is to start
over from scratch, maybe through open

00:23:21.864 --> 00:23:25.614
source, or somebody launches
a new company that within

00:23:25.684 --> 00:23:27.724
large companies now it's hard to,

00:23:29.164 --> 00:23:33.104
to really accelerate into the agentic
era by letting developers just build.

00:23:33.424 --> 00:23:38.804
- This is the classic innovator's dilemma.
These companies have gotten so good,

00:23:39.704 --> 00:23:43.604
so established at the old way,
and therefore their entire

00:23:43.744 --> 00:23:51.424
structure, management layers, processes are
tuned for a time that no longer exists.

00:23:52.084 --> 00:23:57.304
But you can't pivot that.
These are super tankers. It

00:23:57.364 --> 00:23:59.764
just doesn't happen. This is why

00:24:00.732 --> 00:24:04.132
we're finally getting this upset
in the technology industry.

00:24:04.912 --> 00:24:08.732
For a while, I was very
upset about the duopoly

00:24:08.992 --> 00:24:12.712
between Apple and Google on mobile,
because it felt like mobile was the most

00:24:12.752 --> 00:24:16.712
important computing platform
that we had, and I could not see

00:24:16.832 --> 00:24:20.392
a path to unseat either Google or Apple

00:24:20.892 --> 00:24:25.092
sitting on top of that, controlling
everything, taking their toll booth money.

00:24:26.472 --> 00:24:29.972
But the game has changed. This
is no longer the most important

00:24:30.532 --> 00:24:34.212
platform. Mobile phones are
important, but there's also a lot of

00:24:34.312 --> 00:24:38.112
other form factors coming,
whether it's glasses or it's

00:24:38.152 --> 00:24:40.352
earpieces or whatever else have you.

00:24:41.392 --> 00:24:44.332
It's all in play now, but
the computing platforms

00:24:44.392 --> 00:24:48.072
themselves are in play
for the first time in

00:24:48.132 --> 00:24:53.932
probably 40 years, if you look at the
desktop. Linux has been around since '91.

00:24:54.752 --> 00:24:58.672
It's not taken off or taken
over on the desktop. It's taken

00:24:58.732 --> 00:25:02.292
over everything else. All the
devices you have on your desk, your

00:25:02.452 --> 00:25:05.492
fridge, your toaster,
everything runs Linux,

00:25:05.552 --> 00:25:12.872
except for your computer. Now, funnily
enough, your Android is actually Linux, but

00:25:12.912 --> 00:25:15.932
wrapped so sufficiently that
you can't really recognize

00:25:15.972 --> 00:25:19.142
it. But now there's an
opening, and the opening

00:25:19.272 --> 00:25:23.212
is exactly as you say it. If
there's a piece of software you

00:25:23.232 --> 00:25:30.432
depended on that bound you to Windows,
it is completely within reach for

00:25:30.492 --> 00:25:34.352
you personally to start rewriting
it. And maybe you won't get to

00:25:34.432 --> 00:25:37.852
100% coverage, but this is the
old joke about Microsoft Office.

00:25:38.812 --> 00:25:42.572
"I only use 5%." Yeah, well,
we all use a different 5%.

00:25:42.632 --> 00:25:45.422
Well, what if we all
just build our own 5%?

00:25:45.972 --> 00:25:49.702
What if I just took the functionality
that I need and just did

00:25:49.702 --> 00:25:53.092
that? That is a completely
different challenge and

00:25:53.252 --> 00:25:57.142
one agents are incredibly
capable of doing right now,

00:25:57.232 --> 00:26:01.012
today, and I've done it a lot
of times over. So one of the

00:26:01.052 --> 00:26:04.852
amazing things I've found with the
latest agent engaged is I have

00:26:04.912 --> 00:26:12.872
become a polyglot programmer, something I
was absolutely not before. I was a Ruby

00:26:13.012 --> 00:26:17.152
programmer-- ... first and foremost, and
then I dabbled a little bit in Bash--

00:26:17.172 --> 00:26:20.952
... when I had to. And in
the last two months, I've

00:26:20.972 --> 00:26:24.672
written C++. I've written
three applications that have

00:26:24.852 --> 00:26:28.252
shipped in Omarchy Quattro. I wrote a

00:26:29.372 --> 00:26:32.832
writing app. I was using this app called
Typora, which is a very nice app-

00:26:32.872 --> 00:26:33.052
- Great, great app

00:26:33.072 --> 00:26:36.952
- ... which in itself is based on another
app called iA Writer, which was my real

00:26:37.012 --> 00:26:42.352
love on the Mac- ... for
a clean, simple Markdown

00:26:42.482 --> 00:26:44.662
writing environment. That's
where I write all my essays.

00:26:45.272 --> 00:26:47.422
And then I moved to Linux,
and I couldn't get iA Writer,

00:26:48.232 --> 00:26:51.972
so I moved over to this other tool
called Typora. It was a piece of

00:26:51.992 --> 00:26:56.712
shareware, and it had a lot of
features I just didn't need. And about

00:26:58.152 --> 00:27:01.902
six weeks, seven weeks ago, I thought, "Do
you know what? I only need 5% of Typora,"

00:27:02.272 --> 00:27:03.121
which is already a basic app.

00:27:04.172 --> 00:27:07.712
I literally told the agent to get
going, that I wanted it written in

00:27:07.752 --> 00:27:11.442
C++ and Qt, because that
would fit well with the

00:27:11.472 --> 00:27:13.371
aesthetic of what I was
building with Quattro.

00:27:14.512 --> 00:27:18.212
And in, I think, about 20 minutes,
it had the first version. I

00:27:18.252 --> 00:27:21.652
started using it, and it wasn't
quite right. Within two days,

00:27:22.112 --> 00:27:26.012
I'd given up Typora, and
I wrote and have written

00:27:26.152 --> 00:27:29.992
all of my essays since
that moment in Omawrite.

00:27:30.512 --> 00:27:34.502
- Can I ask for your advice and your
vision about something? So what I find

00:27:34.532 --> 00:27:37.812
with agents, you can actually write
something like a replacement for

00:27:37.832 --> 00:27:42.722
Typora that is perfectly
customized to you, to your needs-

00:27:42.792 --> 00:27:49.192
- Correct with just one user in mind. And
then there's a version of that that is-

00:27:50.072 --> 00:27:53.852
starts with one user in mind but expands
to a larger audience. So I've actually

00:27:53.872 --> 00:27:57.552
written a large number of software
just for me, and I find it

00:27:58.032 --> 00:28:01.812
easier to do and better to
get my situation done. For

00:28:01.852 --> 00:28:06.572
example, I have a video editor I've written,
which is very easy to do just for me.

00:28:06.652 --> 00:28:10.632
- I've never, unlike you, built something
that's used by a large number of

00:28:10.672 --> 00:28:14.652
people, and I find that a bit
scary and intimidating. So,

00:28:14.752 --> 00:28:18.332
like, at this, with this agentic
age, how do you take a step

00:28:18.872 --> 00:28:22.552
to actually build something that solves
your problem, which I think is a

00:28:22.612 --> 00:28:23.512
beautiful way to build,

00:28:24.392 --> 00:28:28.242
but also expand it to actually useful
for other people? Like, is there

00:28:28.312 --> 00:28:32.132
advice you can give, and do you
also see the problem of how

00:28:32.172 --> 00:28:34.472
easy it is to just build
a tool for yourself?

00:28:35.312 --> 00:28:39.212
- Yes, but it is simpler than you
think. By the time you're done

00:28:39.492 --> 00:28:43.912
building the tool for yourself, you simply
tell your agent to put it on GitHub.

00:28:43.992 --> 00:28:47.792
You don't even have to do anything else.
It will figure out how to put that repo on

00:28:47.892 --> 00:28:51.792
GitHub. It'll write a nice README.
It'll start using GitHub releases so

00:28:51.802 --> 00:28:55.172
that you can track things. It'll
actually be a better software

00:28:55.212 --> 00:28:57.002
maintainer than you could ever be-

00:28:57.002 --> 00:29:00.832
... because it is far more patient,
it is far more diligent in

00:29:00.852 --> 00:29:05.542
doing the drudgery of managing
open source software than you are.

00:29:05.892 --> 00:29:08.772
And you can simply get on with the joy

00:29:09.492 --> 00:29:13.352
of using the software that you built
and evolving it as you see fit. Now,

00:29:13.472 --> 00:29:18.692
this gets into the big argument we've been
having in open source for a while. Is

00:29:18.992 --> 00:29:22.852
AI contributions good or
bad for the maintainer?

00:29:23.492 --> 00:29:26.752
There's a lot of maintainers
right now who are quite

00:29:26.812 --> 00:29:30.692
upset about the fact that they're suddenly

00:29:30.732 --> 00:29:34.292
getting a huge influx of pull requests, of

00:29:34.372 --> 00:29:40.602
contributions from people who may not be
the best programmers or programmers at all-

00:29:41.212 --> 00:29:46.512
... towards their software. I look at that
argument and go, "Are you kidding me?"

00:29:47.632 --> 00:29:56.592
Here is a vein of free contributions
that you can take or don't take,

00:29:57.132 --> 00:30:01.032
but you're complaining about the fact
that they're there? It sounds straight

00:30:01.112 --> 00:30:04.732
out of- My steak is too
juicy and my lobster too

00:30:04.912 --> 00:30:07.332
buttery. What are you complaining about?

00:30:07.612 --> 00:30:11.452
This is the most amazing thing.
This is what we proclamated for so

00:30:11.512 --> 00:30:15.252
long was going to be the magic
of open source, that we could

00:30:15.352 --> 00:30:18.992
all contribute to it, but it was, of
course, never true. There was only a small

00:30:19.032 --> 00:30:22.862
subset of people who contribute to open
source, and that was the very highly skilled

00:30:22.952 --> 00:30:26.922
wizards. So this is a bit of a
reformation moment. We had this

00:30:26.952 --> 00:30:30.522
disintermediation between us and
the computer, and it was this

00:30:31.032 --> 00:30:34.531
class of clerics and
priests called programmers,

00:30:35.152 --> 00:30:39.092
and suddenly they're being
disintermediated here by was

00:30:39.132 --> 00:30:42.932
it the 95 theses by Luther in 1500s,

00:30:43.532 --> 00:30:47.412
nailing them to the door? And one
of those was very clearly about

00:30:47.452 --> 00:30:51.512
the intermediation, that you should have
direct access to the higher intelligence.

00:30:51.632 --> 00:30:55.492
- Mm-hmm. But don't you need the wizards to
keep a high bar of excellence in the code

00:30:55.512 --> 00:30:55.712
base?

00:30:56.212 --> 00:31:00.052
- 100% you do, which is also something
that's always been true about open

00:31:00.112 --> 00:31:03.932
source, which is the second argument
that really grinds me. I've been

00:31:03.972 --> 00:31:07.672
running open source projects for
25 years. I have literally looked

00:31:07.772 --> 00:31:11.572
at the output of thousands,
if not tens of thousands of

00:31:11.612 --> 00:31:15.132
programmers. I feel like I
have the statistical basis to

00:31:15.192 --> 00:31:21.752
assert that most programmers, they suck.

00:31:21.782 --> 00:31:24.592
And I don't mean that
in the way it sounds.

00:31:24.612 --> 00:31:28.352
That was why I paused for a hot second
here. I mean, they suck in the sense

00:31:28.432 --> 00:31:32.092
that they don't write the code I
want to have written. They don't

00:31:32.112 --> 00:31:35.252
prepare their bug reports
with all the relevant

00:31:35.292 --> 00:31:39.232
information. They don't detail
their pull requests with the

00:31:39.372 --> 00:31:42.752
why. They don't bother to fill in

00:31:42.832 --> 00:31:46.302
needed code comments. They don't
double-check their work. They don't

00:31:46.312 --> 00:31:50.192
write unit tests. They don't do all
of these things it takes to create

00:31:50.212 --> 00:31:54.072
good software. But do you know
who'll do all that stuff? Agents,

00:31:54.652 --> 00:31:58.392
if you tell them to. They're very
diligent at following your instructions,

00:31:58.772 --> 00:32:02.752
most of the time, sometimes to a fault.
But it actually means that if you take

00:32:02.812 --> 00:32:06.752
the median programmer and
their pull requests towards

00:32:06.792 --> 00:32:08.612
an average open source project,

00:32:09.442 --> 00:32:12.152
they're already getting
outclassed by agents. I would

00:32:12.432 --> 00:32:16.372
rather get an agent-written
pull request to one of my

00:32:16.392 --> 00:32:20.152
projects than I'd get one written
by a human, and it's not just

00:32:20.192 --> 00:32:24.172
because the quality's
better. It's also because

00:32:24.412 --> 00:32:28.272
I feel a lot less bad if I just
reject it. You didn't even

00:32:28.292 --> 00:32:31.692
write it, so I can simply look at what
your agent wrote on your behalf and

00:32:31.732 --> 00:32:34.472
go, "Eh, don't want it."

00:32:36.222 --> 00:32:40.192
That's always been true in open source, and in
fact, I think this is one of the problems with

00:32:40.252 --> 00:32:48.392
open source maintainers. They have way too
much ball of neuroticism and anxiety that

00:32:48.452 --> 00:32:56.212
requires them to look upon any contribution
as an obligation on their part to do

00:32:56.332 --> 00:33:00.112
everything to get that to land
in the code base. That's not

00:33:00.172 --> 00:33:04.572
true at all. You can simply say, "No," or
even better, you can say, "No, thank you."

00:33:05.992 --> 00:33:09.272
And if you get into the mode
of realizing that your project

00:33:10.532 --> 00:33:13.912
is allowed to exist and
evolve according to your

00:33:13.932 --> 00:33:17.702
vision, according to your
roadmap, it becomes so much

00:33:17.772 --> 00:33:21.612
easier in the agentic age to
decline the contributions you don't

00:33:21.752 --> 00:33:25.612
want because you don't
even inconvenience or hurt

00:33:25.672 --> 00:33:29.452
the feelings of a human. It's just
a clanker, and the clanker won't

00:33:29.512 --> 00:33:33.032
mind. In fact, the
producers of clankers, the

00:33:33.392 --> 00:33:38.732
labs, love when you spend tokens in
vain. They get paid just the same.

00:33:39.032 --> 00:33:42.432
So, in my opinion, this
is the absolute best

00:33:42.442 --> 00:33:46.642
time to ever have been an open
source software maintainer.

00:33:46.692 --> 00:33:53.472
Not only do we get this wealth of
glorious pull requests made by

00:33:53.572 --> 00:33:57.552
agents with all the boxes ticked,
we also get to tap into a

00:33:57.612 --> 00:34:02.532
creativity of people who did not have
access to contribute to a project before.

00:34:02.812 --> 00:34:06.321
I've been running the Omarchy project
now for a little over a year,

00:34:07.132 --> 00:34:11.052
and in the last three months working on
Quattro, I have merged over 1,000 pull

00:34:11.092 --> 00:34:16.232
requests. Quite a lot of those pull
requests were written by people who were

00:34:16.272 --> 00:34:20.492
not classical programmers, or were
programmers in other domains, not

00:34:21.692 --> 00:34:25.012
Linux operating system or
distribution development.

00:34:25.752 --> 00:34:29.712
They were able to contribute their good
ideas because of agents, and I got

00:34:29.772 --> 00:34:36.092
to cherry-pick the very best of that- ...
because it was there. Otherwise, those

00:34:36.152 --> 00:34:40.012
ideas would just have lived
inside their heads. Again,

00:34:40.052 --> 00:34:43.792
Is- that is not the purpose of open
source, that we tap into the collective

00:34:43.852 --> 00:34:47.662
intelligence and creativity
of the whole goddamn planet,

00:34:48.012 --> 00:34:51.652
and we channel that towards a commons

00:34:51.892 --> 00:34:55.842
where we all benefit from the
combined efforts of everyone.

00:34:56.432 --> 00:35:01.052
Now, there's currently, I think, about
400 unmerged pull requests on Omarchy,

00:35:01.572 --> 00:35:05.122
about double what it was a week
ago. So the acceleration is

00:35:05.172 --> 00:35:07.902
real, but so are the tools.

00:35:08.192 --> 00:35:11.952
I'm not reviewing every pull request
anymore. I haven't been reviewing them for

00:35:12.072 --> 00:35:16.852
quite some time now. I have the
agents- ... review them for me-

00:35:17.012 --> 00:35:20.482
... and then they will give me a summary
about whether something is ready for the

00:35:20.492 --> 00:35:24.732
decision, the human decision. Should
we merge or should we not merge?

00:35:25.212 --> 00:35:29.832
I don't need to look at all the pull
requests that are either wrong, duplicated,

00:35:30.692 --> 00:35:35.972
bad. An agent can sort that chaff away
from me. I just get to look at the pearls-

00:35:36.252 --> 00:35:39.472
... the good ideas that
are ready to go, the bug

00:35:39.612 --> 00:35:43.592
fixes that the agent has
validated in a VM on my behalf.

00:35:44.112 --> 00:35:47.692
All the drudgery of replicating
and managing open source

00:35:47.752 --> 00:35:53.472
projects is evaporating at lightning
speed, and we are left with the,

00:35:55.152 --> 00:35:59.702
with the golden juicy parts, the
bone marrow of software development-

00:35:59.732 --> 00:36:03.152
... deciding what should this
thing do and where should it go.

00:36:03.352 --> 00:36:06.612
- Do you think the good ideas, ultimately
the kernel of the good ideas

00:36:06.632 --> 00:36:10.012
originated in the human mind,
so the individual contributors

00:36:11.200 --> 00:36:18.090
Like, can it be done by agents, or is
the PR ultimately requires a human idea?

00:36:18.900 --> 00:36:20.420
Like how to improve Omarchy,

00:36:21.340 --> 00:36:24.320
all those incoming... Or can it
all just be a pool of agents?

00:36:24.700 --> 00:36:28.540
- That was the mode and the way
I was thinking about agents

00:36:28.580 --> 00:36:35.690
in the first agentic age from
November 24 to February 28.

00:36:35.960 --> 00:36:39.700
I thought all the ideas would originate
with the humans. They would tell

00:36:39.740 --> 00:36:42.810
their agents what to
build, and off they went.

00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:47.030
I don't think that's true anymore at all.
I have seen things you people wouldn't

00:36:47.080 --> 00:36:54.850
believe, ideas coming out of models
so great that it makes me humble-

00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:58.949
... as a person who otherwise prides
himself on having good ideas.

00:36:59.670 --> 00:37:03.350
The agents are incredibly
capable of creative thought,

00:37:04.040 --> 00:37:11.440
and folks who are still stuck in the
analysis that agents are parrots-

00:37:11.449 --> 00:37:15.239
... just regurgitating the ideas that are

00:37:15.300 --> 00:37:19.220
already there are delusional about
the progress that's been made in

00:37:19.260 --> 00:37:20.640
the last six to nine months.

00:37:21.340 --> 00:37:25.200
- So some people listening to you right now
will say DHH is suffering from the old case

00:37:25.340 --> 00:37:33.320
of AI psychosis. Can you steel man the
case that you are in fact experiencing in

00:37:33.340 --> 00:37:36.920
a state of delusion, like One Flew Over the
Cuckoo's Nest, and can you argue against it?

00:37:37.500 --> 00:37:41.000
- I'm in a state of delirium.
That's what the state I'm in.

00:37:41.680 --> 00:37:45.940
Because I have been working
with computers for 40 years,

00:37:47.560 --> 00:37:53.700
and I've never seen the things that
I've seen in just the last two months.

00:37:54.100 --> 00:38:01.420
A whole career, a whole life dedicating
to the love of computers as my main

00:38:02.300 --> 00:38:04.860
focus for the working hours and then some,

00:38:05.760 --> 00:38:10.640
suddenly completely upended, suddenly
completely rewritten. Of course I'm delirious.

00:38:11.780 --> 00:38:18.540
Who looking at this reality is not? If you
are not delirious or at the very least

00:38:19.100 --> 00:38:23.020
very excited... Actually, I guess that's
not even true. If you're not recognizing

00:38:23.080 --> 00:38:28.180
the gravity of the moment,
that's the delusion. That's the

00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:32.160
psychosis. The psychosis is believing
that the world is barely different. We

00:38:32.220 --> 00:38:39.860
just have some electronic parrots reciting
things to us. Now, in my opinion, the

00:38:39.880 --> 00:38:43.840
reason this argument is actually brought
up is because people are not able to see

00:38:43.900 --> 00:38:47.560
the fruits of the progress. That
goes to your point earlier.

00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:54.480
Where is the amazing software? Why isn't
American GDP running at 12% year over year?

00:38:55.420 --> 00:38:59.700
First of all, give it a minute. It
hasn't even been a year, goddammit.

00:39:01.020 --> 00:39:07.960
But second of all, I feel on my own account,
I have actually brought the pudding.

00:39:08.220 --> 00:39:11.840
Omarchy Quattro launched
on Friday. It's been

00:39:11.940 --> 00:39:17.000
downloaded by tens of thousands of people.
And they like it. They like it a lot.

00:39:17.080 --> 00:39:21.030
- We should mention that going to
Perplexity here, Omarchy is a highly

00:39:21.080 --> 00:39:24.980
opinionated Arch Linux-based desktop
distribution centered on the Hyprland

00:39:25.100 --> 00:39:28.500
Wayland tiling compositor.
It was created by David

00:39:28.580 --> 00:39:32.160
Heinemeier Hansson, DHH, as a polished

00:39:32.180 --> 00:39:35.740
developer workstation set
up with a cohesive visual

00:39:35.800 --> 00:39:39.280
style and many everyday
tools already configured.

00:39:39.700 --> 00:39:45.470
- Let me put it. Omarchy is a beautiful,
modern, and opinionated Linux system.

00:39:45.560 --> 00:39:48.780
It's an alternative operating
system to macOS and Windows,

00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:50.480
and it's freaking amazing.

00:39:50.760 --> 00:39:54.620
- And the Quattro thing is you're
pushing towards the age of agents.

00:39:54.840 --> 00:39:57.740
- That's right. That's the latest version that
we just dropped out, which is also funny.

00:39:58.300 --> 00:40:02.040
The Omarchy project is
only a year old and a

00:40:02.100 --> 00:40:08.040
little bit. I started it last summer in
between sessions at the 24 Hours of Le Mans-

00:40:08.380 --> 00:40:12.100
... where I had watched one
too many YouTube videos on

00:40:12.220 --> 00:40:16.060
Linux Rising and got bitten
by that bug. Started working

00:40:16.120 --> 00:40:19.810
on this second iteration of my

00:40:19.860 --> 00:40:23.670
attempt at creating a better Linux operating
system. The first iteration we talked about

00:40:23.740 --> 00:40:27.240
last time was called Omakub, was built
on top of an existing system called

00:40:27.300 --> 00:40:33.540
Ubuntu, and it was fine. But the
ambition I was able to pour into the new

00:40:33.560 --> 00:40:37.380
version, Omarchy, because I started
seven layers deeper down the

00:40:37.440 --> 00:40:41.300
stack, was completely different, but
it was still done in a pre-agentic

00:40:41.380 --> 00:40:46.400
world. I wrote all those batch
scripts by hand in the beginning,

00:40:47.220 --> 00:40:52.140
and then I got to see the midway
point where things changed over.

00:40:52.240 --> 00:40:56.000
I did a couple of versions of
Omarchy that had partial agent

00:40:56.080 --> 00:40:58.120
acceleration, and then three months ago,

00:40:59.020 --> 00:41:04.080
it was full throttle, 100%, everything
is written by agents but steered by me.

00:41:04.680 --> 00:41:06.530
And that just ended up being

00:41:07.620 --> 00:41:11.370
a very different experience and a
very different operating system

00:41:11.460 --> 00:41:17.080
because I was suddenly granted a
limitless ceiling on my ambition.

00:41:17.700 --> 00:41:25.210
I could look at any feature in Windows, on
Mac, on other Linux systems and say, "I

00:41:25.240 --> 00:41:31.600
want that," and the agents would deliver.
So everything I wanted was suddenly

00:41:31.620 --> 00:41:36.020
within reach, which meant that
I got a little delirious.

00:41:36.310 --> 00:41:40.220
Who would not get delirious if suddenly
a genie pops out of the bottle

00:41:40.500 --> 00:41:44.000
and says, "You can have whatever
you want. Every feature

00:41:44.660 --> 00:41:46.969
you've ever dreamed of
in an operating system-

00:41:47.070 --> 00:41:50.920
... I can deliver them to you, most
of them in five minutes, a few in

00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:54.280
20, and if we really go hog wild,
it's gonna take me two hours."

00:41:54.320 --> 00:41:57.830
- Mm-hmm. Have you seen Requiem
for a Dream, the movie? So the,

00:41:58.360 --> 00:42:00.460
There's a drug-like effect here.

00:42:00.580 --> 00:42:05.940
I find it personally-- We'll talk about
many levels of this, but it is overwhelming

00:42:07.340 --> 00:42:11.130
how much power is placed
in our hands so suddenly,

00:42:11.660 --> 00:42:15.580
and it's very hard to know, it's like with
"The Hobbit" or something, what to do

00:42:15.620 --> 00:42:19.410
with it, and I find myself truly
overwhelmed with the multitasking,

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:28.112
On the verge of almost burnt out. Super
excited about so many things, but ultimately

00:42:28.172 --> 00:42:31.232
looking back, you want to focus
on one thing and really ship

00:42:31.632 --> 00:42:35.612
- I don't have that sense. I don't have
the sense of overwhelm. I don't have the

00:42:35.652 --> 00:42:42.452
sense of dread. I don't have the sense
of distortion because I have a mission.

00:42:42.512 --> 00:42:48.552
I'm going somewhere, and therefore, I
can channel all this new power towards-

00:42:48.592 --> 00:42:48.862
- Yeah, it's wonderful

00:42:48.862 --> 00:42:51.932
- ... a singular goal and
outcome, creating the perfect

00:42:51.992 --> 00:42:59.692
computer. And therefore, all my
investigations with AI are focused towards

00:42:59.772 --> 00:43:02.412
that end. Now, I also have a day job.

00:43:02.462 --> 00:43:06.112
And we also use agents there, and
that's also targeted towards sort of

00:43:06.192 --> 00:43:12.632
specific outcomes. But with the
Omarchy experience, I got to tap in

00:43:14.292 --> 00:43:18.212
straight in the back of
my head and increase

00:43:18.272 --> 00:43:21.822
the bandwidth between ideas arriving in my

00:43:21.972 --> 00:43:28.572
brain and software emerging on the
screen. It was like going from dial-up-

00:43:28.852 --> 00:43:35.572
... to fiber. I think on your podcast, Elon
talked about the actual human bandwidth-

00:43:35.692 --> 00:43:39.632
... that Neuralink and other
projects are trying to accelerate.

00:43:39.812 --> 00:43:45.152
Like, the bandwidth we're communicating
over right now is quite low because

00:43:46.172 --> 00:43:49.952
we're limited by our cognition.
We're limited by the rate of

00:43:50.052 --> 00:43:53.842
speech. You're not limited by those
things when you're working with a

00:43:53.912 --> 00:43:58.432
swarm of agents. Now, let
me caveat this by saying,

00:44:00.012 --> 00:44:03.192
if I'd heard myself talk like this-

00:44:03.312 --> 00:44:07.092
... nine months ago, I would
probably have used the label AI,

00:44:07.592 --> 00:44:11.432
AI psychosis. I think it
would be fitting because

00:44:11.492 --> 00:44:14.162
nine months ago when people were talking
like this, they weren't shipping,

00:44:15.222 --> 00:44:19.052
and I think that's the ultimate difference,
that there were people who saw this

00:44:19.112 --> 00:44:22.712
early. I was not actually that
early. As we've talked about, I

00:44:22.752 --> 00:44:26.712
was rather skeptical, and I was using
AI in certain ways, but I was not

00:44:26.732 --> 00:44:30.132
the first person who downloaded Claude
Code. I think Boris released that in end of

00:44:30.212 --> 00:44:33.532
February. I don't think I
installed Claude Code until

00:44:34.132 --> 00:44:38.532
September. So there was about
six months there where pioneers

00:44:39.352 --> 00:44:42.992
saw these glimmers of what the future
was gonna look like. It wasn't there

00:44:43.092 --> 00:44:46.952
yet because the intelligence wasn't there.
The harnesses weren't there, but they could

00:44:46.992 --> 00:44:50.172
see the glimmers. I couldn't.
Tobi, Tobi Lütke, the CEO of

00:44:50.212 --> 00:44:54.112
Shopify and now my
partner in crime on this

00:44:54.372 --> 00:44:58.092
Omarchy project in part, he's
gotten pilled with Omarchy as well.

00:44:58.512 --> 00:45:02.432
He saw these things very early,
and he tried to tell me,

00:45:03.252 --> 00:45:07.332
and I wasn't seeing it. I was
not seeing what he was seeing.

00:45:07.492 --> 00:45:08.052
- You were a hater.

00:45:08.692 --> 00:45:15.052
- Yes. I was still on Earth. And he was...
He had already boarded the rocket and was

00:45:15.072 --> 00:45:18.432
heading towards space. I
remember actually reading his

00:45:19.152 --> 00:45:24.602
memo to the company, the AI memo, and
thinking, "Ah, seems a little much."

00:45:24.652 --> 00:45:28.372
Like, you're making a big deal out
of something that I cannot yet

00:45:29.032 --> 00:45:31.732
feel is a tangible thing.

00:45:32.102 --> 00:45:36.032
Because where's the output of this? I try
using it. It writes code I don't like.

00:45:36.492 --> 00:45:39.912
It wants to interrupt me all the time.
Where are you getting this from? Uh,

00:45:41.472 --> 00:45:46.132
so I get it. I totally get
it. If you've not seen what

00:45:47.272 --> 00:45:50.112
the current quality of
intelligence can produce,

00:45:51.372 --> 00:45:55.152
of course you're gonna look at someone and go
like, "They sound a little nutty," because

00:45:55.272 --> 00:45:59.232
all your experience up until this point
would tell you that they are nutty. Because

00:45:59.292 --> 00:46:03.132
if you look at the history of computer
programming, for the last 40,

00:46:03.232 --> 00:46:06.952
50 years, people have been promising that

00:46:07.252 --> 00:46:10.812
regular people could write code just
by talking. We're gonna have fourth

00:46:10.832 --> 00:46:13.552
generation languages.
Lisp was once upon a time

00:46:14.572 --> 00:46:18.382
presented, Smalltalk was presented
as these environments that would

00:46:18.432 --> 00:46:22.032
let regular people write their own
applications, and none of it came

00:46:22.252 --> 00:46:25.932
true in the sense beyond, like, Microsoft

00:46:26.112 --> 00:46:30.052
Access databases or Excel
spreadsheets. That, those are end

00:46:30.152 --> 00:46:33.432
user programming environments, but it's
nothing like what we're able to do now.

00:46:33.452 --> 00:46:36.652
And then, I mean, I'm not even talking
about end user. I'm talking as me,

00:46:37.252 --> 00:46:41.032
someone who's been programming
for 25 years, seeing

00:46:41.072 --> 00:46:44.791
the output and the acceleration and
the quality and then shipping it,

00:46:45.612 --> 00:46:48.032
and I think that's what's gonna
change the entire conversation

00:46:49.012 --> 00:46:52.812
quite quickly. The few laggards
who are still on the fence

00:46:52.852 --> 00:46:56.352
about whether this is actually useful,
who are still trapped in a meme

00:46:56.432 --> 00:46:59.812
from early '25 about mechanical parrots,

00:47:00.332 --> 00:47:04.012
they're simply going to be overwhelmed
by the evidence that's about to

00:47:04.132 --> 00:47:04.912
flood over them.

00:47:05.212 --> 00:47:07.532
- So I hope to see,

00:47:08.452 --> 00:47:14.812
projects like Omarchy, several of them
that, like, really show agent first.

00:47:15.312 --> 00:47:17.152
- It's coming. All of it is coming.

00:47:17.552 --> 00:47:21.432
- In the spaces, in the silly spaces that I
already mentioned, like video editing, all

00:47:21.532 --> 00:47:23.392
these kinds of apps that are like Typora-

00:47:24.032 --> 00:47:24.312
- Yes.

00:47:24.332 --> 00:47:25.012
- ... this kind of stuff.

00:47:26.172 --> 00:47:29.912
- It's gonna come, and I've already
seen the early inklings of this.

00:47:30.512 --> 00:47:35.502
One of the really neat features
about Omarchy is that it has

00:47:36.452 --> 00:47:40.252
a far more robust plugin system
that allows you to extend your

00:47:40.312 --> 00:47:44.032
operating system and rewrite
it really in terms of its

00:47:44.112 --> 00:47:46.832
user interface and its
panel and its features

00:47:47.412 --> 00:47:55.032
by creating your own plugins that can be
cloned off of tools we ship in the box. For

00:47:55.072 --> 00:47:58.912
example, say you want a different calendar.
Omarchy ships with a calendar. You

00:47:58.992 --> 00:48:02.982
click the little clock. It pops
up a calendar, and it doesn't

00:48:03.072 --> 00:48:06.652
have iCal support, for example. It
doesn't consume your appointments.

00:48:07.372 --> 00:48:11.132
There's about 17 implementations
of that already, and in

00:48:11.232 --> 00:48:18.332
three days, we had 330 plugins on the
Omarchy plugin marketplace. I have

00:48:18.512 --> 00:48:22.272
never seen growth like
that with any project

00:48:22.282 --> 00:48:26.192
I've ever been involved in. I've never
seen participation that broadly.

00:48:26.262 --> 00:48:30.182
I've never seen that many people
be able to create software that's

00:48:30.212 --> 00:48:36.824
meaningful for them- Also be usable
for others so quickly. All of

00:48:36.844 --> 00:48:40.084
it is driven by the fact
that Omarchy ships a set of

00:48:40.124 --> 00:48:43.604
skills that tells any agent
you bring to it how to

00:48:43.664 --> 00:48:47.194
create extensions to the
operating system, and

00:48:47.264 --> 00:48:52.254
therefore affording you the
vision of the true malleable...

00:48:53.944 --> 00:48:56.454
I was about to say agentic.
I hate that fucking word.

00:48:56.504 --> 00:48:59.864
And the reason in part I hate that word
is, first of all, it's become marketing

00:49:00.104 --> 00:49:03.344
slob speak- ... at this point. Like,
it's just slapped onto everything.

00:49:04.764 --> 00:49:07.584
I wish we had a different word
that just meant AI doing stuff.

00:49:08.004 --> 00:49:09.604
- Vibe coding feels wrong, too.

00:49:09.784 --> 00:49:13.304
- It does, because vibe coding to
me smells exactly like script

00:49:13.404 --> 00:49:15.384
kiddies did in the early 2000s.

00:49:15.784 --> 00:49:19.764
People applying PHP scripts they
just downloaded offline they don't

00:49:19.784 --> 00:49:21.134
understand anything of.

00:49:21.444 --> 00:49:25.134
And while, I mean, that's true in the
sense that much of the vibe coding,

00:49:25.184 --> 00:49:28.944
including my own vibe coded projects,
and as I just mentioned, I have several.

00:49:29.244 --> 00:49:33.244
I've written things in C++.
I've written things in Rust.

00:49:33.644 --> 00:49:39.144
Rust. I hate Rust with
a passion. Rust to me

00:49:39.204 --> 00:49:41.964
is like pouring acid in my eyes
when I have to look at the code.

00:49:42.044 --> 00:49:43.644
- Wait, wait, wait, wait. Why
do, why do you hate Rust?

00:49:43.924 --> 00:49:47.404
- It is, in my opinion, the ugliest programming
language- ... that has been invented-

00:49:47.534 --> 00:49:48.584
... in probably the last 40 years.

00:49:49.004 --> 00:49:50.784
- So it's the opposite of Rails and Ruby.

00:49:50.824 --> 00:49:52.484
- It's the opposite. But-

00:49:53.124 --> 00:49:53.584
- You've written it

00:49:53.664 --> 00:49:57.404
- ... I've written applications
in Rust because the output

00:49:57.684 --> 00:50:01.664
of Rust is actually amazing. The memory
safety, the fact that it's a system

00:50:01.724 --> 00:50:05.644
language, the fact that it's
highly efficient and so forth is

00:50:05.664 --> 00:50:09.524
incredible. So Rust, to me, can both be

00:50:09.604 --> 00:50:13.564
the most repugnant programming
language ever devised for

00:50:13.604 --> 00:50:19.324
human consumption and a wonderful
platform for agentic engineering.

00:50:19.784 --> 00:50:24.504
These two truths can
coexist easily in my head.

00:50:24.584 --> 00:50:28.204
- Do you think that there can be a point at
which we can just call agentic engineering

00:50:28.224 --> 00:50:31.124
programming? Can we just
change what programming

00:50:31.184 --> 00:50:35.124
means? 'Cause who's actually going
to be programming the old-school way

00:50:35.504 --> 00:50:36.364
in a few months?

00:50:36.824 --> 00:50:40.184
- I don't think we should reuse the
term because for me, programming

00:50:40.284 --> 00:50:44.764
implies an understanding
of certain primitives,

00:50:45.864 --> 00:50:50.774
loops, conditions, variables. All the
constructs of programming languages

00:50:52.064 --> 00:50:55.684
are what constitutes programming
itself, not the creation of programs.

00:50:55.804 --> 00:50:59.584
Because you could say in the
pre-agentic era, well, your

00:50:59.644 --> 00:51:03.634
CEO is programming. He's hiring a bunch of
programmers, he's telling them what to do, and

00:51:03.684 --> 00:51:05.454
out comes software that he can sell.

00:51:05.864 --> 00:51:09.444
Eh, I don't think most people would call that CEO
a programmer, and I don't think we should call

00:51:09.504 --> 00:51:13.264
the vibe coder a programmer
either. And vibe

00:51:13.284 --> 00:51:17.184
coding, if we define it here, is you
tell an agent to build software for

00:51:17.264 --> 00:51:20.904
you. You do not look at the implementation.
That, to me, is what separates vibe

00:51:20.984 --> 00:51:26.434
coding from programming or, let's
say, agent-accelerated development.

00:51:26.524 --> 00:51:28.524
- But don't you think, so to
push back a little bit,

00:51:29.604 --> 00:51:33.464
don't you think a programmer,
old-school programmer, you, doing

00:51:33.524 --> 00:51:37.474
agentic engineering is a different
kind of agentic engineering than

00:51:37.504 --> 00:51:41.194
a non-programmer doing agentic
engineering? What I mean is, if you know

00:51:42.584 --> 00:51:46.234
what a for loop is, if you know what function
programming is, if you know some of the

00:51:46.324 --> 00:51:49.984
basic good principles of software
engineering, the way you do

00:51:50.044 --> 00:51:53.724
natural language-based agentic
engineering will be different and more

00:51:53.764 --> 00:51:57.504
systematic, and this, the scale and the

00:51:57.584 --> 00:52:02.254
variety of things you can actually
build is much bigger than a vibe coder.

00:52:02.613 --> 00:52:05.734
- I'm just gonna extend the pro case here a
little bit just for the sake of the argument.

00:52:05.744 --> 00:52:05.934
- Right.

00:52:07.184 --> 00:52:14.544
- I actually think for a while it
was to my deficit to know as

00:52:14.604 --> 00:52:18.504
much as I know about programming because I

00:52:18.564 --> 00:52:22.113
was instructing the
agents to do things as I

00:52:22.124 --> 00:52:25.634
prescribed them to do, and they
were very good at that. And in the

00:52:25.664 --> 00:52:29.604
first agentic moment, let's not
call it an era, it was something

00:52:29.613 --> 00:52:35.064
that lasted three months. Moment. In
the first agentic moment, that felt

00:52:35.364 --> 00:52:39.204
very productive. I could get more productivity
out of telling the agents what to

00:52:39.244 --> 00:52:42.544
do in the way I would've done
it, and I used all of my

00:52:42.564 --> 00:52:45.664
experience as a programmer
to do just that.

00:52:47.224 --> 00:52:54.584
Then I was a little late on the next moment,
and the next moment allowed me, allowed

00:52:54.644 --> 00:52:59.734
anyone, to describe outcomes- ...
to describe problems to the agents

00:53:00.624 --> 00:53:05.464
and get better solutions than if you
had a programmer prescribe the path.

00:53:05.904 --> 00:53:08.604
- See, okay, so we're having
fun arguing here. Well

00:53:09.604 --> 00:53:12.484
so are you suggesting that
there's problems for which

00:53:12.764 --> 00:53:16.234
programmers are worse than
non-programmers at agentic engineering?

00:53:16.234 --> 00:53:20.124
- 100%. And the reason I say
that, let's define it here.

00:53:20.604 --> 00:53:24.564
The reason I say that is there's
a lot of programmers who are not

00:53:24.684 --> 00:53:27.864
very good product managers. Software

00:53:28.564 --> 00:53:32.224
is product management. What
should it do? Who should it do it

00:53:32.284 --> 00:53:36.244
for? How should it do it? How should
it look? What are our priorities? What

00:53:36.264 --> 00:53:40.944
do we start with first? What does
version one include? All of those skills

00:53:42.424 --> 00:53:46.244
are not easily or equally
distributed across all programmers.

00:53:46.624 --> 00:53:50.344
And in the agentic era
where you are letting

00:53:50.704 --> 00:53:54.524
an agent, an AI, do the implementation,
these are the skills you

00:53:54.564 --> 00:53:58.354
need. And I happen to sit in both
camps, and this is why I love this

00:53:58.404 --> 00:54:02.144
moment. I still get to live a little bit
in the old world where I look at the full

00:54:02.184 --> 00:54:05.944
implementation because there are
domains and areas where I feel

00:54:06.344 --> 00:54:09.864
that still brings value, and
then I also live in the future.

00:54:10.664 --> 00:54:14.324
And as I said, when I created
Omawrite, I've not looked at a

00:54:14.604 --> 00:54:18.284
single line of that C++. I
actually made it a point to

00:54:18.324 --> 00:54:22.084
myself that I was gonna treat it as an
experiment. This is 100% a black box.

00:54:22.404 --> 00:54:26.004
I will treat it as though I
was any other user who had

00:54:26.024 --> 00:54:29.644
opinions about how their digital
typewriter should work, and

00:54:29.704 --> 00:54:33.174
therefore felt like it
was a fair experiment as

00:54:33.244 --> 00:54:36.868
if a writer of any other sort who had

00:54:36.908 --> 00:54:40.598
opinions about how software should be done
and how it should work. As you mentioned,

00:54:40.908 --> 00:54:44.408
video editors who know exactly
where they don't like what Adobe

00:54:44.448 --> 00:54:48.388
Premiere's doing and how it could be
better, but don't have the capacities to

00:54:48.428 --> 00:54:50.528
improve it- ... how that could be.

00:54:50.788 --> 00:54:54.548
- The pushback, though, I think what
programmers do well, good programmers do

00:54:54.628 --> 00:54:59.248
well, is systematic design,
like think in terms of systems-

00:54:59.268 --> 00:54:59.338
- Correct

00:54:59.448 --> 00:55:00.248
- ... and rigor.

00:55:00.308 --> 00:55:00.648
- Yes.

00:55:00.708 --> 00:55:04.668
- And I think still, and probably
for a long time to come,

00:55:04.708 --> 00:55:08.148
even natural language-based
control of agentic systems will

00:55:08.188 --> 00:55:11.788
require a certain kind
of rigor. Not overly

00:55:11.848 --> 00:55:15.488
structured rigor, but really explain very

00:55:15.588 --> 00:55:19.448
specifically the goals
of the system, how to

00:55:19.468 --> 00:55:23.318
do the verification, what kind of
security testing. You wanna do all

00:55:23.348 --> 00:55:27.168
those kinds of things. Although
then to push back on that, the

00:55:27.208 --> 00:55:28.898
system will... should
be smart longer term.

00:55:28.988 --> 00:55:30.608
- I would've said the same
thing six months ago.

00:55:30.708 --> 00:55:30.858
- Yeah, so this has changed.

00:55:30.877 --> 00:55:34.758
- And I think the same thing would've been true
six months ago. What I have found since,

00:55:35.108 --> 00:55:39.088
especially even just the
last few weeks of the

00:55:39.128 --> 00:55:42.788
agentically accelerated
development of Mach-E, is that

00:55:43.668 --> 00:55:48.788
more often than not, I have the humility
to recognize that the agent knows best.

00:55:49.308 --> 00:55:51.658
- You can legitimately say,
"Make sure it's secure."

00:55:52.108 --> 00:55:59.418
- Correct. And it would know more about
what that entails. Now, you're laughing-

00:55:59.648 --> 00:55:59.798
- What a crazy world

00:55:59.798 --> 00:56:01.408
- ... but it's true. And in fact-

00:56:01.808 --> 00:56:02.118
- Oh my God

00:56:02.148 --> 00:56:09.548
- ... a good parallel to this is the AGENTS
MD/CLAUDE MD files. There was a hot moment

00:56:09.948 --> 00:56:14.578
where it was all the rage about micro-
optimizing that. Oh, you tell your agent this-

00:56:14.578 --> 00:56:18.348
... you tell your agent that, and you ended
up with this huge file of instructions

00:56:18.848 --> 00:56:22.818
for your agent. One of the
things that Boris, working on

00:56:23.288 --> 00:56:27.098
Claude Code, shared in an interview
recently about Opus 5 was

00:56:27.128 --> 00:56:30.918
that the system prompt that
they ship for Opus 5, and

00:56:30.928 --> 00:56:33.938
presumably also Fable, shrunk by 80%

00:56:35.128 --> 00:56:38.688
because the agent not only
needed far less human

00:56:38.708 --> 00:56:42.568
instruction, it was actually being damaged

00:56:43.108 --> 00:56:46.977
by overly prescriptive
humans. And any programmer

00:56:47.008 --> 00:56:50.808
who's had a pointy-haired boss
knows exactly what that is like.

00:56:51.068 --> 00:56:54.648
When the boss walks into the
room, doesn't know anything,

00:56:55.108 --> 00:56:59.088
starts telling you how to
program, how to code. What do you

00:56:59.148 --> 00:57:02.988
do? You sulk. You write shittier code if

00:57:03.028 --> 00:57:06.128
you're mandated to do things that
are against your better judgment.

00:57:06.208 --> 00:57:07.788
Why would an agent not be the same?

00:57:08.028 --> 00:57:11.628
- Actually, to push back against myself, I
do find the actual skill I'm currently

00:57:11.648 --> 00:57:15.588
developing, which I along those lines
is probably what everybody's trying to

00:57:15.608 --> 00:57:19.788
develop, is not to overspecify things,
is to get out of the system's way.

00:57:20.268 --> 00:57:20.288
- Of course.

00:57:20.298 --> 00:57:25.128
- Is to specify enough to provide a
kinda high-level vision and guidance.

00:57:25.468 --> 00:57:28.578
- It's even better because you
don't even need that. The

00:57:28.908 --> 00:57:32.688
fundamental insight of modern software
development came from the agile

00:57:32.768 --> 00:57:37.688
software development movement. In the
late '90s, early 2000s, a group of

00:57:38.608 --> 00:57:42.528
smart, honest, and brave people came together
and said, "The way we've been trying to

00:57:42.568 --> 00:57:46.528
do software development for
the last 40 to 50 years has

00:57:46.537 --> 00:57:49.808
not worked, will not work.
We've been trying to

00:57:49.948 --> 00:57:53.838
specify upfront what software
should look like. We thought

00:57:53.868 --> 00:57:57.488
that we could ask humans what
they wanted, write it all

00:57:57.548 --> 00:58:01.368
down, apply our systems
thinking and our rigor to a

00:58:01.548 --> 00:58:05.468
spec sheet, hand that spec sheet
over to a group of programmers

00:58:05.508 --> 00:58:09.037
who would then implement just that, and
everyone would be happy." Well, guess what?

00:58:09.048 --> 00:58:12.438
No one was happy. Because no one
knows what they want until they

00:58:12.488 --> 00:58:16.088
receive it. You don't know what
a program should do until you

00:58:16.188 --> 00:58:19.718
play with it. So in the
agentic age, you should

00:58:19.748 --> 00:58:23.537
resist the temptation to be
overly specific upfront. Be as

00:58:23.728 --> 00:58:29.008
vague as you can to manifest something,
then interact with the something.

00:58:29.368 --> 00:58:32.938
The way you arrive at good software is
you write a little bit of software,

00:58:33.408 --> 00:58:37.128
and then you try to use it. It is in
the process of using software that you

00:58:37.148 --> 00:58:38.688
discover what you really want.

00:58:38.988 --> 00:58:42.968
It's where you discover what's
important and what's not important. And

00:58:43.068 --> 00:58:46.148
agents are incredible at allowing you to

00:58:46.748 --> 00:58:50.668
bumble into this experience of
realizing what you want because

00:58:50.808 --> 00:58:51.768
you don't know what you want.

00:58:51.848 --> 00:58:55.577
- Mm-hmm. And I've actually done something
similar to what you suggested, which is

00:58:55.608 --> 00:59:00.948
implement different designs and totally
different implementations, and then

00:59:01.128 --> 00:59:05.028
I create HTML pages for myself,
PHP pages, where I vote on

00:59:05.048 --> 00:59:06.148
what I like more and so on.

00:59:06.218 --> 00:59:10.028
And it does this iterative thing, and it's
very nice and fun. The interaction with

00:59:10.068 --> 00:59:13.748
the agentic system, if you create a nice
interface for yourself, it can be super fun,

00:59:14.188 --> 00:59:17.828
and you're focusing on the design part and
the most fun parts of the design part.

00:59:18.028 --> 00:59:20.428
- Correct. And this is what
humans are really good at.

00:59:20.508 --> 00:59:21.088
- Yeah, taste.

00:59:21.128 --> 00:59:24.668
- Differential evaluation. You give me three

00:59:24.748 --> 00:59:28.708
options, I pick one of them. Now,
humans actually fall off a cliff if

00:59:28.748 --> 00:59:32.488
you give them 22 options. That's the paradox
of choice. But you give them three options,

00:59:32.528 --> 00:59:36.048
they very quickly within a split
second will tell you what they like.

00:59:36.848 --> 00:59:40.728
There are all these ways
that exploit the fact that

00:59:40.768 --> 00:59:44.228
humans will make these snap judgments
that are actually pre-intellectual,

00:59:44.568 --> 00:59:47.948
right? Like, they're coming from
the gut, and then they arrive up in

00:59:48.048 --> 00:59:51.888
the, in the brain, and the brain
tries to rationalize why the

00:59:51.908 --> 00:59:55.468
gut said what it said. And if you're

00:59:57.128 --> 01:00:00.548
willing to let go of some of
that intellectual pre-processing

01:00:00.708 --> 01:00:06.228
rationalization and simply let your gut
drive, the agentic age is a revelation.

01:00:06.308 --> 01:00:10.148
- So this is a fascinating thing to
ask you because you have sort of

01:00:10.208 --> 01:00:16.548
famously for a long time talked about,
like, detailed chiseling of beautiful

01:00:17.128 --> 01:00:21.088
Rails Ruby code, and now you have switched

01:00:21.128 --> 01:00:24.848
in a matter of months to not
doing that. So, like, what is

01:00:24.928 --> 01:00:28.648
your current setup look like?
'Cause you're, I assume, are still

01:00:28.688 --> 01:00:32.448
looking for the beauty, for the chiseling,
for the crafting, but you're just

01:00:32.488 --> 01:00:35.018
crafting in a different medium.

01:00:35.658 --> 01:00:39.608
- And at different levels of abstraction. When
I'm working in Ruby code, and we have a lot

01:00:39.648 --> 01:00:43.618
of Ruby code because it's in our entire
business, and I'm asking agents to make changes

01:00:43.628 --> 01:00:47.012
to that code, I still sweat the details.

01:00:48.572 --> 01:00:54.172
What I'm coming to realize
is that the economic payoff

01:00:54.972 --> 01:00:58.892
of that sweat is diminishing
rapidly. The reason

01:00:59.452 --> 01:01:03.372
why I, for 25 years, was
sweating every line of

01:01:03.492 --> 01:01:10.572
code so judiciously was because I knew
the payoff of keeping an architecture

01:01:10.612 --> 01:01:16.722
coherent and malleable was software
that could change and evolve-

01:01:16.722 --> 01:01:20.672
quickly with a small team,
and not exorbitant cost,

01:01:20.972 --> 01:01:24.932
and not introducing a bunch of bugs when you
change one thing over the other. That was

01:01:25.032 --> 01:01:28.872
the driving economic argument
for why you should write

01:01:28.932 --> 01:01:32.842
beautiful code, 'cause beautiful code is
easier to understand, it is simpler, it

01:01:32.872 --> 01:01:39.332
is more malleable. That was premised
on humans doing the modifications.

01:01:40.612 --> 01:01:45.032
I think it is an open question to
which degree this still matters. Now,

01:01:46.832 --> 01:01:50.772
it does matter, and the reason I say
that, at least for the moment, is that

01:01:50.812 --> 01:01:57.892
tokens are still scarce. At this moment
in time, we are all token limited.

01:01:58.852 --> 01:02:02.552
Well, not all, but anyone who doesn't
have endless budgets are token

01:02:02.592 --> 01:02:06.472
limited. So therefore,
there is great payoff

01:02:06.532 --> 01:02:10.452
to writing systems that agents
have an easier time dealing with

01:02:10.472 --> 01:02:14.072
and evolving without having to relearn
the entire context. Just like humans,

01:02:14.832 --> 01:02:18.692
if they can make iterations
and changes to the code base

01:02:18.712 --> 01:02:21.992
without wrecking the architecture,
they can make the next

01:02:22.072 --> 01:02:25.632
change just as cheaply as the
last one. And this is the

01:02:25.692 --> 01:02:31.832
classic ball of mud where you end up
with a system that's a ball of mud-

01:02:31.872 --> 01:02:35.732
... because it's just put together
in a way where nothing is

01:02:35.772 --> 01:02:39.752
connected and it's just a real mess, right?
I've seen that with agents, and I've seen

01:02:39.792 --> 01:02:43.602
it in our own code bases where,
again, the first PR is, like,

01:02:43.612 --> 01:02:47.592
mediocre of quality, and then if you add
another PR on top of that, and then five more

01:02:47.672 --> 01:02:51.312
down the line, it's not very good, right?
So there's still a payoff to that,

01:02:51.652 --> 01:02:55.221
but that payoff is premised on
our current moment. I am now able

01:02:55.412 --> 01:02:59.332
to... And this is, by the way, where the
AI psychosis really comes in, when you

01:02:59.372 --> 01:03:02.812
try to extrapolate what nine months
from now it's gonna look like.

01:03:02.892 --> 01:03:06.392
What two years from now it's gonna look
like. But as an intellectual experiment,

01:03:07.072 --> 01:03:09.812
I think the first computer
I used was a Commodore 64.

01:03:10.092 --> 01:03:10.832
- Me too. Cool.

01:03:11.312 --> 01:03:15.912
- It had one megahertz
CPU and 64K of memory.

01:03:16.292 --> 01:03:20.132
Everyone who wrote software
for that machine internalized

01:03:20.192 --> 01:03:23.932
certain constraints. Well, all
the constraints. That machine was

01:03:24.012 --> 01:03:27.872
nothing but constraints. If
you put that programmer in a

01:03:27.912 --> 01:03:31.332
time machine and teleported him or her to

01:03:31.372 --> 01:03:35.152
2026 and gave them a
modern computer today and

01:03:35.212 --> 01:03:38.632
said, "Now write me a piece of software,"
they'd be lost for a little bit

01:03:38.992 --> 01:03:41.632
because all their techniques and all their

01:03:42.452 --> 01:03:46.432
heuristics would simply be wrong. Or
not wrong, because efficient software

01:03:46.452 --> 01:03:50.822
is still beautiful. They would be out of
date with the value that they could create.

01:03:51.312 --> 01:03:55.252
The amount of optimization you have
to apply for a one megahertz computer

01:03:55.272 --> 01:03:59.052
to produce a video game is just very
different from what you have to apply today.

01:03:59.252 --> 01:04:03.212
- Just imagine that guy that was programming
back then on a Commodore. Just re-

01:04:03.332 --> 01:04:04.632
remember all those,

01:04:05.532 --> 01:04:10.302
like what programming felt like and what
it meant, highly constrained resources-

01:04:10.312 --> 01:04:11.092
... how slow everything is.

01:04:11.292 --> 01:04:12.672
- Beautiful in many ways. Beautiful.

01:04:12.752 --> 01:04:14.842
- It's chiseling. But-

01:04:15.132 --> 01:04:15.142
- It-

01:04:15.142 --> 01:04:16.942
- ... imagine that person coming now.

01:04:17.792 --> 01:04:21.732
- It's romantic too, though. Let's also imagine
that. This is one of the things, whenever

01:04:21.792 --> 01:04:27.112
we bemoan modern car culture, for
example, right? And then we think like,

01:04:27.212 --> 01:04:30.752
"Oh, wasn't it better when we all had the
horse-drawn carriages and so on?" No, it wasn't.

01:04:31.012 --> 01:04:35.352
Do you know what New York smelled like
when we used horses for transportation?

01:04:35.612 --> 01:04:38.292
It was an open sewer. They
just shat everywhere.

01:04:38.352 --> 01:04:42.652
- Yeah, but have you played Red Dead Redemption?
You know how cool those cowboys were-

01:04:42.712 --> 01:04:42.922
- That is the way-

01:04:42.922 --> 01:04:43.192
- ... on the horse?

01:04:43.262 --> 01:04:47.162
- ... to experience the nostalgic
past, when you don't get the all

01:04:47.172 --> 01:04:49.832
the furry impulses and senses.

01:04:50.052 --> 01:04:53.942
- So basically programming, old
school programming by hand is

01:04:53.952 --> 01:04:57.012
kinda like the cowboy culture that we
romanticize and make movies about.

01:04:57.612 --> 01:05:01.132
- It's already happening, the
romanticization of handwritten code.

01:05:01.212 --> 01:05:04.962
And I have some of it because
it was a very romantic

01:05:05.052 --> 01:05:11.552
era. I'm grateful to have
been alive for 20 years of

01:05:12.352 --> 01:05:16.312
economically valuable handwritten code.
That was a, that was a good time.

01:05:16.632 --> 01:05:19.792
- Where handwritten beautiful
code was also valuable.

01:05:20.052 --> 01:05:23.932
- Correct. Because handwritten
beautiful code exists

01:05:24.172 --> 01:05:27.572
as much today as it did
yesterday. There are people who

01:05:27.932 --> 01:05:31.872
willfully write new video
games for the Commodore 64,

01:05:32.232 --> 01:05:36.092
or for the second Mega Drive, or
for other vintage consoles. In

01:05:36.132 --> 01:05:39.782
fact, one of my favorite
video games of late is

01:05:40.172 --> 01:05:42.972
ModRetro's. Do you know Palmer Luckey's-

01:05:43.092 --> 01:05:49.392
... side geschäft, which is recreating old
consoles. So he's recreated the Game Boy.

01:05:49.992 --> 01:05:50.532
- Nice.

01:05:51.192 --> 01:05:55.112
- It's amazing. Look it up. It's
really cool. But what's even

01:05:55.192 --> 01:05:58.692
cooler than the fact that he recreated
this hardware and the original

01:05:58.732 --> 01:06:02.552
chunkiness, but brought it up
to date with sort of modern

01:06:02.592 --> 01:06:06.292
screens and so on, now they just put
out the Nintendo 64 too. Incredible.

01:06:06.552 --> 01:06:11.902
Super cool. Wild attention to detail,
input lag, and all the other things.

01:06:12.032 --> 01:06:15.952
But what's also very cool, that
one, I have to wave. That one

01:06:16.152 --> 01:06:19.452
right there, and hover over it, 'cause then
you get to see what I'm talking about.

01:06:19.512 --> 01:06:22.672
That is the Chromatic- ...
Tetris re-implementation.

01:06:23.152 --> 01:06:23.182
- Wow.

01:06:23.292 --> 01:06:26.421
- I played a lot of Tetris
on the original Game Boy.

01:06:26.712 --> 01:06:29.752
Probably one of my top three
favorite games of all time.

01:06:30.892 --> 01:06:39.042
And they rewrote it with one change.
If you hit up, the brick slams.

01:06:39.352 --> 01:06:42.232
And that speeds up the game by about 400%.

01:06:43.292 --> 01:06:47.752
It is an incredible game, and
it's a new Game Boy game.

01:06:47.912 --> 01:06:51.908
I think the Game Boy came out
in- '89 or '88. It's a very

01:06:51.968 --> 01:06:55.348
old machine. The fact that there's
still people writing new old

01:06:55.428 --> 01:06:59.128
games is awesome, and the reason
they do it in part, now here

01:06:59.448 --> 01:07:03.368
they're doing it because they want Tetris, but there are
people who do it just for the love of the constraints,

01:07:03.748 --> 01:07:07.588
for the love of the romantic
notion of writing that. Just

01:07:07.728 --> 01:07:11.688
like there are people still riding
around on horses, not to get

01:07:11.708 --> 01:07:15.388
places fast, but because
they like the mode of

01:07:15.488 --> 01:07:19.048
transportation where it's
a literal biological

01:07:19.208 --> 01:07:21.528
being below you propelling you forward.

01:07:21.908 --> 01:07:25.608
- And also, we should say that that
beautiful code is great training

01:07:25.688 --> 01:07:29.848
data. So in some sense, the beauty
that we've built over a few decades-

01:07:30.088 --> 01:07:30.368
- Yes

01:07:30.508 --> 01:07:31.488
- ... continues.

01:07:31.768 --> 01:07:38.968
- We gave birth to this moment. What
a privilege, and I feel that very

01:07:39.008 --> 01:07:42.888
personally because I... Almost all of
the code I've ever written is public

01:07:42.968 --> 01:07:46.908
code. The vast majority of my career has
been dedicated to writing open source code,

01:07:47.428 --> 01:07:51.028
and therefore, some of that code,
some of those beautiful lines

01:07:51.248 --> 01:07:55.228
are in the training set. And in fact, I've

01:07:55.308 --> 01:07:59.248
heard people do this when they write
Ruby code. They ask the agent, "Write

01:07:59.288 --> 01:08:00.988
it like DHH would."

01:08:01.138 --> 01:08:04.898
And they're pleased with the result, and
that does warm my heart a little bit, that

01:08:05.408 --> 01:08:09.108
I helped give birth to this moment in my
tiny little small part. And anyone else

01:08:09.448 --> 01:08:12.788
who contributed open source code or any code
at all that the agents have had access to

01:08:13.328 --> 01:08:15.908
over the last, well, entire time

01:08:16.928 --> 01:08:20.908
programming has been around as
a discipline, is now part of

01:08:20.968 --> 01:08:22.068
these new agent overlords.

01:08:22.668 --> 01:08:26.638
- So you don't have a little bit of
sadness that writing beautiful

01:08:26.768 --> 01:08:28.928
code by hand is now less and less useful?

01:08:29.388 --> 01:08:32.908
- Am I sad that if I had
to get a job tomorrow,

01:08:33.428 --> 01:08:36.668
it's unlikely that I could apply my
skills in such a way that I would be

01:08:36.908 --> 01:08:41.168
paid to write these
manual lines of code? No.

01:08:42.088 --> 01:08:45.808
I did it for 25 years. Like, I
wanna see something new here,

01:08:45.848 --> 01:08:49.548
especially if we're all gonna live forever,
which I hope we don't. Maybe we'll get to that.

01:08:49.568 --> 01:08:53.478
But, like, I've done that. That's enough.
That's fine. I don't feel any more

01:08:53.568 --> 01:08:57.488
nostalgic about that than I do
about the fact that I don't have to

01:08:57.508 --> 01:09:01.428
spend my time in the field with
a hoe or at the assembly line.

01:09:01.488 --> 01:09:05.408
- Well, hold on a second. You are
one of the best programmers

01:09:05.428 --> 01:09:09.418
in the world at writing handcrafted
beautiful code, appreciators

01:09:09.448 --> 01:09:10.828
of beauty with great taste.

01:09:11.628 --> 01:09:11.768
- Correct.

01:09:11.888 --> 01:09:13.548
- That has been replaced.

01:09:13.848 --> 01:09:14.468
- I say humbly.

01:09:14.748 --> 01:09:18.288
- Yeah. That has been replaced.
Like, you had now...

01:09:18.788 --> 01:09:22.448
You're just fast evolving, and
you're able to... One of your

01:09:22.598 --> 01:09:26.568
really great qualities is you're able
to change your mind and evolve very

01:09:26.608 --> 01:09:29.628
quickly. But, like, you had
to kill that other person.

01:09:30.188 --> 01:09:34.068
- I am grateful for every moment that
brought me to where I am right now, and

01:09:34.148 --> 01:09:39.408
therefore, I have also tried, and
maybe this did take some practice. I

01:09:39.448 --> 01:09:43.148
will concede that. Maybe I
wasn't always like this, but

01:09:44.008 --> 01:09:47.858
stoic philosophy, amor
fati, loving your fate,

01:09:48.728 --> 01:09:52.708
is an incredibly liberating
way to live. And

01:09:53.548 --> 01:09:56.748
you could attack that and say,
"Well, that's a point of privilege."

01:09:57.548 --> 01:10:01.308
True, but also I had the
same directional point

01:10:01.488 --> 01:10:04.728
when I didn't have all this,
quote unquote, "privilege."

01:10:05.148 --> 01:10:08.788
So I think there's a way to live
and interact with the world

01:10:09.108 --> 01:10:12.748
where you accept the things you can
change and the things you cannot change

01:10:13.188 --> 01:10:15.208
and fall in love with all of it.

01:10:15.348 --> 01:10:19.108
Fall in love with the fact that
the world today has jumped

01:10:19.528 --> 01:10:23.948
two decades forward into technology
industry from where it was last year.

01:10:24.328 --> 01:10:27.808
- So can we talk about the general
anxiety that programmers feel,

01:10:28.568 --> 01:10:35.268
going through the same transformation?
They've maybe gone to university

01:10:36.308 --> 01:10:40.148
majored in computer science, dreamed of
being programmers and building stuff,

01:10:40.468 --> 01:10:44.308
high salary, and now everything's
changing, and there's deep

01:10:44.388 --> 01:10:47.948
anxiety about, "What do I do
when... with my life?" Can you

01:10:47.988 --> 01:10:50.948
empathize with that, and what the
hell are they supposed to do?

01:10:51.508 --> 01:10:55.318
- Hugely. Because just because
I have this disposition, I'm

01:10:55.368 --> 01:10:59.248
keenly aware that that's not
evenly distributed, that

01:10:59.288 --> 01:11:02.588
not everyone sits with this position
and this optimism for the future.

01:11:03.188 --> 01:11:06.648
But I do think I wanna
separate things a little bit.

01:11:08.068 --> 01:11:11.708
I think you're gonna have a
hard time coping with the new

01:11:11.748 --> 01:11:19.078
reality if the only thing you loved about
programming was the mechanical bits of

01:11:19.808 --> 01:11:25.588
putting the right logical constructs
together to produce something other people

01:11:25.668 --> 01:11:32.748
told you to produce. Because that
mechanical process is under threat.

01:11:33.948 --> 01:11:37.328
If you are, as you just mentioned,
excited about building things,

01:11:38.428 --> 01:11:42.398
I don't think you're under threat at all.
In fact, I think there's a great argument

01:11:42.448 --> 01:11:49.318
for us needing far more builders than
what we have now, and if you look at

01:11:49.348 --> 01:11:53.248
the employment stats, it's fuzzy.

01:11:53.308 --> 01:11:56.828
It's not clear what's gonna happen
at all. Some stats actually show an

01:11:56.928 --> 01:11:59.458
increase in openings because

01:12:00.668 --> 01:12:04.348
the advent of AI is so
dramatically lowering the price of

01:12:04.408 --> 01:12:08.398
programs that people want a lot
more programs. This is the classic,

01:12:09.348 --> 01:12:13.188
the Jevons paradox, that says when the price
of something goes down, there's gonna be more

01:12:13.248 --> 01:12:18.488
demand for it. And this is the
example of the ATMs too. When the ATM

01:12:18.528 --> 01:12:22.488
originally came, a lot of
bank tellers were very

01:12:22.528 --> 01:12:26.398
afraid for their job because suddenly there
was a machine that could dispense money from

01:12:26.428 --> 01:12:30.208
people's account. Well, what
the ATMs did was lower the

01:12:30.268 --> 01:12:34.188
price of a branch, and suddenly
banks could afford to open a lot

01:12:34.248 --> 01:12:38.238
more branches, and we ended up with more bank
tellers than we did before. Now, none of this

01:12:38.288 --> 01:12:41.888
is guaranteed. There are also
moments where things do change. The

01:12:42.108 --> 01:12:45.948
vast majority of people
worked in the fields up until

01:12:46.248 --> 01:12:50.288
the late 1800s, and then
suddenly we got mechanized-

01:12:51.188 --> 01:12:55.037
harvesting tools, and we did not
need human labor with a hoe and

01:12:55.108 --> 01:12:59.518
an ox out there. We had mechanical
tools to do that for us.

01:13:00.448 --> 01:13:04.108
In that moment, are there people
who were threatened that their

01:13:04.568 --> 01:13:08.368
job and their livelihood was at stake?
Yes. Was that a reasonable thing to

01:13:08.648 --> 01:13:13.628
feel threatened about? Yes. The
Luddites smashing the weaving machines-

01:13:13.728 --> 01:13:17.708
... in England had the same thing. And I
think maybe that's the better parallel

01:13:17.788 --> 01:13:21.528
than people working the fields because these
were actually highly skilled professionals

01:13:21.548 --> 01:13:23.408
doing a job that they liked on

01:13:24.288 --> 01:13:27.288
rather favorable conditions, not
slaving outside in the sun all day.

01:13:28.248 --> 01:13:33.677
But where we live now, would we
still like for clothing to be this

01:13:34.708 --> 01:13:38.518
heavily constrained resource?
Like, what if this T-shirt was

01:13:38.518 --> 01:13:42.268
$400? I mean, okay, then I'd have

01:13:42.328 --> 01:13:45.748
two. Well, my wardrobe probably
looks like I have two. But

01:13:46.748 --> 01:13:53.528
I think the general progress of
mankind depends on productivity

01:13:54.308 --> 01:13:58.198
improvements. And what are productivity improvements?
This is very important because in the abstract,

01:13:58.768 --> 01:14:01.968
I think everyone says, "Yay,
productivity. That's good."

01:14:02.848 --> 01:14:06.648
What did you think, this was all just
vibes? No. Productivity means fewer

01:14:06.748 --> 01:14:09.828
people to do the same number
or the same job. Now,

01:14:10.748 --> 01:14:14.038
the amount of job you want done may
increase, and therefore you get more people,

01:14:14.908 --> 01:14:17.748
but it also may not. There may be pockets

01:14:17.808 --> 01:14:21.508
where a company just
needs a certain set of

01:14:21.588 --> 01:14:25.568
fixed tasks done, and suddenly they
can do them with a 10th the number

01:14:25.608 --> 01:14:29.408
of people. This, by the
way, could... is tragic

01:14:29.488 --> 01:14:33.358
and difficult in the moment for the
individual being laid off. It's also amazing

01:14:33.428 --> 01:14:37.368
for the economy at large. Suddenly
you've freed up these resources who can

01:14:37.388 --> 01:14:40.148
now go do more productive
things. This is how the whole

01:14:40.678 --> 01:14:44.568
economy evolves and improves
and how we get growth,

01:14:44.728 --> 01:14:48.248
and growth is good. I think this
is the other thing to make a

01:14:48.348 --> 01:14:51.848
strong defense for, that growth in

01:14:51.928 --> 01:14:55.888
general is a good thing.
We do not want to wind the

01:14:55.948 --> 01:14:59.648
clock back to 1920 or 1950. We should

01:14:59.688 --> 01:15:02.908
be excited about the fact
that we've discovered new

01:15:02.988 --> 01:15:06.968
technology that allows us
to do vastly more. And

01:15:08.008 --> 01:15:11.948
while some of the tasks that may
be taken over by AI were tasks

01:15:11.958 --> 01:15:15.548
that humans were excited to do, and
they're just no longer economically

01:15:15.568 --> 01:15:19.508
viable, a lot of the tasks were just
drudgery. This is certainly true

01:15:19.608 --> 01:15:23.568
of my programming. There
were moments of programming

01:15:23.668 --> 01:15:27.488
I thought were amazing. These were the
moments that produced that state of

01:15:27.588 --> 01:15:34.408
flow we talked about last time, and
they were ecstatic. How often did they

01:15:34.448 --> 01:15:37.788
happen out of the course of a
year? If you took your average

01:15:37.928 --> 01:15:41.778
programmer, how much time
out of 2,000 hours at

01:15:41.808 --> 01:15:45.928
the job did that person
spend in a flow state?

01:15:47.548 --> 01:15:54.108
100? 200? 25? I think all those answers
could be plausible for someone.

01:15:54.768 --> 01:15:58.308
And taking that drudgery,
handing it over to

01:15:58.468 --> 01:16:02.188
machines, that is the
history of civilization.

01:16:03.408 --> 01:16:08.388
- Yeah, I mean, the hours spent debugging.
So programming has so many painful,

01:16:09.568 --> 01:16:14.528
painful hours in it. And now they're gone.
For the... I mean, for the most part,

01:16:14.668 --> 01:16:15.608
it's just fun.

01:16:16.008 --> 01:16:20.068
- A lot of it is gone in a lot of domains,
and the rest of it may be gone very soon.

01:16:20.968 --> 01:16:24.758
So that's where some of the
romanticism also comes in

01:16:24.758 --> 01:16:27.048
... because pain in the moment

01:16:27.988 --> 01:16:31.778
is difficult. It's hard. It's
annoying. Pain in the rear

01:16:31.848 --> 01:16:35.068
view mirror is accomplishment, proud,

01:16:35.588 --> 01:16:39.228
learning, steps forward, all
the things, right? So we

01:16:39.308 --> 01:16:43.208
encompass all of this as part of
the human experience, that we hate

01:16:43.228 --> 01:16:46.498
pain in the moment, but we cherish it when

01:16:46.548 --> 01:16:50.448
it's five minutes past and we
don't remember the hardship. We

01:16:50.508 --> 01:16:51.468
just remember the progress.

01:16:51.908 --> 01:16:55.708
- So certainly at the societal
level, at the macroeconomic

01:16:55.748 --> 01:16:59.668
level, growth is exciting. But the individual
level, there's going to be a lot of

01:16:59.688 --> 01:17:03.388
anxiety potentially a lot
of suffering. So by way of

01:17:03.428 --> 01:17:07.198
advice, if you're like a
young DHH or young developer

01:17:07.248 --> 01:17:11.688
programmer now, what
would you advise they do?

01:17:11.768 --> 01:17:15.208
- Don't try to anticipate anything.
You will literally go crazy.

01:17:15.468 --> 01:17:18.488
Because even the smartest
brains in the business

01:17:18.988 --> 01:17:22.808
cannot anticipate what two
model hops from here is

01:17:22.848 --> 01:17:26.688
going to look like. It's an
absolute waste of time, and you

01:17:26.728 --> 01:17:30.668
will develop an AI psychosis
trying to deduce what two

01:17:30.708 --> 01:17:34.288
years from now is gonna look like. Focus
on right now, and right now is the most

01:17:34.328 --> 01:17:38.388
incredible time to be into computers.

01:17:38.648 --> 01:17:43.588
You can make them do the most amazing
things if you lean in. If you

01:17:45.188 --> 01:17:51.748
maybe for a hot moment force yourself
to learn where the state of the art is,

01:17:52.968 --> 01:17:57.048
I double dog dare you not to get
excited about what's possible.

01:17:57.088 --> 01:17:59.078
- And would you recommend building publicly?

01:17:59.608 --> 01:18:03.328
- I think it's optional actually
to build publicly. I think it's

01:18:03.368 --> 01:18:07.088
nice to do because then you get to be
part of a community, and there's some

01:18:07.248 --> 01:18:10.988
camaraderie in open source. Not
some camaraderie. There's an

01:18:11.048 --> 01:18:14.408
enormous amount of
camaraderie. Open source is an

01:18:14.588 --> 01:18:18.188
incredible source of
community and camaraderie,

01:18:18.628 --> 01:18:22.108
and it's also a great
way to stem some of that

01:18:22.268 --> 01:18:26.228
personal existential dread. If
you're amongst other humans, you can

01:18:26.268 --> 01:18:29.608
get infected with good
forms of mind viruses,

01:18:29.988 --> 01:18:33.688
exciting forms of mind viruses, the ones
that tell you that it's all gonna be okay,

01:18:34.448 --> 01:18:38.228
and the future looks bright, and if we

01:18:38.268 --> 01:18:41.748
build a bunch of things together,
we can do things none of us ever

01:18:41.788 --> 01:18:45.608
dreamed we could do. I was just
talking to Ryan Hughes, who's been

01:18:45.648 --> 01:18:48.918
my main partner on Omarchy
almost since day one,

01:18:49.428 --> 01:18:52.198
and we were literally talking
about yesterday how when

01:18:53.016 --> 01:18:56.596
We started on this journey
together. Our ambitions were very

01:18:56.696 --> 01:19:00.485
modest. We're just like, "Can we
get this distro to not crash at

01:19:00.756 --> 01:19:04.476
3:00 AM because of an Arch
package that just got pushed out

01:19:04.536 --> 01:19:08.466
outside of our control? That would
be amazing. Then it'd be great." Now

01:19:08.496 --> 01:19:12.076
we're leaning back and thinking,
"How can we take over the world?

01:19:12.766 --> 01:19:20.656
How can we make Omarchy go to Mars? How can
we do everything we've ever dreamed of?"

01:19:21.436 --> 01:19:24.996
And that level of ambition
inflation is partly

01:19:25.036 --> 01:19:28.925
because things have gotten better with
agents, but hugely also because we've

01:19:28.996 --> 01:19:32.596
surrounded ourselves with others
excited about the same journey.

01:19:33.356 --> 01:19:37.976
And if you're just sitting
in your little isolated cave

01:19:38.416 --> 01:19:41.926
worrying about the future, yeah,
you're gonna go a little nuts.

01:19:42.336 --> 01:19:46.136
I think if we learned
anything during COVID is

01:19:46.196 --> 01:19:49.336
that people will go nuts
sitting in their little cave

01:19:49.836 --> 01:19:53.346
inside by themselves worrying
about what's gonna happen. That is

01:19:53.396 --> 01:20:00.296
indistinguishable from depression,
ruminating endlessly about things you can't

01:20:00.336 --> 01:20:06.356
control. I'm sorry to channel some Jensen
Huang here, but that's just loser talk.

01:20:07.416 --> 01:20:12.876
You don't have to be a loser.
You can choose to lean in and-

01:20:13.216 --> 01:20:16.956
... win. And win is very
broadly defined as learning

01:20:17.056 --> 01:20:21.296
more, making more, contributing
more, being part of more.

01:20:23.276 --> 01:20:28.676
And at the end of the day, what are your
choices? You don't have a choice, mate.

01:20:29.316 --> 01:20:32.936
The future's coming whether you like it
or not, so you might as well choose to be

01:20:32.976 --> 01:20:33.856
excited about it.

01:20:34.096 --> 01:20:37.656
- But I mean, we should emphasize
the fact that planning is nearly

01:20:37.696 --> 01:20:41.536
impossible with AI. I mean,
most of us plan our life a

01:20:41.576 --> 01:20:44.936
little bit. When we're younger, you
have, like, hopes and dreams. There's a

01:20:44.956 --> 01:20:47.866
reason you go to college.
There's a plan underlying that.

01:20:47.896 --> 01:20:48.456
- I agree with that.

01:20:48.716 --> 01:20:48.956
- But there-

01:20:48.986 --> 01:20:49.456
- I'll concede that.

01:20:49.556 --> 01:20:50.916
- ... it is kinda-

01:20:50.996 --> 01:20:51.236
- This is different.

01:20:51.336 --> 01:20:56.916
- ... nuts that it's impossible to really
plan. Because, like, you really don't know,

01:20:58.196 --> 01:21:02.335
if there will even be a Claude
Code, a Codex, and Cursor

01:21:03.196 --> 01:21:07.056
six months from now. Maybe it'll be
you'll be talking to WhatsApp. Maybe

01:21:07.076 --> 01:21:10.556
it'll be all open call type.
Maybe it'll just all be voice.

01:21:10.976 --> 01:21:14.876
Maybe Omarchy will take over the world and it'll
be voice. Maybe there won't be operating systems

01:21:14.916 --> 01:21:18.856
anymore. You'll just... There'll be this
glowing orb that just you just talk

01:21:18.916 --> 01:21:22.636
to because if natural language
is enough to accomplish

01:21:22.756 --> 01:21:26.536
all tasks in life, like, what is
life about? Like, what are the major

01:21:26.576 --> 01:21:30.466
tasks you need to accomplish in life?
If you can just do that with voice, and

01:21:30.516 --> 01:21:34.446
it writes all the programs for you,
and all is systematized, and what,

01:21:34.476 --> 01:21:38.116
what are the jobs needed then?
Maybe it's more about the service

01:21:38.176 --> 01:21:43.836
industry. Maybe there won't be programmers,
like agentic engineers at all. And so

01:21:44.936 --> 01:21:48.596
yes, your advice is wise to focus on
the moment, but that doesn't mean

01:21:48.676 --> 01:21:52.416
that it's just a crazy rollercoaster
ride, 'cause we don't know

01:21:52.816 --> 01:21:55.176
what this December or what
this January will look like.

01:21:55.476 --> 01:21:59.345
- We don't know, and therefore, we
should choose to have faith that it's

01:21:59.356 --> 01:22:03.356
gonna pan out. That is my best advice

01:22:04.396 --> 01:22:10.716
towards fighting anxiety, is that you
have a choice, to a large degree,

01:22:11.476 --> 01:22:13.736
of how to lean.

01:22:13.856 --> 01:22:19.596
Do you wanna lean into PDoom? PDoom is gonna
happen whether you lean into it or not.

01:22:21.596 --> 01:22:22.916
You don't have control.

01:22:22.936 --> 01:22:26.916
- But there's also actual daily schedule of
how much learning are you doing, how much

01:22:26.996 --> 01:22:30.896
building are you doing, how much
uh, diversification you're doing.

01:22:31.316 --> 01:22:35.176
And for example, I traveled across
rural China recently, and I did

01:22:35.196 --> 01:22:40.896
it quite deliberately because you're
talking to people in China, in,

01:22:41.116 --> 01:22:46.816
in rural anywhere, where time moves much
slower, where there's no discussions

01:22:46.896 --> 01:22:50.856
about agentic engineering. And the ti- and

01:22:50.996 --> 01:22:53.966
I started by going on the,
on the Great Wall of China.

01:22:54.796 --> 01:22:58.656
That's timeless. That spans
centuries. And, you know, there

01:22:58.696 --> 01:23:02.096
was, like, this kind of tension of FOMO,
like maybe I'm missing out on something.

01:23:02.676 --> 01:23:06.306
But then I very deliberately
wanted to find the

01:23:06.336 --> 01:23:10.156
timelessness of human existence,
really plug into the fact that,

01:23:10.216 --> 01:23:13.786
okay, it always feels like
everything's changing uh,

01:23:14.056 --> 01:23:18.016
throughout human history but, you
know, the universals are still true,

01:23:18.576 --> 01:23:22.356
of what matters in life. And I just wanted
to make su- keep connected to that.

01:23:22.596 --> 01:23:26.296
- You're not gonna miss anything.
This is the part that actually

01:23:26.336 --> 01:23:30.096
grinded my gears in the
early phases of this

01:23:30.156 --> 01:23:34.056
transition. Everyone was
so up in arms that if you

01:23:34.076 --> 01:23:37.836
didn't... It's loops now. Oh, no, no,
we're done with loops. It's graphs

01:23:37.896 --> 01:23:41.736
now. Oh, no, no, we're done with that. It's, it
harnesses this, right? They're, they're constantly

01:23:41.856 --> 01:23:45.536
churning through the frontier,
which in one way is actually

01:23:45.936 --> 01:23:49.736
very exciting. This is what happens
when a new field opens up. It's like a

01:23:49.796 --> 01:23:53.536
new portal, and it's all spilling
out. That's exciting. But it

01:23:53.596 --> 01:23:57.206
also means that if I had just been
backpacking for the last year,

01:23:58.456 --> 01:23:59.706
hadn't touched a computer,

01:24:00.636 --> 01:24:04.516
hadn't witnessed this agentic
moment, and I just showed up

01:24:04.616 --> 01:24:07.736
yesterday, do you know what? I
would've been caught up in two weeks.

01:24:08.856 --> 01:24:12.836
There's not any accumulation,
which is in some ways a

01:24:12.896 --> 01:24:16.796
great relief. If you missed
the past year, you can catch

01:24:16.816 --> 01:24:18.476
up to the frontier in two weeks.

01:24:19.516 --> 01:24:23.356
If you're a programmer who was out
hiking the Himalayas for a year and

01:24:23.436 --> 01:24:26.996
you come back, you can catch up in two
weeks. And this is actually the great

01:24:27.056 --> 01:24:30.936
credit to the progress we're
experiencing. We have so

01:24:31.036 --> 01:24:34.606
many experiments running
simultaneously right now that are

01:24:34.606 --> 01:24:38.456
constantly and ruthlessly
sorting what works, what

01:24:38.496 --> 01:24:42.416
doesn't work. You don't have to
remember or even be part of that

01:24:42.456 --> 01:24:44.876
entire journey. You can just
show up for the results.

01:24:45.136 --> 01:24:49.126
- But the key step there, when you come
back from the backpacking journey,

01:24:49.816 --> 01:24:53.886
is to be willing to become a totally
new, different human because-

01:24:55.184 --> 01:24:56.424
Things are changing so fast.

01:24:56.864 --> 01:24:56.874
- That's right.

01:24:56.874 --> 01:25:00.624
- I mean, literally, if you left for the
backpacking journey- ... in October last

01:25:00.724 --> 01:25:03.744
year and came back in April or May-

01:25:04.024 --> 01:25:06.784
- You might as well have been in
the cryo chamber for 100 years

01:25:06.804 --> 01:25:10.784
- ... it's like, "What, what do you mean? We're
not programming anymore?" This, it's a it's a

01:25:10.884 --> 01:25:11.864
huge transformation.

01:25:12.044 --> 01:25:13.844
- It's startling, and I actually... I mean,

01:25:14.864 --> 01:25:18.644
I'm putting some of this on
slightly for effect. I also

01:25:18.864 --> 01:25:22.624
recognize that it's okay to
grieve for a hot moment.

01:25:22.704 --> 01:25:27.664
That's not my disposition. It's not how
I do it, but I accept that that's a

01:25:29.124 --> 01:25:33.284
time-tested mechanism for coping with

01:25:34.264 --> 01:25:38.244
a sense of loss. And it's okay to
have a sense of loss that the world

01:25:38.284 --> 01:25:42.924
that you knew and maybe you were
very fond of is no longer the same.

01:25:43.064 --> 01:25:46.933
- I maybe I should say out loud and
explicitly that I do have that, the Native

01:25:47.004 --> 01:25:50.664
American with a tear rolling down
my... Like, there's a sadness.

01:25:51.264 --> 01:25:54.804
It's a goodbye. It's a goodbye
to the old world of programming.

01:25:55.104 --> 01:25:56.524
- That's, it's so fascinating to me.

01:25:56.984 --> 01:25:58.444
- I've spent so many years-

01:25:58.824 --> 01:26:02.484
- But it all amounted to this moment.
That is, to me, what makes the

01:26:02.504 --> 01:26:04.104
entire journey so meaningful.

01:26:05.104 --> 01:26:08.944
AI did not arrive from the sky.
It was not alien technology that

01:26:08.964 --> 01:26:12.404
had blasted through the universe and
suddenly showed up on our shores.

01:26:13.044 --> 01:26:16.924
AI has been around for as long as
computer science has been around.

01:26:17.344 --> 01:26:21.024
In the '50s, they thought the problem
was gonna be cracked in about a

01:26:21.044 --> 01:26:24.654
decade, the smartest people at the
time. It took a little longer,

01:26:25.484 --> 01:26:29.154
but the reason it took a little
longer was in part because we took

01:26:29.184 --> 01:26:32.944
some blind turns on the
road to AI and neural

01:26:33.004 --> 01:26:36.744
networks and thought that there were
other symbolic representations that were

01:26:36.764 --> 01:26:40.134
gonna carry the day, and they didn't.
And that probably set us back

01:26:40.224 --> 01:26:43.904
about 15 years. So some
of it was also we had to,

01:26:44.004 --> 01:26:47.844
Go through the gaming
revolution, 3D games. If we had

01:26:47.904 --> 01:26:51.424
not had Quake, if we had not had
Duke Nukem, if we had not had

01:26:51.604 --> 01:26:55.484
Unreal Tournament, we'd never have gotten AI
because we would never have gotten the GPUs,

01:26:55.904 --> 01:27:00.204
and therefore we would never have been
able to get AI in the shape it is now. So

01:27:01.124 --> 01:27:05.533
everything we did up until this
moment was required to get there.

01:27:05.564 --> 01:27:08.944
You were part of that.
Holy... What a blessing.

01:27:09.924 --> 01:27:13.484
What a blessing to have contributed
in your small part, either by

01:27:13.564 --> 01:27:17.464
training or by playing video games.
Literally, you could just choose to

01:27:17.504 --> 01:27:20.184
think of it that way. All
those times you spent in,

01:27:21.004 --> 01:27:23.454
for my case, in the '90s just
playing video games, they were-

01:27:23.454 --> 01:27:24.324
- You were contributing.

01:27:24.364 --> 01:27:27.184
- Yeah, I was contributing to the AI
revolution right there and then moment.

01:27:27.244 --> 01:27:30.824
- To the progress of human civilization.
No, for sure. Civilization

01:27:30.844 --> 01:27:34.784
progresses with the death of the old
and the birth of the new, and it

01:27:34.824 --> 01:27:38.784
continues in this way, but it's still the
grieving process is a part of it, I think.

01:27:39.044 --> 01:27:42.964
- Take a moment. It's okay. Take
a... And also, you don't even

01:27:43.024 --> 01:27:50.464
have to accept that it's all dead. Like,
if you are still very attached to the hand

01:27:50.484 --> 01:27:55.724
chiseling, which if you'd asked me a
year ago, I would have predicted that I

01:27:55.744 --> 01:27:58.624
would've been more
attached. I'm surprised-

01:27:58.784 --> 01:27:59.494
- I'm kinda surprised

01:27:59.494 --> 01:28:03.484
- ... a little that I've not been more attached.
But the reason I've not been more attached,

01:28:03.604 --> 01:28:05.924
I think, is that I found
something more fun.

01:28:07.324 --> 01:28:11.214
And if it hadn't been like that, if AI had

01:28:11.244 --> 01:28:15.134
just replaced the thing I loved
and gave me something I hated, I'd

01:28:15.164 --> 01:28:15.944
probably be a little bitter.

01:28:16.064 --> 01:28:19.884
- I mean, this really feels... It's hilarious
and awesome to watch and inspiring to watch.

01:28:19.924 --> 01:28:25.424
It's like Picasso all of a
sudden like, getting access to

01:28:26.424 --> 01:28:30.344
to image generation and getting
super excited and switching in a

01:28:30.384 --> 01:28:34.154
matter of months. That's literally what happened.
So, like, it's an artist that appreciated beautiful

01:28:34.264 --> 01:28:37.704
code, and all of a sudden you're
talking about having fun-

01:28:37.844 --> 01:28:41.484
- I think Picasso is actually a good
parallel because if you look at his early

01:28:42.184 --> 01:28:45.804
work where he learned his
craft, it was painting

01:28:46.664 --> 01:28:50.424
realistic paintings, right?
Like, he was training to

01:28:50.524 --> 01:28:55.604
be a master of the old ways of depicting
reality the best way we knew how,

01:28:56.464 --> 01:29:01.414
and then along comes all these other
forms of expression. Cubism comes along.

01:29:01.684 --> 01:29:05.624
And all these forms of
abstract expression, he's

01:29:05.684 --> 01:29:09.624
not decrying that he's
not doing Renaissance

01:29:09.724 --> 01:29:13.704
paintings anymore, that he's not just
painting the perfect depiction of an

01:29:13.804 --> 01:29:17.544
apple. He's reimagining the
apple to be a freaking square,

01:29:18.384 --> 01:29:22.304
and he's getting excited
about that. And I feel that

01:29:22.314 --> 01:29:26.164
transition, that we've
gone from this one mode of

01:29:26.204 --> 01:29:30.034
expression, and we've suddenly
opened the gates where

01:29:31.264 --> 01:29:36.424
the, like, the color spectrum is so
much wider. I'm seeing more. And it's

01:29:37.284 --> 01:29:41.184
perhaps because the way I became
a programmer. I did not become

01:29:41.244 --> 01:29:46.244
a programmer because a deep
love of if statements.

01:29:46.344 --> 01:29:49.314
I became a programmer
because I wanted programs.

01:29:49.484 --> 01:29:53.444
I wanted things to exist that
did not exist. And at first,

01:29:53.564 --> 01:29:56.194
I had to learn how to
program to make that happen.

01:29:56.864 --> 01:30:00.364
Then I happened to fall in love with
programming as a craft, and then I spent two

01:30:00.444 --> 01:30:08.924
decades really diving deep on that. But now
I'm back. I'm back to having an idea and

01:30:08.984 --> 01:30:12.944
being impatient beyond belief
to see it exist in the world,

01:30:13.424 --> 01:30:20.704
and AI has just shrunk that down to
almost nothing. So it's an acceleration

01:30:20.764 --> 01:30:22.564
of a rediscovery.

01:30:23.524 --> 01:30:27.214
Now, I understand that that's not true
for everyone. There are plenty of

01:30:27.224 --> 01:30:31.054
programmers who just loved programming right
from the beginning, and they were attracted

01:30:31.104 --> 01:30:35.084
to the logical constructs and so forth. I mean,
I've been there for two decades, so I get

01:30:35.124 --> 01:30:41.154
it. But I also think that you're able
to discover new sides of yourself-

01:30:41.154 --> 01:30:45.174
... if you let it in. Again,
I keep coming back to this

01:30:46.524 --> 01:30:51.124
almost regret minimization framework,
to borrow Jeff Bezos's terms here.

01:30:52.144 --> 01:30:56.004
Are you gonna look at yourself two
years from now and think, "Oh, I spent

01:30:56.044 --> 01:30:59.644
my time well being pissy
about the present,

01:31:00.084 --> 01:31:03.504
about the fact that my
industry changed"? Or

01:31:04.276 --> 01:31:08.236
Would I two years from now look
back upon this moment and be proud

01:31:08.276 --> 01:31:12.196
of myself for leaning in,
for maybe having a moment of

01:31:12.236 --> 01:31:16.176
grief? Again, there's room for all
of it. And then going, "I'm gonna

01:31:16.216 --> 01:31:20.056
learn how the world spins now, and I'm
gonna make not just the best of it, I'm

01:31:20.096 --> 01:31:21.256
gonna make more of it."

01:31:22.016 --> 01:31:25.836
- I mean, it is true, a lot of
programmers, a lot of friends of mine,

01:31:26.776 --> 01:31:29.196
me, obviously you, are having fun.

01:31:30.216 --> 01:31:37.156
- I have had more fun with computers in
the last three months than at any time

01:31:37.476 --> 01:31:38.116
previously.

01:31:38.796 --> 01:31:42.436
- Quick bathroom break, and
then I gotta ask you about

01:31:42.856 --> 01:31:44.986
how you actually interact
with the systems now.

01:31:45.036 --> 01:31:45.936
- Yes.

01:31:46.596 --> 01:31:54.336
- I gotta ask you about how has your
programming setup changed? So keyboard,

01:31:54.456 --> 01:31:57.156
voice, what's the IDE?

01:31:57.756 --> 01:32:02.376
- It's crazy to think about now, but yeah,
I used TextMate for almost 20 years.

01:32:02.596 --> 01:32:04.276
I used TextMate starting in

01:32:05.556 --> 01:32:09.376
2005, I think. I helped get the
first version out, and then I just

01:32:09.416 --> 01:32:13.316
wasn't interested. I wasn't in the
market for an alternative. And

01:32:13.356 --> 01:32:20.756
it wasn't until the switch to Linux that
I was forced out of my habitat. And

01:32:21.056 --> 01:32:23.136
now with the switch to,

01:32:24.476 --> 01:32:28.436
what are we calling it? Agentic engineering?
Oh, I fucking hate that term. We gotta

01:32:28.496 --> 01:32:32.476
come up with something that sounds
as plain as programming, but

01:32:32.556 --> 01:32:34.676
encapsulates the fact that
it's with agents. But-

01:32:35.136 --> 01:32:36.406
- I still think it should be called
programming at this point.

01:32:36.406 --> 01:32:37.856
- ... All right, let's just
call it programming.

01:32:38.496 --> 01:32:41.876
Programming with agents requires a
different tool set. It really does.

01:32:42.376 --> 01:32:46.556
And the main change here is that

01:32:47.716 --> 01:32:52.416
you're going from single thread programming
in your head to parallel processing.

01:32:53.956 --> 01:32:58.696
When I was writing code, chiseling it
by hand in TextMate or even Neovim,

01:32:59.496 --> 01:33:03.436
Not that long ago, I would just focus
on one problem at the time, and

01:33:03.596 --> 01:33:07.356
I would methodically work my way through it,
and that was actually the portal to flow.

01:33:07.436 --> 01:33:09.676
The portal to flow was deep,

01:33:10.536 --> 01:33:14.016
immersion into a single problem,
see it through to the end.

01:33:15.356 --> 01:33:18.836
That's not how it works with
agents, in part because the

01:33:18.876 --> 01:33:24.856
agents are at once both too fast and too
slow. They don't give you an immediate

01:33:25.996 --> 01:33:29.976
reply on something that you
asked them to do that's the

01:33:30.056 --> 01:33:34.896
same as typing on a keyboard. So
you have to let the agent cook-

01:33:34.956 --> 01:33:38.296
... for a bit, and therefore
you realize, well,

01:33:39.436 --> 01:33:43.316
if I just sit around waiting for them,
first of all, that doesn't feel productive.

01:33:43.536 --> 01:33:47.296
Even if the agent, just one of them, can be
highly productive, it does not feel productive.

01:33:47.316 --> 01:33:50.616
It does not feel good. It feels actually
like you're a little bit useless.

01:33:50.656 --> 01:33:52.146
And maybe I had a moment,

01:33:52.976 --> 01:33:56.876
when the first agentic moment was
there and we, I was running mostly

01:33:56.936 --> 01:34:00.536
one agent at a time where I felt
like, "I don't know about this."

01:34:00.576 --> 01:34:04.326
But you can solve a lot of
hard problems by simply

01:34:04.356 --> 01:34:08.216
throwing more resources at it.
This is the whole scaling law of

01:34:08.276 --> 01:34:12.206
AI of itself, right? That
if you parallelize these

01:34:12.216 --> 01:34:14.945
things and you're not running one
agent, but you're running a handful,

01:34:15.956 --> 01:34:22.016
you can feel like you're in a flow
state because you're constantly doing

01:34:22.976 --> 01:34:26.885
programming work in the sense that you're
making decisions, and you're helping either

01:34:26.936 --> 01:34:30.906
unblock an agent because it has a question
about which direction to take, or

01:34:30.936 --> 01:34:34.366
you're ready for a new task. And
to do that, you need a different

01:34:34.396 --> 01:34:38.116
setup. I started first
doing it in tmux and just

01:34:38.156 --> 01:34:42.116
having separate panes and having
separate splits. Basically,

01:34:42.196 --> 01:34:45.996
a terminal with tabs is a good way to think
about it. You open a bunch of tabs. I think

01:34:46.516 --> 01:34:49.836
most humans know exactly how that
works. They don't work in just one tab.

01:34:50.036 --> 01:34:52.736
- So still sticking to the terminal, CLI.

01:34:53.136 --> 01:34:53.596
- Absolutely.

01:34:53.996 --> 01:34:55.996
- So you're not using
Claude Code app or the-

01:34:56.046 --> 01:34:56.086
- No.

01:34:56.116 --> 01:34:57.246
- ... Codex app or the-

01:34:57.436 --> 01:35:01.396
- I love the fact that this
agent revolution was kicked

01:35:01.476 --> 01:35:05.336
off in the terminal because I was
already a huge fan of TUIs, terminal

01:35:05.456 --> 01:35:08.936
user interfaces, and the terminal
in general. That feels like a,

01:35:10.396 --> 01:35:14.276
a really nice place to be. It's a
beautiful place to be. The modern terminal

01:35:14.316 --> 01:35:21.496
is just a good-looking place to
work. So I like that. And then

01:35:22.416 --> 01:35:26.276
this fact of having multiple
agents, especially once it's not

01:35:26.336 --> 01:35:29.856
just multiple agents running on your own
machine, but you start running multiple

01:35:29.936 --> 01:35:35.136
machines. Now tmux alone is not enough to

01:35:35.156 --> 01:35:38.996
keep track of it, and that's why as
of late I've switched to this thing

01:35:39.036 --> 01:35:44.436
called Herdr. And Herdr is
essentially tmux plus agent

01:35:44.916 --> 01:35:48.266
notifications. So whenever your agent is
done and needs something for you, it goes

01:35:48.636 --> 01:35:54.136
ding, a little bell telling
you it's ready for its human,

01:35:54.236 --> 01:35:58.036
uh... Is it master or servant?
I'm not quite sure always.

01:35:58.656 --> 01:36:02.606
But it is ready for a decision,
and it also keeps track of

