- Exactly.

01:50:13.440 --> 01:50:13.820
- It's crazy.

01:50:13.860 --> 01:50:17.100
- Which again, pairs... This was
why I was so excited that-

01:50:17.180 --> 01:50:21.390
... all these agent harnesses
arrived as TUI's, arrived as CLIs-

01:50:21.440 --> 01:50:24.960
... has arrived as a
terminal because suddenly,

01:50:25.280 --> 01:50:28.920
a whole new generation of both
programmers and regular users

01:50:28.960 --> 01:50:33.420
discovered the glory of the terminal. The
terminal was actually one of the most,

01:50:34.340 --> 01:50:38.250
impressive and productive
user interfaces we can have.

01:50:38.340 --> 01:50:42.340
Now, we've built user interfaces
since then that were easier to learn.

01:50:43.160 --> 01:50:46.500
The mouse is a wonderful invention for

01:50:47.260 --> 01:50:51.180
letting someone who doesn't know a lot about
computers just click around and find things out, but

01:50:51.220 --> 01:50:54.980
the terminal is for people who want to

01:50:55.080 --> 01:50:57.300
learn their systems, who want
to learn the keyboard commands.

01:50:57.320 --> 01:50:59.560
- So you think the TUI
is gonna stick around?

01:51:00.040 --> 01:51:03.760
- 100%. Now, it's not gonna be the only
thing. Of course it's not, because

01:51:03.840 --> 01:51:07.420
these labs now have trillion-dollar
valuations, so they need to be able to

01:51:07.480 --> 01:51:09.240
reach billions of people.

01:51:09.280 --> 01:51:12.760
And I don't think we're gonna teach billions
of people the intricacies of the TUI.

01:51:13.800 --> 01:51:17.760
So we're gonna have other tools, and we
already have other tools, which is, by

01:51:17.780 --> 01:51:22.840
the way, one of the sweet things
about Linux, the ChatGPT Codex tool.

01:51:22.920 --> 01:51:23.380
- Yeah, I saw that.

01:51:23.600 --> 01:51:27.440
- Literally within two hours of that
announcement dropping, I had it wrapped

01:51:27.540 --> 01:51:31.120
up and ready to go in Omakub.
Already now, if you go to... You can

01:51:31.160 --> 01:51:33.470
actually hit the Omakub key-

01:51:33.800 --> 01:51:37.970
... and then just write ChatGPT. It
should pop up as, like, install.

01:51:38.600 --> 01:51:39.500
And then just hit return.

01:51:41.040 --> 01:51:42.260
- Sudo password for Lex.

01:51:42.660 --> 01:51:46.640
- Yeah, whatever you put in. And then it's just gonna
install it, and it's gonna pop it up, and you have

01:51:47.100 --> 01:51:47.640
ChatGPT.

01:51:47.940 --> 01:51:48.160
- Nice.

01:51:48.360 --> 01:51:52.180
- And that's how a lot of the apps are, and
all the agentic harnesses are built in that

01:51:52.240 --> 01:51:53.829
way.

01:51:53.829 --> 01:51:57.480
Um, Tailscale, as I talked about, if you
wanna hop on that, is that way. Uh, Dropbox.

01:51:57.600 --> 01:52:00.500
And the reason all these things are
in there is because I use them.

01:52:00.560 --> 01:52:04.420
It's because I built this system for me,
and I thought, first and foremost, how do I

01:52:04.540 --> 01:52:08.400
build my dream computer just
for me? And if I get my

01:52:08.480 --> 01:52:12.440
dream computer, I am assured there are other
people who are gonna have the same dreams

01:52:12.500 --> 01:52:12.920
as I do.

01:52:13.300 --> 01:52:17.220
- Now, you're obsessed about getting
the installation to be under one

01:52:17.280 --> 01:52:21.260
minute, and a bunch of people
asked you online why. So

01:52:21.340 --> 01:52:25.240
Why? Why were you so obsessed
to getting the installation to

01:52:25.300 --> 01:52:27.420
be less than 60 seconds?

01:52:27.680 --> 01:52:31.600
- First, let me quote Mitchell
Hashimoto, who created

01:52:31.640 --> 01:52:35.609
Ghostty terminal that's awesome and
also available on Omakub and was also

01:52:35.660 --> 01:52:39.420
the founder of HashiCorp,
who had this great post on

01:52:39.520 --> 01:52:42.980
X not too long ago that
had the killer line, "The

01:52:43.020 --> 01:52:47.000
pursuit of excellence
deserves no explanation."

01:52:47.960 --> 01:52:55.220
Wanting to make something as good and as
fast and as beautiful as possible has

01:52:56.400 --> 01:53:02.620
no need for justification. We
should want beautiful, fast systems

01:53:02.880 --> 01:53:06.840
that are a delight to use. Now, the funny
thing with the install time is it didn't

01:53:06.920 --> 01:53:10.910
actually start with a one-minute
mission. I was being modest when it got

01:53:10.940 --> 01:53:14.870
going. I thought if I could set up
my entire system in 15 minutes,

01:53:15.280 --> 01:53:19.180
it would already be such a dramatic
improvement over what came before it that I

01:53:19.220 --> 01:53:23.100
would be perfectly happy. And it came
off the fact that the benchmarking

01:53:23.140 --> 01:53:27.040
had been skewed so out of whack. You
talked about the Commodore 64 being

01:53:27.080 --> 01:53:27.910
your first computer, right?

01:53:28.460 --> 01:53:32.060
When you unwrap that Commodore
64 and you hit the on button,

01:53:32.640 --> 01:53:36.460
in I think about less than one
second, the basic interpreter

01:53:36.500 --> 01:53:40.460
was ready to accept your command.
It was literally almost instant.

01:53:40.500 --> 01:53:43.900
There was virtually no boot time.
Do you know how long it took me

01:53:44.420 --> 01:53:48.220
to set up a new Mac that I had
bought for actually the one

01:53:48.240 --> 01:53:52.560
app that I still haven't vibe coded my way
into on Linux, which is Adobe Lightroom?

01:53:53.860 --> 01:53:56.720
Brand-new computer, out of the box.

01:53:57.640 --> 01:54:01.300
I set it up. There's a software
update. Before I was ready to

01:54:01.320 --> 01:54:06.879
install Adobe Lightroom that I had it
fully up to date, it took 42 minutes.

01:54:08.260 --> 01:54:11.940
That's grotesque. That's
absurd. That's an insult

01:54:12.500 --> 01:54:15.320
to everyone who likes computers,
that you get a new computer and

01:54:16.140 --> 01:54:20.080
before you can use it, you have
to spend 42 minutes doing updates

01:54:20.440 --> 01:54:24.330
on something that was already installed
on it out of the box from the factory.

01:54:24.840 --> 01:54:28.820
That is just preposterous, and it's
crazy that that's not even the

01:54:28.840 --> 01:54:31.900
worst thing. I also got a PC, um,

01:54:32.720 --> 01:54:34.800
because all these Linux machines,
they come as PCs, right?

01:54:34.880 --> 01:54:34.980
- Right.

01:54:35.000 --> 01:54:38.700
- And they have Windows pre-installed.
So I got one. This was just

01:54:38.960 --> 01:54:43.280
three weeks ago. Brand-new machine,
Intel Panther Lake, the whole shebang.

01:54:44.180 --> 01:54:46.420
An hour and 35 minutes

01:54:47.400 --> 01:54:51.020
from unwrapping the thing until it's
ready to use. How long did you take

01:54:51.060 --> 01:54:53.020
before you think you were in Omakub?

01:54:53.900 --> 01:54:55.240
- I mean, it's less than a minute for sure.

01:54:55.300 --> 01:54:57.160
- A minute.

01:54:57.200 --> 01:55:01.120
We can build computers like this. We have
the technology. We've had the technology

01:55:01.200 --> 01:55:04.780
since like 1981 when the
Commodore 64 first came

01:55:04.840 --> 01:55:08.600
out. The fact that we now have a
computer that's a trillion times faster

01:55:08.880 --> 01:55:12.600
and you have to spend 42 minutes or an hour
and a half setting it up before it's ready to

01:55:12.700 --> 01:55:18.220
use is so infuriating to me that it
became a passion. And it's funny

01:55:19.040 --> 01:55:22.900
Just while we were having a little break here,
I was talking to Ryan Hughes, my co-conspirator

01:55:22.940 --> 01:55:26.800
here. We now have, we think,
a line where we can create

01:55:26.820 --> 01:55:30.100
these special turbo images that are
pre-made for specific computers

01:55:30.180 --> 01:55:34.130
because it relies on some
knowledge of the hardware, like

01:55:34.180 --> 01:55:37.620
the Dell XPS. We're gonna
make a Dell XPS turbo

01:55:37.760 --> 01:55:43.950
image that can install in about
12 seconds. A full Linux system

01:55:43.950 --> 01:55:44.470
installed in about 12 seconds.

01:55:44.470 --> 01:55:45.180
- That's incredible.

01:55:45.840 --> 01:55:52.500
- That obsession is what gives me this
great satisfaction with using computers,

01:55:52.680 --> 01:55:56.000
that we do not accept things the
way they are. And this is why this

01:55:56.100 --> 01:56:01.660
agent-accelerated reality that I
now inhabit is so invigorating.

01:56:01.720 --> 01:56:05.480
Because I can look at these kinds of
constraints and go, do you know what? Um,

01:56:06.540 --> 01:56:10.400
I could have stopped when we were at five minutes.
That would already have been fast enough.

01:56:10.480 --> 01:56:12.600
This was the argument on X, right?

01:56:12.740 --> 01:56:16.600
Why do you keep going? Five minutes
is fast enough. Okay, maybe

01:56:16.660 --> 01:56:20.500
for you, but for me, 12
seconds is a lot more fun. And

01:56:21.020 --> 01:56:23.839
I always try to think
these first principles.

01:56:24.720 --> 01:56:28.560
Okay, in that machine,
actually hit the Omarchy,

01:56:28.700 --> 01:56:32.480
key again, and then do speed. You'll see a

01:56:32.520 --> 01:56:34.760
list come up, and one of
them is, like, disk speed.

01:56:35.440 --> 01:56:37.520
- Speed test, network speed, disk speed.

01:56:37.840 --> 01:56:39.760
- Disk speed. Do do that
one, and then run it.

01:56:40.680 --> 01:56:44.530
So this is testing the performance of the
built-in NVMe drive, and what are you

01:56:44.560 --> 01:56:44.750
getting?

01:56:45.560 --> 01:56:47.070
- Seven GB a second.

01:56:47.620 --> 01:56:51.520
- So seven gigabytes a second. That's
how fast that drive is. Why can we

01:56:51.580 --> 01:56:53.880
not install a Linux
distribution in one second?

01:56:54.720 --> 01:56:57.680
The Omarchy distribution is 5.8 gigabytes.

01:56:58.460 --> 01:57:01.980
If we can run at seven gig... Now, of
course, you can't actually do that

01:57:02.160 --> 01:57:05.520
because the USB key is a lot slower
than that, but you should be able to

01:57:05.700 --> 01:57:09.060
maximize the underlying physics

01:57:09.780 --> 01:57:13.740
and protocols that we're dealing
with, and that's your goal. We gotta

01:57:13.760 --> 01:57:15.080
get all the way back down to that.

01:57:15.140 --> 01:57:20.320
- Yeah. I love that you're doing a Linux
distribution. 'Cause I mean, you could be

01:57:21.240 --> 01:57:25.120
do... You could be building anything. But
the fact that you're building a Linux

01:57:25.140 --> 01:57:28.740
distribution means you're rethinking
about what a computer is, 'cause a

01:57:28.880 --> 01:57:32.390
operating system is the fundamental,
like, the foundation of how

01:57:32.450 --> 01:57:33.290
... you interact with a computer.

01:57:33.400 --> 01:57:34.800
- Yes. And it's been so stale.

01:57:34.900 --> 01:57:35.190
- Yeah, it has.

01:57:35.190 --> 01:57:38.640
- The macOS we have today is

01:57:38.740 --> 01:57:42.580
scarcely different from the macOS we
had 10 years ago. In fact, it's worse

01:57:42.760 --> 01:57:46.340
in many ways because Apple continues to

01:57:46.500 --> 01:57:50.360
chokehold the control on
users. They don't want them to

01:57:50.380 --> 01:57:54.000
install software that they
haven't already said good-

01:57:54.060 --> 01:57:57.390
... to go for. They don't want
users to reconfigure their

01:57:57.440 --> 01:58:01.300
hotkeys without doing it manually.
They didn't want to change the

01:58:01.340 --> 01:58:05.020
500-millisecond animation
switching workspaces.

01:58:05.400 --> 01:58:08.570
It is an infuriatingly
locked-down computer. Now,

01:58:09.440 --> 01:58:12.880
to Apple's credit, it's a pretty
good computer for being locked down,

01:58:13.660 --> 01:58:17.420
but I don't want a locked-down computer.
I wanna own my computer. Better

01:58:17.500 --> 01:58:21.400
yet, I wanna mutate my computer,
and this is where the agentic

01:58:21.410 --> 01:58:24.040
age needs a new operating system.

01:58:24.880 --> 01:58:28.760
When you can vibe code whatever app
comes to your mind, you should be able

01:58:28.780 --> 01:58:32.740
to vibe code your operating system. You should
be able to change anything, how it looks,

01:58:32.840 --> 01:58:36.480
how it works, what panels are
there, what are not there, and that

01:58:36.520 --> 01:58:40.200
requires Linux. As simple as that.
There are no... None of the other two

01:58:40.260 --> 01:58:41.500
operating systems can deliver this.

01:58:41.960 --> 01:58:45.860
- But can you speak to the, I think
somebody asked you, like, "Who the hell

01:58:45.920 --> 01:58:48.560
are you to think you could do
better than Ubuntu?" For example.

01:58:49.520 --> 01:58:53.390
Or, or like, "Why are you doing it when Ubuntu
is already pretty good?" And your answer

01:58:53.480 --> 01:58:54.750
was, "I thought I could do better."

01:58:55.660 --> 01:59:00.400
So can you speak to, this was on
X, can you speak to, like, where

01:59:01.360 --> 01:59:05.200
the craziness comes from,
thinking that you as

01:59:05.280 --> 01:59:09.130
one person or maybe with Ryan, you
could start building something that's

01:59:09.160 --> 01:59:12.440
going to be better than Ubuntu
or Arch or any of this?

01:59:12.900 --> 01:59:16.320
- Well, I'll say that it
didn't start quite as

01:59:16.360 --> 01:59:20.220
grandiose. When I got going with
my Linux journey, I actually just

01:59:20.280 --> 01:59:22.560
started with Ubuntu, and I just
built on top of it. Here's a

01:59:23.380 --> 01:59:26.960
system that already does a lot of the
things I wanted to do, and at least it's

01:59:27.060 --> 01:59:31.040
Linux. So can I just build on top of it and make
it more modern, make it actually look good,

01:59:31.080 --> 01:59:34.560
and have the programs I wanna use?
I got, like, some version of that,

01:59:35.260 --> 01:59:39.219
and then I discovered that there's seven
layers deeper you could go, and as, the

01:59:39.540 --> 01:59:43.360
closer you get to the metal, the closer you
get to the kernel, the closer you get to the

01:59:43.400 --> 01:59:46.580
individual packages and so
forth, the more freedom you

01:59:46.640 --> 01:59:50.620
discover. So as I discovered more and
more freedom, I got more and more

01:59:50.640 --> 01:59:54.289
ambitious, and I realized, oh, surprise,

01:59:54.380 --> 01:59:58.190
surprise, I have strong opinions about
how computers should work, how they

01:59:58.220 --> 02:00:01.620
should look, what programs
should be installed, and

02:00:01.680 --> 02:00:05.200
whether they should take
42 minutes to install or

02:00:05.260 --> 02:00:09.140
45 seconds. That's the current
world record, by the way.

02:00:09.200 --> 02:00:13.090
So if anyone installs Omarchy Quattro
after this and can beat 45 seconds,

02:00:13.200 --> 02:00:16.880
take a screenshot, send it to me, and you'll
be the new holder of the world record.

02:00:16.960 --> 02:00:19.880
- When, how long ago did you
cross the 60-second mark?

02:00:19.920 --> 02:00:22.220
- It's funny because it's like

02:00:23.460 --> 02:00:27.930
that the old limit on, what was it? It
was a four-minute mile or something-

02:00:28.020 --> 02:00:28.070
- Yeah. Uh-huh

02:00:28.070 --> 02:00:30.190
- ... like that in 1952-

02:00:30.190 --> 02:00:33.650
... that people thought was impossible for
humans to beat. Then one guy beats it,

02:00:34.060 --> 02:00:36.810
and literally within the next few
months, three more guys do, right?

02:00:37.080 --> 02:00:40.500
So we had this goal. I originally
had the goal it was two minutes.

02:00:40.560 --> 02:00:43.970
And then we beat two minutes, and I'm like,
"Well, if we beat two minutes, why can't we beat

02:00:44.360 --> 02:00:47.900
one minute?" And I remember thinking
like, "That'd be crazy. You can't

02:00:47.940 --> 02:00:51.720
install a modern operating system
in one minute when they're all..."

02:00:52.460 --> 02:00:56.430
If the other ones are taking
42 minutes, right? Like Apple,

02:00:56.920 --> 02:01:00.820
full of a lot of smart people, like, it took
42 minutes, so that's gotta be the bar.

02:01:00.880 --> 02:01:04.850
No, no, it's not the bar because there's
no speed limit. Like, none of the things

02:01:04.880 --> 02:01:08.860
you take for granted are fixed
pillars of reality in almost

02:01:08.870 --> 02:01:12.080
any case. Until you get
down to the seven gigabytes

02:01:12.940 --> 02:01:16.860
per second that drive is able to transfer,
you've not reached the limit yet,

02:01:16.920 --> 02:01:17.800
so you should just keep pushing.

02:01:18.600 --> 02:01:22.308
And of course, then it becomes a game
in itself and a satisfaction itself.

02:01:22.408 --> 02:01:26.208
Every second I could shave off was an
excitement. And when we broke the one-minute

02:01:26.308 --> 02:01:28.468
barrier, which was really
only broken, I think,

02:01:29.368 --> 02:01:32.988
three weeks ago or something like that,
maybe even two weeks ago, I just got crazy.

02:01:33.228 --> 02:01:37.208
And then I got a swarm of agents
to run all these auto research

02:01:37.288 --> 02:01:41.168
loops of trying all these different theories
on like, oh, if you change this thing, if you

02:01:41.228 --> 02:01:45.208
change that thing, what if you cut this out? What
if you do things in parallel? What if while you

02:01:45.268 --> 02:01:47.178
were typing in your username, we start

02:01:48.008 --> 02:01:51.938
preloading packages into memory system, we
can fire them in faster. And it becomes a

02:01:51.968 --> 02:01:55.408
fun game. And I think that's how you should think
about product development. It should be fun.

02:01:55.888 --> 02:01:59.848
You should, it should be frivolous.
It should be trying to overshoot

02:01:59.888 --> 02:02:03.868
the target and making it so much better
than what it quote unquote needs to be.

02:02:04.328 --> 02:02:08.258
- Especially now because you can move so quickly and
you can try so many different approaches because of

02:02:08.288 --> 02:02:09.228
the agents.

02:02:09.508 --> 02:02:13.088
- Yes, that just makes it more satisfying. But I
think this was always true. If you look at the

02:02:13.128 --> 02:02:17.208
greatest Mercedes-Benz of all time,
I think it was called the W126.

02:02:18.688 --> 02:02:22.648
I saw this wonderful documentary on it.
It was steered by this guy who for years

02:02:22.728 --> 02:02:25.828
had worked in Mercedes'
department for safety.

02:02:26.858 --> 02:02:30.708
And suddenly he gets put in charge
for the whole S-Class project, and he

02:02:30.788 --> 02:02:34.448
just puts in all of it. This is where
you got the auto-retending seat

02:02:34.528 --> 02:02:38.328
belts and washers on the headlights and

02:02:38.608 --> 02:02:42.528
everything. This car was so
overbuilt for the market.

02:02:42.608 --> 02:02:46.008
It was unbelievable. But you look at
that and go like, "Well, I want that."

02:02:46.808 --> 02:02:49.368
In just the same way that you look at a

02:02:50.348 --> 02:02:54.328
mechanical watch and go, "It
could go down to 100 meters?"

02:02:54.548 --> 02:02:57.888
Or I think Rolex has one that can
go down to 4,000 meters, the,

02:02:58.568 --> 02:03:00.908
the deep ocean, the deep diver
ocean ... or whatever it's called.

02:03:01.868 --> 02:03:05.768
No one outside of three people in the
world, whoever buy that watch, will

02:03:05.808 --> 02:03:10.148
ever put it to the test. But don't
you wanna be the kind of person who

02:03:11.488 --> 02:03:15.268
is a patron of people who wanna
push the human race that much

02:03:15.348 --> 02:03:19.308
forward? I want the fastest car
breaking the speed limits. I want the

02:03:19.348 --> 02:03:23.308
diver watch that can go down
the Mariana Trench. I want

02:03:23.328 --> 02:03:26.548
the operating system that can
install in less than 60 seconds.

02:03:26.968 --> 02:03:30.668
- So from the individual level,
I admire those people. I think

02:03:30.728 --> 02:03:34.508
everybody should strive for that. But
also the consequences of that we should

02:03:34.568 --> 02:03:39.248
mention, like the pursuit of excellence,
even when the metrics seem to be frivolous,

02:03:40.188 --> 02:03:43.828
the maybe unexpected consequences
of that is other kinds of

02:03:43.908 --> 02:03:47.848
excellences, discoveries, innovations, and all
that kind of stuff that has nothing to do with the

02:03:48.008 --> 02:03:48.328
speed.

02:03:48.648 --> 02:03:49.158
- 100%.

02:03:49.228 --> 02:03:52.608
- It's discovery. It's nice to
have the metric that you're

02:03:52.668 --> 02:03:56.448
chasing be the catalyst,
the engine that drives

02:03:56.488 --> 02:04:00.408
innovation, because you'll just discover. It's
the same reason you go to the moon, you go to

02:04:00.448 --> 02:04:04.108
Mars. It's like, why? Who cares? Well,

02:04:04.188 --> 02:04:07.668
actually, the real thing
is you'll discover a lot

02:04:07.708 --> 02:04:10.868
about chemical propulsion.
You'll discover a lot

02:04:10.968 --> 02:04:14.068
about how humans can live for a long time

02:04:14.908 --> 02:04:18.708
in space. Maybe you'll discover whole
new ways of doing solar energy in

02:04:18.748 --> 02:04:21.208
space or data centers in
space or whatever the hell.

02:04:21.888 --> 02:04:22.018
- That's right.

02:04:22.048 --> 02:04:23.528
- All kinds of... Maybe you'll meet--

02:04:23.538 --> 02:04:23.718
- That's right

02:04:23.718 --> 02:04:28.608
- ...aliens, but you never
know. But setting a goal

02:04:30.008 --> 02:04:32.608
and chasing it, especially when
it's numerical, is really nice.

02:04:32.978 --> 02:04:36.548
- And setting a grand goal
is even better. When you

02:04:36.608 --> 02:04:43.948
refuse to just do what's practical and
you strive for what's slightly beyond

02:04:44.108 --> 02:04:47.968
plausible, that's when the
magic happens. And we should be

02:04:49.068 --> 02:04:52.778
going all in on that. This is one of the
reasons... And in fact, to some degree,

02:04:53.168 --> 02:04:56.888
this specific goal was motivated
by seeing others do this. I mean,

02:04:57.368 --> 02:05:00.768
Elon is the singular individual
in this conversation.

02:05:01.968 --> 02:05:05.808
The guy is so out there in his goals.

02:05:06.218 --> 02:05:10.048
They seem so preposterous at
first glance. I've certainly

02:05:10.228 --> 02:05:16.788
considered them preposterous on many
glances. And then you realize that

02:05:18.068 --> 02:05:21.768
they come true sometimes. Maybe
not always on schedule, and so

02:05:21.868 --> 02:05:25.528
what? So what? So we didn't get
the self-driving car on schedule.

02:05:26.068 --> 02:05:30.268
I gave Elon a lot of shit for that
back in '17. But now it's here,

02:05:31.528 --> 02:05:35.508
and who would have gotten us here except
for the person who thought it was

02:05:35.528 --> 02:05:43.168
possible in '17, who is so
impatient with reality that the

02:05:43.248 --> 02:05:47.128
sheer force is just gonna propel
that reality to the place he

02:05:47.168 --> 02:05:51.208
wants it to be. I think that's deeply
inspiring, and we need these kinds of,

02:05:52.308 --> 02:05:56.288
goalposts to be moved. We need
someone to run the four-minute

02:05:56.388 --> 02:06:00.288
mile, so the rest of us realize,
"Well, okay, but I guess I can run it

02:06:00.348 --> 02:06:04.148
quicker." That's what I try to strive
for, and I really strive for with Omarchy

02:06:04.168 --> 02:06:04.678
because

02:06:05.948 --> 02:06:09.808
it's just more fun to live that way, to
have high aspirations and ambitions. And I

02:06:09.828 --> 02:06:13.788
will say I've actually changed my mind on that
a little bit. I spent quite a lot of time

02:06:15.048 --> 02:06:20.968
speaking the other book that there are a
lot of people who don't actually need those

02:06:21.708 --> 02:06:25.418
ambitions. And I also-- That's
also true. We don't want

02:06:26.148 --> 02:06:29.708
8 billion Elons running around the
world. Would be rather crazy, I think.

02:06:29.768 --> 02:06:33.198
But I've come to realize
that for entrepreneurs,

02:06:33.588 --> 02:06:41.708
it's healthy to have suitable chunky goals.
The idea of just running the Italian

02:06:41.808 --> 02:06:45.728
restaurant can't just be
that. It should be maybe

02:06:45.828 --> 02:06:49.388
I just run the one Italian restaurant,
but I make the best damn pizza

02:06:49.508 --> 02:06:53.688
possible. I make the
most delicious Parmesan.

02:06:55.068 --> 02:06:59.848
We have to strive for excellence to
muster the motivation to keep going.

02:07:00.608 --> 02:07:04.448
- And yeah, to dare to think big
like you could build a Linux-

02:07:05.028 --> 02:07:05.368
- Yes

02:07:05.388 --> 02:07:06.168
- ... distribution.

02:07:07.028 --> 02:07:10.988
Is there some stuff you remember
about what it took to get us to be

02:07:11.068 --> 02:07:14.688
so fast? You mentioned a few things
that were, the agents were discovering.

02:07:15.488 --> 02:07:18.668
- One of the things was to treat the

02:07:19.584 --> 02:07:23.024
... lag of human input as an
opportunity to preload. So--

02:07:23.524 --> 02:07:26.544
Hmm ... there's five questions you answer
when- Yeah ... you set up an Omarchy machine.

02:07:26.564 --> 02:07:30.444
So it's doing stuff in the background. We're doing stuff
in the background. And that was one of the things I didn't

02:07:30.524 --> 02:07:33.544
even consider, which is, I mean,
the oldest trick in the book.

02:07:34.164 --> 02:07:38.064
All sorts of video games for a long
time have done this, where you let the

02:07:38.204 --> 02:07:42.114
player interact with something, and then you
do preloading and so forth in the background.

02:07:42.144 --> 02:07:45.294
So it's an age-old technique, but I just
hadn't thought about it for an installer.

02:07:45.524 --> 02:07:49.014
I thought, first you ask the user some
questions about their password and their

02:07:49.064 --> 02:07:52.994
username and their time zone and so
forth, and then you do the work,

02:07:53.544 --> 02:07:57.204
when, in fact, you could preload that. Now,
the other things were just a bunch of other

02:07:58.084 --> 02:08:02.064
tweaks to how the installer itself

02:08:02.124 --> 02:08:05.764
was doing things in a certain order.
Some of them was also just shrinking it.

02:08:06.104 --> 02:08:10.054
The last version of
Omarchy was 7.5 gigabytes,

02:08:11.064 --> 02:08:13.464
and a lot of the time we're
down to now is literally

02:08:13.504 --> 02:08:17.404
decompressing compressed package
files. That's the bulk of what is

02:08:17.464 --> 02:08:21.434
on the ISO, and that's the bulk of the install
time. So by shrinking it down to, I think we're

02:08:21.444 --> 02:08:25.634
at 5.85 gigabytes now, a
pretty chunky reduction,

02:08:26.484 --> 02:08:30.304
it almost literally translated one-to-one, and
I did some really interesting things there.

02:08:30.404 --> 02:08:34.334
So Omarchy uses the
JetBrains font, which is

02:08:34.384 --> 02:08:38.364
an awesome font, beautiful font,
and actually a font I didn't like

02:08:38.404 --> 02:08:41.644
at first glance, but then it was
the only font I could get to look

02:08:41.784 --> 02:08:45.694
perfect in Mitchell
Hashimoto's Ghostty. Ghostty,

02:08:45.784 --> 02:08:49.764
for whatever reason, did not
wanna render my beloved Bitstream

02:08:49.964 --> 02:08:54.714
Vera Sans just the way I was used
to it. Mm-hmm. But it rendered

02:08:55.724 --> 02:08:59.464
this JetBrains font perfectly,
so I switched over. But it...

02:08:59.884 --> 02:09:03.644
The standard package for
JetBrains font on Arch

02:09:03.744 --> 02:09:07.664
is 200 megabytes because it includes
all these variations and all

02:09:07.704 --> 02:09:08.994
these subtypes and whatever.

02:09:09.904 --> 02:09:13.764
And I realized, like, we're not using
any of that. We're using the monospace

02:09:13.864 --> 02:09:17.604
version in this one Nerd,
uh- Yep... font patched

02:09:17.684 --> 02:09:21.264
edition. Do you know how big that
is? I think it's 16 megabytes. Hmm.

02:09:21.904 --> 02:09:25.634
So I simply just came up with a
new package, the slim version-

02:09:25.824 --> 02:09:29.604
Mm-hmm... of the JetBrains package. Nice. And
there, right there, I saved 180 megabytes.

02:09:30.284 --> 02:09:33.784
I just went through and did that a bunch
of times. We did that with the NVIDIA

02:09:34.404 --> 02:09:38.164
drivers too. So the NVIDIA drivers, when
they came straight off the, I think the

02:09:38.284 --> 02:09:42.244
Arch repo, were not compressed
with the most extreme form

02:09:42.304 --> 02:09:46.024
of compression, which I think
called ZSTD, which is a

02:09:46.104 --> 02:09:50.024
really slow form of compression. It's slow to
create the archive, but if you're building a

02:09:50.104 --> 02:09:53.604
package- Hmm... you have all the time in the
world if that means you can then shrink the ISO.

02:09:53.724 --> 02:09:57.244
So on just the two NVIDIA
packages, I think we saved

02:09:57.464 --> 02:10:01.164
200 megabytes. Mm-hmm. So
what I loved about this

02:10:01.184 --> 02:10:05.104
was I imagined myself
being a car designer at

02:10:05.144 --> 02:10:09.024
McLaren. So McLaren makes
some of the lightest

02:10:09.504 --> 02:10:11.644
super sports cars in the world right now,

02:10:12.564 --> 02:10:16.274
and they are absolutely
obsessed with shaving every

02:10:16.584 --> 02:10:23.564
damn last gram off their cars. That's
why even a new 750S McLaren is just way

02:10:23.624 --> 02:10:26.924
lighter than the competition.
They use a carbon fiber

02:10:27.124 --> 02:10:31.114
monocoque, and that helps them over
like Ferrari, for example, that's

02:10:31.114 --> 02:10:35.054
uses aluminum. But they also just have this
obsession with weight, and I would see this

02:10:35.144 --> 02:10:39.004
interview with the McLaren folks talking
to some journalist just going over, "Yeah,

02:10:39.104 --> 02:10:42.984
we saved, uh- ... 370
grams- Yeah ... off that

02:10:43.024 --> 02:10:47.884
one." And I just went, "Wait, what?
There's someone on a car that weighs maybe

02:10:48.864 --> 02:10:54.873
1,040 kilos or 1,380 or something.
They're s- They're worrying about

02:10:55.124 --> 02:10:58.484
370 grams?" Yeah. What obsessed

02:10:58.764 --> 02:11:02.804
maniacs. Hmm. I love them. Yeah. I want
that in my life. I want a McLaren-

02:11:03.724 --> 02:11:07.704
... just for that reason. So I thought
in that moment as I was shaving

02:11:07.784 --> 02:11:11.653
individual megabytes off the
packages, man, I'm kinda

02:11:11.664 --> 02:11:15.484
like a McLaren car developer here, even though- Yeah
... I mean, we haven't even completed that work.

02:11:15.524 --> 02:11:19.334
The package could shrink even
more. But this is actually this

02:11:19.384 --> 02:11:22.764
other conflict that we've had with
Omarchy and the traditional Linux

02:11:23.224 --> 02:11:26.804
community is bloat. Mm-hmm. This idea that

02:11:26.904 --> 02:11:30.484
it's actually not kosher in
certain circles to ship with

02:11:30.584 --> 02:11:34.424
pre-installed software because
who are you to make decisions

02:11:34.484 --> 02:11:38.374
about what programs people
should use? And I'm like, "Dude,

02:11:38.403 --> 02:11:42.324
it's in the fucking name. It's called
Omarchy because the Oma part is

02:11:42.384 --> 02:11:45.844
short for omakase, which
literally means chef's choice.

02:11:46.284 --> 02:11:49.954
I'm the chef. I'm making the
choices, and this is the

02:11:50.004 --> 02:11:53.744
collection of applications I think is
awesome," which by the way has a video

02:11:53.784 --> 02:11:57.664
editor. It ships with OBS- Mm-hmm ...
to do your recording. It's just in

02:11:57.704 --> 02:12:01.664
there. Yeah. OBS is pre-installed.
Nice. Kdenlive is a

02:12:01.684 --> 02:12:05.594
video editor, timeline editor, which is great.
I edit all my videos with that. And then I made

02:12:05.624 --> 02:12:09.414
my own clip editor. You talked
about the thing with syncing up.

02:12:09.694 --> 02:12:13.644
Yeah. I had this thing with clips, so I would
do a podcast or whatever. I need a clip for X.

02:12:13.724 --> 02:12:17.464
Mm-hmm. And it's kind of a hassle to use Kdenlive
or other- Mm-hmm ... timeline editors, so I made

02:12:17.524 --> 02:12:21.424
Omacut. Mm-hmm. Omacut is included too. Omacut,
cool. It's just for making clips, and you can

02:12:21.464 --> 02:12:25.344
drive the whole thing with keyboard. So you
can go to just the right time mark- Mm-hmm ...

02:12:25.384 --> 02:12:29.284
and then you can hit Control + Space.
Hmm. It moves the clip line to that line.

02:12:29.344 --> 02:12:33.024
Then you go to- Nice ... the end of the clip, and
you hit Alt + Space, and it moves the end line.

02:12:33.344 --> 02:12:37.184
Nice. And then you hit Control + S, and
it saves. Nice. I can make clips so damn

02:12:37.244 --> 02:12:39.264
fast now with Omacut. It's amazing. But

02:12:40.424 --> 02:12:44.324
it has all the software on it. It
has Neovim on it. It has Herder

02:12:44.424 --> 02:12:48.204
on it. It has Tmux. It has a
terminal. It has a bunch of

02:12:48.264 --> 02:12:52.044
themes. It has a bunch of background
images, the best ones I could pick

02:12:52.144 --> 02:12:56.574
out. That takes up some space. It should
because this is not supposed to be this

02:12:57.404 --> 02:13:01.324
just barren landscape where you have to reconstruct
everything. Mm-hmm. This is supposed to be

02:13:01.484 --> 02:13:05.124
a productive system the minute you
unpack it. What is it built in?

02:13:05.884 --> 02:13:09.204
So it's actually mostly
built in Bash for the

02:13:09.824 --> 02:13:13.423
Linux configuration part. Right.
Bash is a great language for that.

02:13:14.204 --> 02:13:17.994
It is made for that really,
for system administration.

02:13:18.414 --> 02:13:21.480
It also has its limitations. You
should not build everything in Bash

02:13:21.740 --> 02:13:23.580
- How are agents with
generating Bash? Pretty good?

02:13:23.660 --> 02:13:25.830
- Oh, amazing. Except for one thing,

02:13:27.160 --> 02:13:30.660
and I have to smack the agents over the back
of the head every single time I catch it,

02:13:31.140 --> 02:13:34.980
and remind it to look at the agent's
MD file, because I will have an

02:13:35.020 --> 02:13:37.400
instruction in there not
to make early exits.

02:13:37.820 --> 02:13:43.450
The agents love to have preconditions
instead of fully expanded conditionals.

02:13:43.900 --> 02:13:47.700
I like something that says,
"If this, then do that."

02:13:47.880 --> 02:13:53.120
Agents like to have precondition or
exit, another precondition or exit.

02:13:53.180 --> 02:13:57.020
And then it falls down to the final
thing it actually wants to do. I hate

02:13:57.080 --> 02:13:59.940
that style. So except
for that, it's gotten

02:14:00.580 --> 02:14:04.500
shockingly, dramatically,
awesomely good, to the

02:14:04.540 --> 02:14:08.530
point where when we talked last time, we
talked about how I would ask the agent

02:14:08.540 --> 02:14:11.180
for a Bash thing, and then I would type it
in myself, and I kinda wouldn't remember.

02:14:11.240 --> 02:14:15.000
And then I took the effort to make sure that
all that competence wasn't draining out of my

02:14:15.060 --> 02:14:18.809
fingers, so I actually learned how to
write Bash myself, and now I'm not

02:14:18.860 --> 02:14:22.660
writing any Bash myself. I have not written any Bash
myself for probably a couple months, because the

02:14:22.700 --> 02:14:26.340
agents have just gotten so good, that
if you give them the few pointers on

02:14:26.460 --> 02:14:29.800
style, and occasionally put pushback
on complexity... This is...

02:14:30.560 --> 02:14:34.330
I tweeted about this a couple of
days ago. It blows my mind how

02:14:34.520 --> 02:14:38.360
often an agent can do something, say
it's done, have the work reviewed by

02:14:38.380 --> 02:14:39.390
another agent-

02:14:39.390 --> 02:14:43.380
... they say it's done, and then I go like,
"Eh, looks a little too complicated for me."

02:14:43.820 --> 02:14:47.520
And then it'll go like, "Oh, yeah,
you're right. You're right. I totally

02:14:47.580 --> 02:14:51.470
overcomplicated this," and it'll cut
it in half. I'm like, "Couldn't it

02:14:51.500 --> 02:14:55.260
just do this, like, from the outset?"
And then someone else reminded me,

02:14:55.320 --> 02:14:59.190
humans are the same way. If
you work on something and

02:14:59.300 --> 02:15:03.200
someone else reviews it and they just are like, "Eh,
it looks a little complicated," quite often you're

02:15:03.240 --> 02:15:04.900
able to go like, "Ah, you're right."

02:15:05.100 --> 02:15:08.440
- See, that, that just... Those
little indications to me

02:15:09.000 --> 02:15:11.280
show that the programmer is still needed,

02:15:12.180 --> 02:15:16.140
that intuition, 'cause I think, what, what
did you say? I have a sense of the landscape

02:15:16.180 --> 02:15:16.760
of the code.

02:15:17.120 --> 02:15:23.060
- When I build something like Omarchy, I, I
do look at the code. I look at the shape.

02:15:23.070 --> 02:15:24.790
And I look at the proportions.

02:15:24.820 --> 02:15:28.570
Like, I don't have to chisel everything myself
anymore. This is, again, when I'm flattering

02:15:28.579 --> 02:15:32.220
myself for... Not one
moment I'm a McLaren audio,

02:15:32.560 --> 02:15:36.410
uh auto engineer, and the next
moment I'm a da Vinci, working

02:15:36.440 --> 02:15:39.400
with his whole studio of students-

02:15:39.410 --> 02:15:42.730
... who are doing all the chiseling on the,
the fine marble, right? And he goes like,

02:15:42.869 --> 02:15:46.820
"Ah, no, the proportions are not quite
right." That's how it feels working with

02:15:46.880 --> 02:15:50.660
agents now. That I can give
feedback like an editor.

02:15:50.860 --> 02:15:54.740
That, do you know what? I'm not gonna write
everything myself, but I can tell you

02:15:54.800 --> 02:15:58.589
when the argument doesn't land. I
can tell you when the shape isn't

02:15:58.700 --> 02:16:02.290
proportional to the problem, or when it
just feels like it's too complicated. And

02:16:02.680 --> 02:16:08.130
shockingly often, it just works. It's
like the early days, where the jokes was,

02:16:08.809 --> 02:16:12.560
"Don't make mistakes." I don't think you have to
tell the agents that anymore, because now the

02:16:12.580 --> 02:16:16.470
harnesses are so good, they run automated
testing. They get that feedback. But

02:16:16.500 --> 02:16:18.740
you actually do have to tell
them, "Make it simpler."

02:16:19.080 --> 02:16:23.060
- Do you use voice at all for the interaction?
Or are you... How much are you typing?

02:16:23.160 --> 02:16:23.730
How much are you-

02:16:23.800 --> 02:16:27.930
- I type the whole thing. I actually,
in Omarchy's built-in Voxtype-

02:16:27.940 --> 02:16:32.020
... Audio transcription, and it's
actually really good. I just find that

02:16:33.420 --> 02:16:37.280
I don't think that way, and
it surprised myself that I

02:16:37.300 --> 02:16:38.940
don't like to talk to the computer.

02:16:39.960 --> 02:16:43.869
Uh, I like when I'm in the car, but when
I'm in front of the keyboard, I just

02:16:43.900 --> 02:16:46.360
wanna type. I also just love
typing. Are you using the voice?

02:16:46.420 --> 02:16:50.610
- I'm an introvert who doesn't like social
interaction- ... or speaking. And-

02:16:50.640 --> 02:16:53.160
- I think that's part of mine too.

02:16:53.680 --> 02:16:57.620
- But, like, for me, for some reason,
voice is really powerful. I want

02:16:57.660 --> 02:17:01.580
to give this... So I, I run with
this thing. It's called Plaud. So

02:17:01.740 --> 02:17:05.600
I, I just hook it up on my,
you know on my T-shirt here.

02:17:05.640 --> 02:17:09.460
You press a button, it starts
recording. And this is actually more,

02:17:09.580 --> 02:17:13.720
in terms of flow, much better experience
than behind a computer. And I,

02:17:14.580 --> 02:17:18.540
uh, save, well, usually
it's agent gen- generated

02:17:19.240 --> 02:17:27.180
problems that I wanna really talk through.
And so I write, I speak a long prompt,

02:17:28.000 --> 02:17:32.200
like, and a lot of it, it's like stream
of consciousness reasoning. So I'll

02:17:33.180 --> 02:17:36.780
sometimes just change my mind about a
design, but all of that is in there,

02:17:37.260 --> 02:17:40.580
and then I have an LLM that processes
that. With Plaud, there's a,

02:17:41.059 --> 02:17:45.020
obviously an API. You can grab it,
transcribe it, clean up the transcript,

02:17:45.360 --> 02:17:48.400
'cause you, you want it to be...
'Cause I'm referring to files.

02:17:49.480 --> 02:17:53.540
I'm referring to technical
concepts, like files and functions-

02:17:53.600 --> 02:17:56.350
... and so on, that you wanna
make sure the transcription, the-

02:17:56.350 --> 02:18:00.340
... the speech-to-text gets correctly. So you wanna
have a dictionary. You want it to be aware of the code

02:18:00.400 --> 02:18:04.160
base when you process the files.
And then you have this gigantic

02:18:04.240 --> 02:18:08.060
thing that's often 10, 15,
20 minute me discussing the

02:18:08.120 --> 02:18:11.520
full design, and I find that to be

02:18:12.420 --> 02:18:16.300
really, really powerful.
Because it doesn't have the

02:18:16.320 --> 02:18:20.200
problem overs- of
overspecification because of how

02:18:20.280 --> 02:18:24.009
much stream of consciousness
thinking the system has

02:18:24.100 --> 02:18:28.050
about what you're imagining.
And this is really good

02:18:28.080 --> 02:18:32.070
for the early steps of a design. Like,
if I wanted to build Omarchy from

02:18:32.070 --> 02:18:35.960
scratch, the first stages, I
talk through. It could be an

02:18:36.040 --> 02:18:40.180
hour. There's been prompts that I had
for an hour, and I'm just running and-

02:18:40.260 --> 02:18:40.450
- That's amazing

02:18:40.480 --> 02:18:47.460
- ... and speaking. I highly
recommend people try a voice-based-

02:18:48.380 --> 02:18:48.390
- So-

02:18:48.390 --> 02:18:50.380
- ... speaking for prolonged
periods of time.

02:18:51.180 --> 02:18:51.280
- I haven't actually-

02:18:51.320 --> 02:18:52.350
- As opposed to very specific

02:18:52.360 --> 02:18:55.020
- ... I haven't, yeah, I haven't used it that,
and maybe that's how I'm using it wrong.

02:18:55.300 --> 02:18:59.250
I try to just type or
speak two sentences, and

02:18:59.280 --> 02:19:03.080
then I get a little frustrated
that I could've typed that just as

02:19:03.140 --> 02:19:06.790
fast maybe. But I have not
tried this whole thing of just

02:19:06.840 --> 02:19:09.260
talking continuously for 20 minutes.

02:19:09.340 --> 02:19:12.520
- And especially you because
you speak very fast.

02:19:12.600 --> 02:19:16.520
- Yeah. No, I could see it. I think because
the reason I've been, I've been trying to

02:19:16.540 --> 02:19:18.030
do it for things like replying to emails,

02:19:18.840 --> 02:19:22.730
and it got to a point
where I thought it could

02:19:22.760 --> 02:19:26.660
help. And it sort of did, but then it would
make a small mistake, and I'd have to

02:19:26.700 --> 02:19:29.930
correct the mistake. But if you're doing
stream of conscious to an LLM who doesn't

02:19:29.980 --> 02:19:33.770
care about all this other stuff anyway-
... that probably works way better.

02:19:34.140 --> 02:19:36.900
- There's two things you have to do.
One, okay, you have the raw audio,

02:19:37.780 --> 02:19:41.300
transcribes it. I use ElevenLabs for
transcription. They're probably the best

02:19:41.380 --> 02:19:42.210
speech-to-text model.

02:19:43.240 --> 02:19:47.220
There's still mistakes there, so you
need to have a prompt that has a-

02:19:48.100 --> 02:19:50.610
access to a dictionary and to a lot of-

02:19:50.620 --> 02:19:52.000
- How do you build the dictionary?

02:19:52.040 --> 02:19:55.760
- First of all, there's a dictionary of terms
that are relevant to you. Then have it

02:19:55.819 --> 02:19:59.760
aware of... So you have an agent that looks
through, when you're talking about code,

02:20:00.380 --> 02:20:04.140
looks through your entire code base for
key terms that you use often. Also, when

02:20:04.160 --> 02:20:07.780
we're talking about messaging, Gmail, and
WhatsApp, and so on, it's aware of all the

02:20:07.840 --> 02:20:11.260
people and common terms
that you get wrong.

02:20:11.460 --> 02:20:12.640
- You've really gone deep in this.

02:20:13.280 --> 02:20:15.000
- That, to me, is a problem
that needs to be solved-

02:20:15.010 --> 02:20:18.680
... because, uh, one of the issues
you probably have speech to text

02:20:19.920 --> 02:20:22.240
is that it makes mistakes sometimes.

02:20:22.340 --> 02:20:24.980
- Correct. Then I can't use
it for dictating an email.

02:20:25.280 --> 02:20:28.059
- Right. If it never makes mistakes, you-

02:20:28.080 --> 02:20:28.960
- Then it would be more appealing.

02:20:29.040 --> 02:20:32.380
- Yeah, and you are okay being
not perfectly real time,

02:20:32.480 --> 02:20:35.810
actually. You're okay
waiting five seconds-

02:20:35.840 --> 02:20:39.640
... potentially. I don't know what your
use case is. I'm okay waiting five

02:20:39.660 --> 02:20:42.620
seconds- ... 10 seconds for
it to process everything.

02:20:43.060 --> 02:20:45.910
- You gotta vibe code this into an app
that does all of this such that-

02:20:46.600 --> 02:20:47.900
... it just works.

02:20:47.980 --> 02:20:54.670
- I think I mentioned to you offline
that it's very surprising to me that,

02:20:54.800 --> 02:20:59.600
translation is not done perfectly.
Speech-to-speech translation.

02:20:59.620 --> 02:21:03.600
- It seems like you've gotten much closer to
the best of what's possible by putting all

02:21:03.640 --> 02:21:04.280
these things together.

02:21:05.560 --> 02:21:09.360
- But, you know, when you're shipping that
kind of stuff, you do have to start

02:21:09.380 --> 02:21:10.400
thinking about latency.

02:21:11.000 --> 02:21:14.040
- Don't let the perfect be
the enemy of good. I mean,

02:21:15.160 --> 02:21:19.080
if you're suddenly enabling something people
wouldn't be doing before at all, it's okay to

02:21:19.120 --> 02:21:23.070
take five seconds longer. I think this
is one of the areas that Steve Jobs was

02:21:23.120 --> 02:21:27.000
so good, that he realized, like,
the first iPhone was terrible

02:21:27.020 --> 02:21:32.620
in all sorts of ways, right? Absolutely
horrendously slow data connection, very

02:21:32.680 --> 02:21:35.860
underpowered, all the things.
But the product was so

02:21:35.880 --> 02:21:39.850
compelling that it just didn't
matter. You'd be shipping

02:21:39.900 --> 02:21:43.660
too late if you waited until everything
was just right, just perfect.

02:21:43.700 --> 02:21:47.649
If you're solving a problem that people
currently don't have a solution for, they're

02:21:47.660 --> 02:21:48.800
willing to give it five seconds.

02:21:49.520 --> 02:21:53.500
If you're in a competitive market, yeah, okay, it's different.
But that means the problem's already solved, and you

02:21:53.540 --> 02:21:54.180
don't need to solve it.

02:21:54.480 --> 02:21:58.960
- Mm-hmm. Well, I do think there's a few...
So, like, Whisper Flow is really good.

02:22:00.060 --> 02:22:02.550
A few people have stepped up
that speech-to-text thing.

02:22:02.860 --> 02:22:05.900
- Yeah. Yeah, we're using
Voxtype. But others have-

02:22:05.910 --> 02:22:06.900
- Uh, Vo-Voxtype is open source?

02:22:07.180 --> 02:22:11.320
- It is. It's just using one of the open
models. I forget which parrot model

02:22:11.340 --> 02:22:15.300
it's using or something like that. It
works quite well for short commands, so I

02:22:15.340 --> 02:22:19.520
have used it for that. Uh, I
think you just hold down F9-

02:22:19.600 --> 02:22:21.310
... and it starts a dictation.

02:22:21.620 --> 02:22:22.590
- Oh, so it's already in there?

02:22:23.000 --> 02:22:25.800
- You just have to get online.
It'll offer you to install-

02:22:25.900 --> 02:22:25.970
- Got it

02:22:25.970 --> 02:22:26.500
- ... the Voxtype package.

02:22:26.510 --> 02:22:26.810
- Got it. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

02:22:26.840 --> 02:22:30.760
- Because the Voxtype package includes
a model that's 150 megabytes, and I

02:22:30.800 --> 02:22:31.680
was like, "Ah."

02:22:32.340 --> 02:22:32.410
- Yep, yep.

02:22:32.440 --> 02:22:34.560
- "I don't know if I can carry that." So-

02:22:35.180 --> 02:22:35.300
- Yep

02:22:35.460 --> 02:22:39.360
- ... we don't have that in there by
default, but we have the option set up.

02:22:39.720 --> 02:22:43.340
And I actually just yesterday was thinking

02:22:43.580 --> 02:22:47.140
we really need to combine all
of these things. So Omarchy

02:22:47.220 --> 02:22:51.080
Quattro is truly amazing
as a malleable operating

02:22:51.160 --> 02:22:55.940
system where you can add functionality
to it just by talking to your agent.

02:22:56.000 --> 02:22:59.900
But that's exactly what we should be
doing. There's a lot of people who would

02:22:59.960 --> 02:23:03.720
find it very natural just to talk
to their computer and say, "Hey,

02:23:03.840 --> 02:23:08.940
can you make me a stock panel?
I wanna track Apple and Dell,"

02:23:10.100 --> 02:23:11.780
and just see the agent go off

02:23:12.680 --> 02:23:16.560
through, entirely through voice, both in
terms of the input and in terms of the

02:23:16.620 --> 02:23:20.480
output, and then your operating system
just changes. This was the vision

02:23:20.560 --> 02:23:22.480
of Iron Man and Jarvis and-

02:23:22.580 --> 02:23:22.700
- Yeah

02:23:23.020 --> 02:23:26.800
- ... bespoke applications that just appear

02:23:26.880 --> 02:23:30.820
magically. I'm thinking the
area of AI I find truly

02:23:30.860 --> 02:23:34.660
intriguing are these live gaming models, I
don't know if you've seen that, where the

02:23:34.720 --> 02:23:38.440
AI's inventing literally the
next frame, but you can actually

02:23:38.480 --> 02:23:42.320
play the game. And I think if they're
able to do that, why can't we do

02:23:42.380 --> 02:23:46.220
that with our operating system? Why
can't we just talk to it and ask it

02:23:46.280 --> 02:23:50.260
to be a different way or change, and
it'll all just change? I think this, by

02:23:50.280 --> 02:23:54.160
the way, is one of the other
breakthroughs that is going to lead to

02:23:54.180 --> 02:24:00.700
the total domination of Linux. The agents
have taken all the hardship out of

02:24:00.720 --> 02:24:06.240
diagnosing Linux systems and turned
the fact that Linux produces

02:24:06.280 --> 02:24:12.450
these overly specific, totally arcane error
messages into its greatest advantage.

02:24:12.700 --> 02:24:14.460
When Linux has an issue,

02:24:15.260 --> 02:24:19.160
the agent can take that very specific error
message that makes no sense to a normal

02:24:19.220 --> 02:24:22.940
human and correlate it with the fact
that the agent was pre-trained on

02:24:23.120 --> 02:24:27.780
40 million lines of Linux code, so it knows
exactly where to look and dial it down.

02:24:27.820 --> 02:24:28.060
- Yeah.

02:24:28.080 --> 02:24:32.060
- I have not had a single problem
on my Linux machine since

02:24:32.080 --> 02:24:35.620
the beginning of this year that an
agent could not diagnose. That was

02:24:35.820 --> 02:24:39.720
not true a year and a half ago.
A year and a half ago, I was

02:24:39.760 --> 02:24:45.490
still searching on forums- ... to
find answers to esoteric questions.

02:24:45.700 --> 02:24:49.680
Now the agents know the source code
of not just the Linux operating

02:24:49.750 --> 02:24:53.720
system, but every single piece of
software I have on that box. The agent

02:24:53.760 --> 02:24:57.690
has access to the source code of all of it.
In fact, this was one thing I built in just

02:24:57.720 --> 02:25:01.560
before we, uh, we shipped. So once
you set up your default agent-

02:25:01.960 --> 02:25:05.720
... Omarchy Quattro has
a crash watcher. If

02:25:05.840 --> 02:25:09.460
any app on your machine
crashes, it'll pop up a little

02:25:09.520 --> 02:25:12.980
thing, ask you whether you want
your AI to diagnose the problem.

02:25:13.160 --> 02:25:14.280
- Yeah. That's amazing.

02:25:14.360 --> 02:25:18.300
- Unbelievable. I've seen
things where there's an

02:25:18.380 --> 02:25:22.340
error in some subsystem. Some, some...
Not even Linux, just some app I

02:25:22.360 --> 02:25:26.150
installed. The agents start
digging through the logs and

02:25:26.340 --> 02:25:29.580
looks up systemd log. Then it
checks out the damn source code.

02:25:30.500 --> 02:25:34.120
Of the application that crashed,
pins down that it's in this

02:25:34.340 --> 02:25:38.200
Rust file line 472 that there's

02:25:38.240 --> 02:25:42.320
an unbounded, unwrapped variable
that overflowed or whatever it is.

02:25:43.440 --> 02:25:48.080
Then offers you whether you wanna file
a bug report with all this detail.

02:25:49.020 --> 02:25:52.160
I'll give you one amazing
anecdote. So I was working with

02:25:52.960 --> 02:25:56.480
this guy JDX, who's working on
mise. This, this package manager

02:25:56.620 --> 02:26:00.520
for fast-moving development tools. This is
how we manage all the agent software and so

02:26:00.620 --> 02:26:04.320
forth. Normal package managers are not
built to be updated seven times a day.

02:26:04.400 --> 02:26:08.120
And all of these agent harnesses are
updated about seven times a day, so we

02:26:08.160 --> 02:26:12.120
needed an out-of-band package manager,
and mise just turns out to be

02:26:12.160 --> 02:26:13.080
perfect for this. Anyway,

02:26:13.920 --> 02:26:17.800
it had a small bug. There was some
race condition which agents are

02:26:17.860 --> 02:26:21.780
very good at finding because when you start running
multiple agents at the same time, they will suss out all

02:26:21.800 --> 02:26:25.740
these race conditions you have in your underlying
infrastructure that was never triggered by a human

02:26:26.100 --> 02:26:30.460
trying to do manual thing. So it
finds this issue, right? I tell

02:26:31.260 --> 02:26:38.060
I tell the agent, "Hey, can you post
this as a bug report to JDX's GitHub?"

02:26:38.860 --> 02:26:42.720
And unfortunately, right
before that, I had had to do

02:26:42.860 --> 02:26:48.940
a QA run on Omarchy itself with eight
different agents. It had found 28 real issues-

02:26:48.950 --> 02:26:52.680
... that it needed to file. Well, it
went to GitHub and tried to file all

02:26:52.900 --> 02:26:56.540
28 issues at the same time,
which it did in about,

02:26:56.720 --> 02:27:00.240
uh, I don't know, 12 seconds. GitHub, not

02:27:00.380 --> 02:27:05.070
unreasonably, marked that as probable
spam and banned- ... my Omarchy bot.

02:27:05.390 --> 02:27:06.960
And then I couldn't do that. So

02:27:07.920 --> 02:27:11.770
I was blocked from the bot having access to
GitHub, so I just told it, "Hey, do you know

02:27:11.820 --> 02:27:15.480
what? Just email JDX. Here's
his email address." I had

02:27:15.560 --> 02:27:17.360
already set it up with hey.com-

02:27:17.380 --> 02:27:20.760
... an email address. We have a
CLI that's in beta right now.

02:27:20.820 --> 02:27:24.280
So I set it up with that so it can send to
email. That's how it gives me reports about

02:27:24.340 --> 02:27:27.860
outstanding issues and PRs. So it sends,

02:27:28.880 --> 02:27:32.270
um, JDX this email about the
bug it found, and he was like,

02:27:32.840 --> 02:27:36.790
"Wait, this is the first time I've
gotten a bug report in unreleased

02:27:36.840 --> 02:27:40.360
software." Because it had
downloaded the source code to mise,

02:27:40.860 --> 02:27:44.560
saw that he had already fixed the bug,
but that it would not fix the problem

02:27:44.600 --> 02:27:47.440
entirely in software they
had not shipped yet.

02:27:47.880 --> 02:27:50.760
Pinpointed the problem,
and he was like, "Damn it-

02:27:50.770 --> 02:27:52.780
... I got a bug report before
we even cut a release."

02:27:53.620 --> 02:27:54.200
- That's amazing. That's amazing

02:27:54.220 --> 02:27:58.560
- ... believable AGI levels
of mind-blowing stuff.

02:27:58.980 --> 02:28:02.729
- And that was actually, if you
remember, that was an open question

02:28:02.840 --> 02:28:04.830
whether AI systems could debug well.

02:28:06.040 --> 02:28:09.940
That was kinda the thought that, yeah,
sure, they can write code, but can

02:28:09.980 --> 02:28:10.710
they find issues and-

02:28:10.740 --> 02:28:14.480
- They are so incredibly good at this,
and this is actually interesting

02:28:14.520 --> 02:28:18.380
because I was on the perhaps same side
of that, a little skeptical about

02:28:18.500 --> 02:28:22.360
can it reason about all these
things. And Mikhail, who's the CTO

02:28:22.600 --> 02:28:25.100
at Shopify, ran a
scientific study on this.

02:28:26.020 --> 02:28:29.840
They had... And this was, I think, late
last year or early this year, where he

02:28:29.900 --> 02:28:33.840
had agents go back through all
the incidents, both outages and,

02:28:33.860 --> 02:28:37.800
and other problems that Shopify had had
in production, trace that back to the

02:28:37.880 --> 02:28:41.600
PR that was merged, and find
out whether PRs that had been

02:28:41.700 --> 02:28:45.030
reviewed by human or PRs
that had been reviewed by

02:28:45.100 --> 02:28:50.540
agents were of higher quality.
Well, lo and surprise-

02:28:50.840 --> 02:28:54.760
... the PRs that have been reviewed
by agents caused far fewer

02:28:54.820 --> 02:28:58.480
issues in production. And this
was with models we had six

02:28:58.640 --> 02:29:02.600
months ago. At this point, 100% in the

02:29:02.640 --> 02:29:06.140
majority of domains we work in today,
agents are better at finding bugs.

02:29:06.920 --> 02:29:10.600
- So you now run agents all day.

02:29:11.020 --> 02:29:14.420
What stands out to you as the better
model? Who's currently winning?

02:29:14.500 --> 02:29:21.880
It seems to be changing constantly. Sol,
Fable, Grok 4.6, Gemini, the Chinese OpenWay

02:29:21.960 --> 02:29:22.440
models.

02:29:23.360 --> 02:29:29.680
- What's amazing to me is that you could
rattle off so many different contenders.

02:29:29.880 --> 02:29:32.860
That this market is so wide open, that it

02:29:33.000 --> 02:29:36.660
does actually change back
and forth, that we have real

02:29:36.720 --> 02:29:40.680
competition, that there are
so many labs that are able

02:29:40.780 --> 02:29:44.360
to get either to the
frontier or close to it.

02:29:44.920 --> 02:29:47.759
That, by the way, is remarkable. I
still don't fully understand that.

02:29:48.199 --> 02:29:52.840
But to answer your question, the best
model in general right now is Fable.

02:29:54.340 --> 02:29:58.070
The second-best model, in
my opinion, is Opus 5. But

02:29:58.980 --> 02:30:01.600
the tier just below Opus 5, and

02:30:02.700 --> 02:30:05.150
it's not even that they're always
below, sometimes they're ahead.

02:30:05.460 --> 02:30:11.940
I'll get to that in a second. I
would rank GPT Sol very good.

02:30:12.960 --> 02:30:16.820
Grok 4.6 I just started
testing a few days ago. I had

02:30:16.860 --> 02:30:20.480
this wonderful test that
I've set up by accident

02:30:20.840 --> 02:30:24.630
where I translated this
Python library into Rust. Uh,

02:30:24.630 --> 02:30:28.100
the screensaver you just saw
with all the cool animation?

02:30:28.140 --> 02:30:31.250
That's powered by a Python library
called Terminal Text Effects.

02:30:32.020 --> 02:30:35.640
Really cool library. We've been using
it since the first day of Omarchy.

02:30:36.540 --> 02:30:40.100
The problem with that is it's written in
Python, so when it starts up, especially on a

02:30:40.120 --> 02:30:44.080
laptop, and it runs in Python,
it uses all of your CPU

02:30:44.200 --> 02:30:48.120
to do these effects, and therefore,
it means it uses about 30 watts

02:30:48.160 --> 02:30:51.780
of energy, and it spins up your
fans, and it drains your battery.

02:30:52.100 --> 02:30:55.960
Doesn't really matter on a local computer, but it
does matter on a laptop. So I thought, "Do you know

02:30:56.000 --> 02:31:01.740
what? This sounds like a problem for Rust."
So first I gave Fable the challenge,

02:31:03.040 --> 02:31:06.860
and all I told it was, "Here's
the source code for the

02:31:06.880 --> 02:31:10.840
Python library," this TTE library that
had a bunch of dependencies and so forth.

02:31:11.630 --> 02:31:15.600
"I want a Rust version of
this with no dependencies, a

02:31:15.660 --> 02:31:19.310
single executable." Like, that's what Rust
does. So basically, I want it in Rust.

02:31:19.760 --> 02:31:23.600
I want it to be pixel perfect,
frame by frame, do a full

02:31:23.660 --> 02:31:26.380
analysis, don't stop
until you're finished.

02:31:26.420 --> 02:31:26.920
- Yep.

02:31:26.960 --> 02:31:27.660
- That was the prompt.

02:31:27.720 --> 02:31:28.940
- Yep.

02:31:29.160 --> 02:31:33.150
- I kid you not, in just under
45 minutes, it was like

02:31:34.460 --> 02:31:38.090
Am I heard all? I'm finished.
I've checked everything.

02:31:38.960 --> 02:31:46.540
I have reduced the startup time
from 86 milliseconds to two

02:31:46.660 --> 02:31:51.340
milliseconds. I have sped
up the execution time by

02:31:51.920 --> 02:31:57.580
9.6 times, I believe it was. The executable
is three megabytes. Do you wanna run it?

02:31:59.540 --> 02:32:00.240
- That's awesome.

02:32:00.440 --> 02:32:01.090
- So I run it—

02:32:01.440 --> 02:32:01.900
- Oh, man.

02:32:02.200 --> 02:32:05.920
- ... and I don't know why
I'm surprised, because this

02:32:05.930 --> 02:32:09.920
translation job is something we've
known for a while that AI is

02:32:09.980 --> 02:32:13.660
pretty good at, but it was still
staggering to me that I could one-shot

02:32:14.480 --> 02:32:18.430
a full translation of a Python library I

02:32:18.460 --> 02:32:21.950
had been using for a year, that others
had been using for much longer,

02:32:22.420 --> 02:32:26.100
and turn it into a Rust executable
without knowing any Rust,

02:32:26.560 --> 02:32:30.320
without looking at the Rust
code at all, and produce this

02:32:30.380 --> 02:32:34.840
executable that I then told the agent
right after, "This is great. Ship it."

02:32:35.800 --> 02:32:39.410
It packaged it up as a new package. It told me,
"What do you want it to call?" "Uh, let's call it

02:32:39.480 --> 02:32:43.400
TTFX. Let's create a new Git repo." It
sets up the Git repo. "Let's create

02:32:43.440 --> 02:32:47.420
a new package, build package for
our build system." It puts that

02:32:47.460 --> 02:32:51.400
up. "Let's push it out.
Let's open the pull request

02:32:51.520 --> 02:32:55.480
to the Omarchy itself so we switch around
from TTE, the Python implementation, to

02:32:55.600 --> 02:32:57.710
TTFX." It does all of it,

02:32:58.540 --> 02:33:02.360
and I'm just sitting there. And again,
I'm already at this point fully

02:33:02.380 --> 02:33:06.220
delirious with agent acceleration, and
I still had to lean back and I go like,

02:33:07.180 --> 02:33:10.320
"This is AGI, isn't it? This
is what AGI looks like.

02:33:11.140 --> 02:33:15.080
If we have... If these moments are just
what it is all the time, this is AGI."

02:33:15.480 --> 02:33:20.820
- Now, as I understand, you also repeated
the same experiment not just with Fable.

02:33:21.160 --> 02:33:25.050
- I did it on all the models. First,
I did it... Actually, funny thing.

