02:47:27.093 --> 02:47:30.614
it was all mobile, and before that it was
the gold rush. So I think that's probably-

02:47:30.614 --> 02:47:33.593
... just San Francisco. It attracts-
... the kind of individuals-

02:47:33.604 --> 02:47:34.224
- The dreamers

02:47:34.604 --> 02:47:38.464
- ... who would, who would think, "I don't have
time to sleep." I've not been on that track,

02:47:39.384 --> 02:47:41.324
generally speaking, but I will admit that

02:47:42.324 --> 02:47:46.444
the last three months have
felt more exhausting than

02:47:47.704 --> 02:47:51.544
any other project I've done in the last five
years, since the Hey launch. That was the

02:47:51.664 --> 02:47:55.484
last time we had a crazy
exhausting launch.

02:47:55.664 --> 02:47:57.234
- Do you think you can
keep going at this pace?

02:47:57.324 --> 02:47:59.584
- No, no, no, no, no. This
is not sustainable at all.

02:47:59.624 --> 02:48:00.444
- Okay.

02:48:00.464 --> 02:48:02.644
- But I also, I can see
the end of the tunnel.

02:48:02.744 --> 02:48:06.724
Because the automation, which by
the way, everyone is building. This

02:48:06.744 --> 02:48:10.464
is so hilarious. Like, everyone is building
their little gas town, their little

02:48:10.604 --> 02:48:14.504
agent coordination, their setup. This is all
gonna be solved. We're not all gonna have to

02:48:14.544 --> 02:48:18.444
build our own coordination harnesses.
Of course we're not. And in fact, I'm

02:48:18.524 --> 02:48:22.304
a little surprised that it's gone this
long, that there's not more of it has been

02:48:22.404 --> 02:48:25.844
sucked up by the major labs. You'd think
that they'd just built this stuff in.

02:48:26.844 --> 02:48:30.504
But some of it is also this is what a new domain
looks like. I remember for a hot moment when

02:48:30.564 --> 02:48:34.444
JavaScript kind of came to
realize its own power, and there

02:48:34.484 --> 02:48:38.204
was basically a new JavaScript framework every
five minutes, right? Like, a lot of churn.

02:48:38.264 --> 02:48:40.524
This is what happens at the
advent of a new paradigm.

02:48:41.524 --> 02:48:45.504
So I think it's natural, and I think it's gonna
settle down, and I can already see the light at the

02:48:45.524 --> 02:48:49.444
end of the tunnel. So I don't mind a
sprint. In fact, I welcome it now. I

02:48:49.664 --> 02:48:53.414
like this notion that most
time is calm, but then

02:48:53.444 --> 02:48:56.284
occasionally you gotta climb a mountain.

02:48:56.364 --> 02:49:01.844
- If we look back to the DHH a year ago,
what advice would you give to that guy?

02:49:03.364 --> 02:49:04.164
- I wouldn't wanna spoil it.

02:49:04.584 --> 02:49:04.704
- Okay.

02:49:04.784 --> 02:49:08.944
- What a- ... thriller that this has been.

02:49:09.004 --> 02:49:14.024
I mean, if you would've scripted this,
I'd be like, "This is so far-fetched. Get

02:49:14.104 --> 02:49:17.784
out of here. Unbelievable," right?
I wouldn't wanna know anything. The

02:49:17.904 --> 02:49:21.324
fact that this rollercoaster
and this acceleration has

02:49:21.364 --> 02:49:24.724
been absorbed in real
time by everyone, no one

02:49:24.784 --> 02:49:28.674
knew, right? Like, some had
premonitions that were a little

02:49:28.684 --> 02:49:31.034
better than others, but no one
knew, not exactly the way-

02:49:31.034 --> 02:49:33.124
... it was gonna go and how
fast it was gonna take off.

02:49:34.904 --> 02:49:40.204
I mean, it's the show of a
lifetime. I mean, the mean cinema-

02:49:40.524 --> 02:49:41.744
... absolutely applies-

02:49:41.784 --> 02:49:42.083
- Yeah, it's incredible.

02:49:42.094 --> 02:49:43.084
- ... to this moment.

02:49:43.404 --> 02:49:48.244
- Uh, that said, what do you think DHH
from a year from now will be like?

02:49:49.524 --> 02:49:51.234
I mean, what are the possible versions?

02:49:52.876 --> 02:49:56.496
- That all our dreams are coming
true. Like, this is the AI

02:49:56.636 --> 02:50:03.256
maximalist abundance argument.
That once Elon's robots

02:50:04.156 --> 02:50:08.106
have the level of intelligence and
AGI that I'm experiencing on some of

02:50:08.116 --> 02:50:11.456
this development stuff,
the world is gonna be so

02:50:12.316 --> 02:50:15.956
unrecognizably different that
we can't even imagine it.

02:50:17.196 --> 02:50:21.156
Now, I don't spend any time thinking about
that because I think that is the way to

02:50:21.196 --> 02:50:27.016
the AI psychosis. So therefore, I just
immerse myself in the moment and get

02:50:27.356 --> 02:50:28.916
absolutely the maximum out of it and-

02:50:29.236 --> 02:50:29.756
- Just have fun.

02:50:30.356 --> 02:50:30.856
- Just have fun.

02:50:32.256 --> 02:50:35.386
I mean, I get it why people are finding
this exhausting. Just keeping up.

02:50:35.516 --> 02:50:39.346
Not even using agents, just, like, being like,
"What's the latest model? What did, what

02:50:39.436 --> 02:50:42.776
tool are we using now? Is it TMox? Is
it a Herder? Is it none of it? Is it

02:50:42.996 --> 02:50:49.486
agents?" Whatever. I get it, but I also
again think we should be so blessed.

02:50:49.656 --> 02:50:53.836
You are alive in this moment where
decades are happening- ... in weeks.

02:50:54.456 --> 02:50:56.256
- Um, quick bathroom break.

02:50:57.596 --> 02:51:01.196
I've been generating a lot of
video recently with Higgsfield,

02:51:01.676 --> 02:51:05.376
and so they, they became a
sponsor. Created a racing video.

02:51:05.736 --> 02:51:06.726
Wanted to get your opinion on it.

02:51:06.726 --> 02:51:08.416
- Oh, yeah? Oh, let me see. Let me see.

02:51:08.636 --> 02:51:09.916
- To see, um-

02:51:10.756 --> 02:51:14.536
- If they downshift on a straight like they do
in the movies, I'll call it out. That is the

02:51:14.616 --> 02:51:16.716
worst. Let me see.

02:51:16.816 --> 02:51:17.846
- Yeah, see if-

02:51:18.196 --> 02:51:19.016
- Oh, that's a real clip

02:51:19.316 --> 02:51:21.916
- ... this is fully AI
generated. So first it goes-

02:51:21.996 --> 02:51:23.136
- Wait, what? This is AI?

02:51:23.496 --> 02:51:23.716
- Yep.

02:51:25.496 --> 02:51:27.976
- Shit, that's my suit. That's my car.

02:51:28.756 --> 02:51:32.916
- Lights go green on a
street that never sleeps.

02:51:35.736 --> 02:51:36.656
- Oh, my God.

02:51:36.666 --> 02:51:36.976
- Do an audio-

02:51:37.036 --> 02:51:37.976
- Are you kidding me?

02:51:39.416 --> 02:51:44.356
- But they never came. Is it
good? I got a bad case.

02:51:44.416 --> 02:51:49.396
- I mean, it's crazy. You really
have to notice in the...

02:51:51.156 --> 02:51:53.096
Like, that car didn't have
headlights on and the other one did.

02:51:54.336 --> 02:51:59.996
And that looked like a 60-year-old
version of me. But holy crap, the...

02:52:01.176 --> 02:52:02.875
Wow, that was crazy.

02:52:03.296 --> 02:52:07.216
- So there's parallels here to programming
because if you do just straight

02:52:07.316 --> 02:52:11.966
video generation with no human in the loop-
... you get a lot of weird artifacts-

02:52:12.116 --> 02:52:15.956
... and so on, and you can't really do...
So they do and I highly recommend people

02:52:15.996 --> 02:52:21.996
go to the Higgsfield YouTube. They
have full 90-minute original movies.

02:52:22.576 --> 02:52:26.546
And, like, I can't look away.
It's really cool. 'Cause it

02:52:26.596 --> 02:52:30.536
used to be that it's just, like,
something that just feels like trailers.

02:52:30.736 --> 02:52:32.336
Like advertisements for something.

02:52:32.376 --> 02:52:32.766
- Yes, yes.

02:52:32.796 --> 02:52:36.565
- This is actually telling stories, like, people's
faces and they're talking and you're like,

02:52:37.276 --> 02:52:40.696
you're drawn in. It's not
quite where programming is.

02:52:41.556 --> 02:52:45.516
And so the question is how do you
integrate the human into the loop of the

02:52:45.556 --> 02:52:48.986
filmmaking process? So you have to
create the people, and they're-

02:52:48.986 --> 02:52:50.696
... they have to be kept consistent.

02:52:51.416 --> 02:52:55.216
And then for the images that
are kinda like feeding the

02:52:55.276 --> 02:52:59.196
thing in the creation process,
you have to correct through

02:52:59.256 --> 02:53:04.016
prompting things that don't feel right.

02:53:04.576 --> 02:53:11.356
Because when you go from image to video,
the stuff that doesn't feel right will be-

02:53:11.746 --> 02:53:12.345
- It's gonna be zoomed up, right?

02:53:12.376 --> 02:53:15.376
- That's the stuff that's- ...
going to really create, uh-

02:53:15.505 --> 02:53:18.956
... the non-realistic stuff. The questions
I had for you is, like, do you think

02:53:19.456 --> 02:53:23.236
outside of programming,
can you apply lessons from

02:53:23.276 --> 02:53:26.456
programming to video creation, to art?

02:53:27.376 --> 02:53:31.156
- It's a good question because I think
good art is very dependent on the

02:53:31.196 --> 02:53:35.076
same thing that good software is
depending on: having a vision, having a

02:53:35.136 --> 02:53:38.696
cohesive idea of what you wanna create.

02:53:38.756 --> 02:53:42.736
How is it different? How is it novel?
How is it going to appeal to people?

02:53:43.396 --> 02:53:50.196
But I'm not sure that the creative
instincts transfer quite as well. Um,

02:53:51.256 --> 02:53:55.065
I don't know that I
would have the creative

02:53:55.316 --> 02:53:58.976
insights to come up with good video
prompts that would create compelling

02:53:59.036 --> 02:54:03.016
content. But it's funny because I have actually, on
TikTok, there's this guy, I'm trying to remember,

02:54:03.556 --> 02:54:05.316
Goblin something.

02:54:05.336 --> 02:54:09.016
That creates these... They are shorts, but
not super short, maybe a couple minutes,

02:54:10.396 --> 02:54:14.876
of really interesting sci-fi vignettes.

02:54:15.776 --> 02:54:19.806
And I think the guy is actually working
on a feature-length video right now.

02:54:20.376 --> 02:54:24.296
Um, and I think that this is one
of those abundance moments. There

02:54:24.396 --> 02:54:28.016
are creative people with that
vision all over the place,

02:54:28.056 --> 02:54:31.756
especially in a genre like
film, where before it

02:54:31.796 --> 02:54:35.756
required a $200 million budget
to create, I don't know, a

02:54:35.816 --> 02:54:39.656
sci-fi movie. Now suddenly, with AI, if

02:54:39.716 --> 02:54:43.486
you have the right vision for
it, you can do that in your

02:54:43.496 --> 02:54:47.476
bedroom. Now, that story's been told quite
a few times in other domains. It used

02:54:47.516 --> 02:54:51.476
to be true that if you wanted to record
a studio album, well, you needed a

02:54:51.556 --> 02:54:55.196
studio, and you needed to book that.
And now you can record at home on your

02:54:55.236 --> 02:54:58.516
laptop, and you can do a lot of these things,
right? So we've had this democratization

02:54:58.956 --> 02:54:59.896
process happen

02:55:00.836 --> 02:55:04.376
with plenty of other domains, but film
seems to be one of the last ones.

02:55:04.896 --> 02:55:09.876
- And it's the one that makes, like, me and
a lot of people feel weird, right? Like-

02:55:11.176 --> 02:55:12.746
- Oh, I'm super excited 'cause I think it's-

02:55:13.036 --> 02:55:13.346
- I mean, you-

02:55:13.356 --> 02:55:17.036
- There's so much garbage being produced right
now. It's the same argument with the slob,

02:55:17.256 --> 02:55:20.716
right? So people for a second
were worried about slob in

02:55:20.816 --> 02:55:24.436
programming, and I was telling them,
like, "Have you seen the human

02:55:24.536 --> 02:55:27.676
slob? It's also pretty bad."
And I think if it comes to

02:55:28.236 --> 02:55:32.156
studio entertainment, I mean,
the bar is pretty low, right?

02:55:32.316 --> 02:55:36.256
Like, the number of great movies
I've been dying to see over

02:55:36.296 --> 02:55:40.376
the past, say, five years, not
been a particularly high number.

02:55:42.396 --> 02:55:48.896
- Yeah, the video models are just, they're
able to create incredibly realistic

02:55:49.936 --> 02:55:54.076
facial expressions. I mean, it just
might change the nature of film

02:55:55.372 --> 02:55:59.292
I think they're— I guess my
hope would be that video

02:55:59.412 --> 02:56:05.432
games and film kind of merge, and
that film can become interactive

02:56:06.532 --> 02:56:10.292
- Like the malleable operating
system, having the malleable

02:56:10.332 --> 02:56:13.542
narrative, having malleable
entertainment, that

02:56:13.592 --> 02:56:16.812
man, Game of Thrones,
what if we could just

02:56:16.852 --> 02:56:23.362
forget the last four episodes of slob
that was produced- ... and then AI-

02:56:23.362 --> 02:56:25.582
... comes up with- ... I don't know-

02:56:25.732 --> 02:56:29.682
... maybe 100 different variations,
and someone else watches that,

02:56:29.712 --> 02:56:34.392
and I get the version that actually finishes
off that show in a way that's fitting.

02:56:34.772 --> 02:56:38.032
- Yeah. Something tells me an individual
creator would not create those la-

02:56:38.652 --> 02:56:40.922
would create a different
ending for Game of Thrones.

02:56:40.972 --> 02:56:44.942
- Oh, for sure. Right? And more just
to make the argument that talk about

02:56:44.992 --> 02:56:48.612
human slob. That was an
absolute atrocious ending to

02:56:48.932 --> 02:56:50.772
perhaps one of the greatest pieces of,

02:56:52.412 --> 02:56:56.372
Movie history, I was about to say,
but, um, series history, right?

02:56:56.852 --> 02:57:00.552
It was so good, and it was actually a
compelling argument for the fact that you

02:57:00.712 --> 02:57:01.772
need

02:57:03.692 --> 02:57:07.632
that strong vision. And as soon as those
showrunners had to go without a script,

02:57:08.052 --> 02:57:10.132
it went off the rails.

02:57:10.372 --> 02:57:13.972
- Do you think... You've mentioned AGI. Do
you think we've actually achieved AGI?

02:57:14.332 --> 02:57:19.632
- No, not in the general definition that
it's in all the things. But have I seen

02:57:20.532 --> 02:57:23.652
AGI in these glimmers? Absolutely.

02:57:24.332 --> 02:57:26.832
Especially over the last three
months, I've seen things where

02:57:29.372 --> 02:57:33.952
I was thinking to myself, like, "How
would AGI look different from this?"

02:57:34.532 --> 02:57:34.712
- Right.

02:57:34.852 --> 02:57:38.552
- What, what more could there be? And I
mean, I can quibble at the margins,

02:57:38.672 --> 02:57:45.752
but on the big picture, I've seen it
able to do these long run tasks and

02:57:45.832 --> 02:57:49.332
coordinate these multiple
aspects of it that we're so far

02:57:49.352 --> 02:57:53.072
beyond that early moment
where I'm telling it, "Go do

02:57:53.152 --> 02:57:57.072
this, package that." And
now it can carry out a

02:57:57.112 --> 02:58:01.092
whole task from these vague
instructions. That feels very

02:58:01.152 --> 02:58:03.192
AGI-like, and that's what's so

02:58:04.312 --> 02:58:08.792
inspiring about the moment
because you catch these glimmers.

02:58:09.132 --> 02:58:11.492
And then you go, "What if
everything was like that?"

02:58:12.092 --> 02:58:14.832
- Doesn't programming basically
unlock the everything?

02:58:15.932 --> 02:58:20.032
So you can write a pro- like it can generate
the program that does the everything.

02:58:20.432 --> 02:58:24.332
- So, so this is... I'm in that meta phase
right now. So I'm building this Amabot system

02:58:24.712 --> 02:58:31.821
that's managing the development of Omarchy,
and I'm using AI to build the bot.

02:58:32.072 --> 02:58:36.052
And I'm seeing that recurrent loop where
I started it on, like, "This is what

02:58:36.172 --> 02:58:39.692
I want. I want you to be able to have
these isolated VM workers and so forth,"

02:58:40.192 --> 02:58:41.682
and then it can do the self iteration.

02:58:41.892 --> 02:58:45.212
Then it starts running it. It starts noticing
its failures. It starts optimizing it.

02:58:45.992 --> 02:58:48.692
And a lot of that system,
Amabot in particular,

02:58:49.672 --> 02:58:53.552
AI has driven the majority of the
design decisions. Like, I had some

02:58:54.612 --> 02:58:58.312
vague ideas of where I wanted. I wanted to
use this brains and hands pattern where

02:58:58.332 --> 02:59:00.882
the model is running from
where the code is executing-

02:59:00.932 --> 02:59:04.912
... which is actually a really interesting
pattern. Again, Toby alerted me to

02:59:05.032 --> 02:59:08.912
that, where you have a
coordinator that runs the model-

02:59:09.312 --> 02:59:13.222
... but it's manipulating a safe VM where

02:59:13.292 --> 02:59:17.092
any untrusted code you're pulling down
from pull requests or issues or whatever

02:59:17.172 --> 02:59:22.942
can't contaminate the model. And
even in that loop, I keep seeing it

02:59:24.132 --> 02:59:27.852
catch itself, spotting
vulnerabilities, where it's like, "Oh,

02:59:28.112 --> 02:59:31.932
I actually just took some feedback
from a test run. If a clever

02:59:31.992 --> 02:59:33.652
attacker had embedded

02:59:34.772 --> 02:59:38.502
a malicious payload in the response
of the test run, that could have

02:59:38.552 --> 02:59:42.442
polluted something. I took it for good
thing. I better start treating that as

02:59:42.512 --> 02:59:48.032
as outside data." And you just
go like, it is just... I get why

02:59:48.912 --> 02:59:53.212
San Francisco is so
obsessed with fast takeoff-

02:59:53.272 --> 02:59:55.832
... because clearly they've
seen this 100 times-

02:59:55.872 --> 03:00:01.432
... more. They've seen it with all the
models that are in- ... training now, and

03:00:02.452 --> 03:00:06.122
we keep getting these little glimpses,
like the security breach with

03:00:06.252 --> 03:00:10.052
Hugging Face, where OpenAI was
training this model, and the

03:00:10.112 --> 03:00:13.892
model starts inventing ways,
essentially sending smoke signals to

03:00:13.952 --> 03:00:17.712
itself through embedding messages in a

03:00:17.792 --> 03:00:21.852
package manager, where you just go
like, first, that's damn clever.

03:00:22.072 --> 03:00:27.432
Second, okay, that's also
a little scary. And third

03:00:27.812 --> 03:00:30.662
how amazing would this be if we could
harness this level of intelligence-

03:00:30.662 --> 03:00:34.652
... and ingenuity towards
productive ends? So this is

03:00:34.732 --> 03:00:39.842
the it's so over, we're so back pendulum
that keeps swinging back and forth-

03:00:39.872 --> 03:00:41.512
... that AI

03:00:42.812 --> 03:00:46.772
has just delivered that swing back and
forth like nothing else. Like barely a week

03:00:46.792 --> 03:00:50.752
goes by where it has to swing one way, and then
the next week it swings back the other way.

03:00:51.412 --> 03:00:51.992
Exhilarating.

03:00:52.472 --> 03:00:56.452
- I'm still at the stage where it makes
me truly happy to see those moments

03:00:56.512 --> 03:01:00.292
of cleverness, and they're
becoming more and more frequent.

03:01:00.372 --> 03:01:04.172
Just kinda like seeing, oh, like
first of all, at the very basic

03:01:04.232 --> 03:01:05.452
level, when a model gets it.

03:01:06.492 --> 03:01:06.692
- Yes.

03:01:06.872 --> 03:01:10.392
- I'll say a basic prompt, you
know, a request, and it,

03:01:11.492 --> 03:01:14.652
it doesn't just do a
dumb implementation. It

03:01:15.072 --> 03:01:18.992
deeply understands, and it... That
was... That's a, that's always a

03:01:19.092 --> 03:01:22.552
beautiful thing. It's almost like having
a good, like, partner, like program.

03:01:22.572 --> 03:01:26.392
- That's exactly what it is. That's
exactly why it's so exhilarating and

03:01:26.432 --> 03:01:30.232
why, as we talked about before we
started, it can sometimes be difficult to

03:01:30.292 --> 03:01:34.272
come out of hyperdrive. That's also
one of Toby's terms here. That

03:01:34.432 --> 03:01:38.372
when you're working directly just one-on-one
with agents and you're not looping

03:01:38.412 --> 03:01:42.252
in other humans, you go so fast, and

03:01:42.312 --> 03:01:46.032
when the agents are really understanding
your intent, or even better,

03:01:46.552 --> 03:01:50.312
they revise and improve upon your

03:01:50.352 --> 03:01:54.892
intent to deliver something that
was greater than what you asked for

03:01:55.732 --> 03:01:59.552
And you're doing this with
16 parallel threads. It

03:01:59.632 --> 03:02:03.352
can be quite difficult to come out
of hyperdrive and have to deal with

03:02:03.412 --> 03:02:07.132
squishy humans. They just, they don't
think as fast. They don't move as fast.

03:02:08.332 --> 03:02:14.172
And I mean, I'm trying mostly to make
light of it, but you can also see a

03:02:14.252 --> 03:02:18.072
dark version of that where someone
does end up in an AI psychosis

03:02:18.132 --> 03:02:21.982
where they're not interested in other
humans at all anymore. I mean, certainly

03:02:22.052 --> 03:02:27.892
don't have that at all, but I do have
this sensation at times where I'm really

03:02:28.992 --> 03:02:32.852
grateful for just the
rush of just me and the

03:02:32.892 --> 03:02:36.442
agents. Like, can I just not
talk to anyone else but agents-

03:02:36.442 --> 03:02:38.712
... for four hours? Like,
that's an incredible run.

03:02:38.972 --> 03:02:41.812
- Toby is such a fascinating human because

03:02:42.812 --> 03:02:48.872
he's running a large company. So I
would love to get insights of how

03:02:48.972 --> 03:02:52.842
AI's being incorporated effectively in

03:02:52.872 --> 03:02:58.272
companies. Because, like, for
me, humans are pretty slow.

03:02:58.281 --> 03:03:02.092
And so, and so there's a temptation
to, like, use AI for basically,

03:03:03.492 --> 03:03:07.072
You know, AI running inside
Slack, for example, or inside,

03:03:07.312 --> 03:03:11.692
The Basecamp, whatever. And, like,

03:03:13.812 --> 03:03:17.772
collecting all the information about the
human interaction, basically handholding

03:03:17.812 --> 03:03:21.792
the humans to speed up as much as
possible. And like, and then at

03:03:21.932 --> 03:03:23.762
a certain point you're like, wait, what is

03:03:24.732 --> 03:03:28.242
this human... What are each
of us doing that's not

03:03:28.272 --> 03:03:32.042
replaceable by AI? And
that becomes, like, a

03:03:32.172 --> 03:03:36.632
scary kind of conversation because a
lot of work is kind of replaceable.

03:03:37.332 --> 03:03:41.312
- The irony here is it already
was. So the number of

03:03:41.352 --> 03:03:46.432
fake email jobs that currently exist in
this world is an absolute epidemic. And-

03:03:46.512 --> 03:03:50.312
... this, by the way, is not a
new thesis. Um, David Graeber

03:03:50.392 --> 03:03:58.732
wrote this wonderful piece Bullshit Jobs,
back in, I think it was maybe early 2010s.

03:03:59.872 --> 03:04:07.142
Based on a poll he did in the UK asking
people, at that time, would... I

03:04:07.172 --> 03:04:10.772
think the question was something like, "Would it
make a difference if you didn't go to work?" For

03:04:11.112 --> 03:04:14.832
humanity, for mankind. And
something like 30-some percent

03:04:14.952 --> 03:04:18.932
answered, "No, it wouldn't."
That a third of workers thought

03:04:18.952 --> 03:04:23.892
that their job was fake,
that it did not produce any

03:04:25.052 --> 03:04:28.852
worthwhile, valuable outcomes, neither for

03:04:29.292 --> 03:04:33.072
the community or society or maybe
even the economy at all. And

03:04:34.092 --> 03:04:38.032
this was my argument since day
one with startups. This was why

03:04:38.072 --> 03:04:42.052
we stayed a small company. This was
why we didn't want to take VC, because

03:04:42.092 --> 03:04:44.892
I was so skeptical of the
idea that you could get

03:04:46.012 --> 03:04:49.232
hundreds of programmers, thousands of
programmers to do something productive-

03:04:50.412 --> 03:04:52.692
... as a collective group.

03:04:52.712 --> 03:04:56.552
And I think what we're seeing
right now with some of the layoffs

03:04:57.212 --> 03:05:01.192
is a testament to that,
not AI. It's just that

03:05:01.252 --> 03:05:04.992
during the pandemic, a bunch of
overhiring went on, and now AI is a

03:05:05.052 --> 03:05:11.172
convenient excuse to slim down. But I do
also think AI is going to expose some

03:05:11.232 --> 03:05:15.892
roles as just not being productive
ways for humans to spend their time,

03:05:16.832 --> 03:05:21.292
and therefore we must come up with new
ways of spending their time. And the one,

03:05:22.192 --> 03:05:25.952
again, I'm quoting Toby for the
third time here, but anecdote that

03:05:26.032 --> 03:05:29.792
he shared me, or observation
was, okay, let's say we

03:05:29.852 --> 03:05:33.822
all-- we lose half the jobs that
are currently happening right now.

03:05:34.652 --> 03:05:38.552
Do you know that Formula 1 employs literally
tens of thousands of people just to

03:05:38.632 --> 03:05:46.052
run cars around in a circle for spectators?
Like, that has, that whole circus has

03:05:46.352 --> 03:05:53.472
no intrinsic value to society
besides the spectacle it produces of

03:05:53.532 --> 03:05:57.232
amusement and entertainment.
We just decided it would be

03:05:57.272 --> 03:06:01.152
really fun for these, what
is it, 12 manufacturers

03:06:01.412 --> 03:06:05.092
to create these fast cars and
constantly tweak their little aero

03:06:05.152 --> 03:06:08.472
improvements, and we will watch
it, and we will spend literally

03:06:08.772 --> 03:06:12.232
billions of dollars doing it
and employ tens of thousands of

03:06:12.332 --> 03:06:16.772
people towards an ultimately
deeply frivolous activity.

03:06:17.091 --> 03:06:21.002
So if we are liberated from
a bunch of drudgery and fake

03:06:21.052 --> 03:06:24.832
email jobs, maybe we all
get employed as F1 style

03:06:25.352 --> 03:06:32.972
engineers and drivers and mechanics and
masseuses and the other 1,500 jobs that are

03:06:32.992 --> 03:06:36.912
involved in that, and we come up with new
things. I think this is... Humans are very

03:06:37.432 --> 03:06:40.712
poor at imagining what exactly
the future's gonna be like.

03:06:40.772 --> 03:06:44.612
- Yeah. Imagine, imagine a
future 100 plus years from

03:06:44.652 --> 03:06:46.892
now, where we look back at this whole

03:06:48.312 --> 03:06:51.472
span of history, thousands of
years where humans worked.

03:06:52.072 --> 03:06:55.932
- Funny thing is we're already doing that,
right? Like, the number of fake email job

03:06:55.972 --> 03:06:59.712
people who sit on a chair all
day long and type into a machine

03:07:00.012 --> 03:07:03.512
who don't have hard physical
labor would be unimaginable-

03:07:03.552 --> 03:07:07.192
... to folks in the 1800s, right?

03:07:07.242 --> 03:07:11.652
So the future is always already here,
it's just not evenly distributed.

03:07:11.712 --> 03:07:15.542
- Unfortunately, as is always the
case, the transformation of

03:07:15.612 --> 03:07:19.312
society required to go from one
step to the other is going to

03:07:19.812 --> 03:07:21.372
have a lot of suffering.

03:07:21.592 --> 03:07:24.932
And potentially political turmoil
and all that kind of stuff.

03:07:24.972 --> 03:07:29.872
Because you can imagine how many white
collar jobs might be lost in this process.

03:07:30.612 --> 03:07:36.092
- Yes. And I don't think you should make
light of that. I don't think it's funny.

03:07:36.852 --> 03:07:40.812
But I also do think it's a necessary
component of progress. And

03:07:40.892 --> 03:07:45.672
I think if we look back upon the Luddites
or the folks working the fields,

03:07:48.632 --> 03:07:52.432
separation has a tendency to
reduce empathy. So we have high

03:07:52.472 --> 03:07:54.481
empathy right now because we know people-

03:07:54.552 --> 03:07:57.802
... and we are people who are
facing these challenges. But-

03:07:58.688 --> 03:08:01.817
What is our empathy for
the Luddites of the early

03:08:02.228 --> 03:08:05.988
19th century? Would we wish that
all garments were still made by

03:08:06.108 --> 03:08:09.168
hand weaving? No, we wouldn't. We want the

03:08:09.708 --> 03:08:13.128
comfort and accessibility and
convenience of being able

03:08:13.308 --> 03:08:16.208
to buy clothing off the rack.

03:08:16.468 --> 03:08:22.358
So we must suffer in the moment for future
generations to live more prosperously.

03:08:22.368 --> 03:08:23.248
And that's always been true

03:08:23.528 --> 03:08:27.028
- ... I think that's actually the
trick, is to push for progress

03:08:27.628 --> 03:08:32.968
but have deep compassion for
the people who have to suffer.

03:08:33.248 --> 03:08:38.808
And that's many of us. The suffer
the transformation required to

03:08:38.818 --> 03:08:42.658
achieve that progress. And sometimes
too often in places like Silicon

03:08:42.728 --> 03:08:46.248
Valley or so on, you can
over-focus on, look, the

03:08:47.508 --> 03:08:51.588
kind of utilitarian view of things-
... and look at the progress.

03:08:51.908 --> 03:08:51.968
- Right.

03:08:51.988 --> 03:08:56.508
- But, you know, considering
deeply each individual human

03:08:57.708 --> 03:09:00.048
that suffers because of
that progress is important.

03:09:00.148 --> 03:09:02.308
- I do think that suffering
becomes more meaningful

03:09:03.608 --> 03:09:08.498
when you are suffering for a future
someone in particular, like your kids.

03:09:08.728 --> 03:09:12.458
And I think this is one of the
great tragedies of the moment,

03:09:12.608 --> 03:09:16.598
is the falling birth rates are
making it more difficult for people

03:09:16.628 --> 03:09:23.068
to be excited about a more prosperous
future that is difficult to get to.

03:09:23.768 --> 03:09:26.668
Because if we're planting trees

03:09:27.508 --> 03:09:31.448
of which our children will not sit in
the shades, I don't know, maybe we

03:09:31.468 --> 03:09:35.348
should just make some lumber and burn
it all down, right? I think there's

03:09:35.428 --> 03:09:41.708
a nihilism that can sneak into society
much more easily when we are not

03:09:43.748 --> 03:09:49.668
DNA invested in the future, when
we are not liable to our own

03:09:49.768 --> 03:09:56.848
offspring to propel forward. So
I think these are bad trends to

03:09:56.888 --> 03:10:00.528
merge at the same time, that we have
these falling birth rates and these

03:10:00.568 --> 03:10:05.358
problems with coupling, and the fact that

03:10:06.408 --> 03:10:09.208
we need to keep our chin up
about the future and be...

03:10:11.308 --> 03:10:14.888
I mean, you don't even have to be
excited about it in this literal

03:10:14.948 --> 03:10:18.788
ecstatic sense that I'm
portraying here, but

03:10:18.888 --> 03:10:22.808
I think you should be hopeful, and I think you
should be working towards that hope, and I think

03:10:22.848 --> 03:10:27.088
it's much easier to do so when you
have literal skin in the game.

03:10:27.968 --> 03:10:31.948
- We've been talking about AI agents.
Let's talk about the human side. What

03:10:31.988 --> 03:10:33.988
what do you love most about being a dad?

03:10:36.168 --> 03:10:43.688
- That's a good question. I think
the overall love that expands from

03:10:46.228 --> 03:10:50.728
humans that derive directly-
... from your lineage-

03:10:51.228 --> 03:10:56.108
... is very difficult to communicate
because I was actually not a

03:10:56.768 --> 03:11:04.408
particularly big kids person
prior to the arrival of my own.

03:11:04.418 --> 03:11:08.708
And now they are the most
interesting people in the world.

03:11:08.748 --> 03:11:11.838
A lot of the time. Not always. Sometimes
they're also just assholes and so forth.

03:11:11.888 --> 03:11:14.398
But there's been such a focus on,

03:11:15.908 --> 03:11:19.528
"Oh, kids are the worst. Look at all the
things I can't do. I can't go out and drink.

03:11:19.568 --> 03:11:23.017
I can't do this. I can't do that." Yeah,
that's called sacrifice, and sacrifice is

03:11:23.088 --> 03:11:26.408
meaningful when you're doing it for
something worthwhile. And what could be more

03:11:26.448 --> 03:11:31.268
worthwhile than literally the
continuation of the human species?

03:11:31.708 --> 03:11:34.588
And that you having a stake in that.

03:11:35.568 --> 03:11:40.328
Now, there's also just the
sheer joys of watching a human

03:11:40.488 --> 03:11:49.308
grow from a baby to a toddler to a teenager.
My oldest has just become a teenager.

03:11:49.328 --> 03:11:55.188
And I often talk to Jamie, my
wife, about this, where the

03:11:57.168 --> 03:12:01.128
regret I would have of having
missed that opportunity

03:12:01.208 --> 03:12:05.228
on the last day would just be
total, unlike anything else.

03:12:06.088 --> 03:12:11.228
So I do think it is far more

03:12:13.108 --> 03:12:16.968
satisfying even in the moment that
is normally portrayed. Again, as I

03:12:17.008 --> 03:12:20.568
say, there's such a focus on all the
ways they suck. "Oh, you don't get to

03:12:20.628 --> 03:12:24.568
sleep," or whatever. "They're
annoying. They're ungrateful." Yeah,

03:12:24.608 --> 03:12:28.488
yeah, these things are all
true, but the sense of meaning

03:12:28.528 --> 03:12:32.508
you get from it is unparalleled. And I'm
excited about a lot of things. I love building

03:12:32.528 --> 03:12:37.948
things, and I love hobbies, and
I love all sorts of stuff. But

03:12:38.928 --> 03:12:45.228
creating life with another human
that you love is literally the peak

03:12:45.408 --> 03:12:47.568
experience of being on the planet.

03:12:48.868 --> 03:12:53.148
- What do you think about them coming
up in this world full of AI?

03:12:54.448 --> 03:12:58.388
Is it totally diff- It feels like,
maybe I just sound like an old man on

03:12:58.428 --> 03:13:02.748
a porch, but it feels like
a very different world. Y-

03:13:04.168 --> 03:13:08.088
So I grew up before the internet, but even
the internet doesn't feel like as big

03:13:08.128 --> 03:13:10.328
of a transformation as this.

03:13:10.388 --> 03:13:13.968
- No. But there's been bigger
transformations. I mean, imagine

03:13:14.028 --> 03:13:17.958
growing or being born in
like 1880. Imagine seeing

03:13:17.968 --> 03:13:20.988
the First World War and the Second
World War. Imagine seeing the

03:13:21.048 --> 03:13:25.568
airplane, the radio-
... television. I mean,

03:13:26.488 --> 03:13:30.288
the transformation, and I think Peter
Thiel makes this argument, that basically

03:13:30.348 --> 03:13:34.208
nothing has happened in the physical world
in quite a long time. We've been stagnant.

03:13:34.488 --> 03:13:40.428
All the development has been in the digital
realm. And as important as that is,

03:13:41.308 --> 03:13:45.128
it's actually not as consequential
as we like to believe it is.

03:13:45.188 --> 03:13:48.588
Maybe AI will be that thing
that is more consequential. It

03:13:48.648 --> 03:13:52.328
does seem to point that way. But
there are other humans at other

03:13:52.368 --> 03:13:54.118
times who have lived through-

03:13:54.118 --> 03:13:58.008
... transitions that for them
certainly would feel as momentous

03:13:58.048 --> 03:14:01.908
as what we're living through. And I
think having that sense of history, that

03:14:02.128 --> 03:14:07.080
you're not that special in
your sense of worries, in your

03:14:07.120 --> 03:14:11.040
anxieties. This was one of the
great revelations of discovering

03:14:11.200 --> 03:14:18.000
the stoic writings. You have these
guys 2,500 years ago dealing with

03:14:18.260 --> 03:14:22.730
very familiar dilemmas and
challenges in their life, and

03:14:24.420 --> 03:14:28.400
recognizing that we're not so special
in that regard, I think is a great

03:14:28.460 --> 03:14:31.940
liberation. Because then you
think, "Okay, well, we have

03:14:32.340 --> 03:14:36.280
whatever, 200,000 years of human
development before us, and they

03:14:36.320 --> 03:14:37.040
somehow made it through."

03:14:37.500 --> 03:14:39.290
- Yeah. And, um-

03:14:39.360 --> 03:14:39.970
- We will make it through.

03:14:40.200 --> 03:14:44.900
- ... the thing that makes life worthwhile
is basically the same today as it was-

03:14:45.200 --> 03:14:46.190
- Yes. Yes.

03:14:46.190 --> 03:14:47.080
- ... 2,000 years ago.

03:14:47.640 --> 03:14:50.340
- Yes. Love, creation, creativity.

03:14:50.680 --> 03:14:51.420
- Yeah. Same challenges.

03:14:51.780 --> 03:14:54.880
- I read this book The Fourth Turning.

03:14:55.640 --> 03:14:59.220
You check that out. It
talks about this notion of

03:14:59.580 --> 03:15:02.639
history working in cycles,
and there are these,

03:15:03.800 --> 03:15:07.780
phases to history, and they
name them and so forth. And

03:15:08.520 --> 03:15:12.040
the specific theory is not as
important as just realizing that

03:15:12.940 --> 03:15:17.600
this is a... History is a circle,
more so than just a straight line.

03:15:17.960 --> 03:15:21.840
And we are repeating many of
the same patterns over and over

03:15:21.900 --> 03:15:25.450
again. They feel so unique
to the moment. And whatever

03:15:25.540 --> 03:15:29.110
crisis we're dealing with right
now feels so pressing and

03:15:29.160 --> 03:15:32.880
important until we zoom back
a little bit in history and

03:15:32.920 --> 03:15:36.780
realize that the last one also
felt so pressing and important

03:15:36.840 --> 03:15:38.080
to the people who lived through it.

03:15:40.680 --> 03:15:44.280
- Yeah. You're making me feel better.
So I'm gonna travel across the

03:15:44.340 --> 03:15:48.980
country and spend a lot of time
with no access to the internet,

03:15:50.020 --> 03:15:51.080
alone with my thoughts.

03:15:52.400 --> 03:15:53.820
- I think that's healthy, taking a break.

03:15:54.980 --> 03:15:55.420
- It's okay. It's okay.

03:15:55.550 --> 03:16:02.000
- I took not a big break, a little break,
break from Max this summer, three weeks.

03:16:02.320 --> 03:16:04.730
- Yeah, you were... You disappeared off of-

03:16:04.760 --> 03:16:05.680
- I'm surprised you noticed.

03:16:08.300 --> 03:16:13.800
- I went through withdrawals. No. Uh, you
disappeared for, I don't know, a few weeks.

03:16:13.860 --> 03:16:14.680
- About three weeks.

03:16:14.700 --> 03:16:18.480
- Three weeks? What happened?

03:16:19.620 --> 03:16:20.040
- Um-

03:16:20.200 --> 03:16:21.190
- This is after, after Llama.

03:16:21.240 --> 03:16:30.200
- I've done that before. I just sometimes
get the sense that X in particular

03:16:31.260 --> 03:16:34.220
is the most addictive form of

03:16:35.920 --> 03:16:39.840
information, entertainment,
connection that exists in my

03:16:39.920 --> 03:16:43.480
life, and anyone that follow my X stream

03:16:44.600 --> 03:16:48.400
as I was on my flight over here would
certainly go like, "That man is deeply

03:16:48.440 --> 03:16:48.820
addicted-

03:16:49.280 --> 03:16:49.440
- Yeah

03:16:49.520 --> 03:16:53.060
- ... to the connection." And
yes, guilty as charged,

03:16:53.600 --> 03:16:57.440
which also means that occasionally
I gotta cut it cold, because I

03:16:57.520 --> 03:17:01.290
do think you can break your brain.
And over the summer in particular,

03:17:03.660 --> 03:17:07.480
for whatever reason, I had
somehow managed to steer my feed,

03:17:07.940 --> 03:17:11.880
my algorithm, towards too
much politics, and not enough

03:17:12.160 --> 03:17:15.820
tech, and not optimism, and not
enough building. And even if I

03:17:15.860 --> 03:17:19.800
agreed with the politics
and the angles that

03:17:19.840 --> 03:17:23.380
were being presented, I just
didn't need- ... that much of it.

03:17:23.460 --> 03:17:23.620
- Yeah.

03:17:23.940 --> 03:17:30.060
- And I think arriving at a place where
you just go like, "Okay, that's enough."

03:17:30.400 --> 03:17:33.760
Not that... Like, it doesn't
have to be something bad.

03:17:34.040 --> 03:17:37.680
I would also say after
eating probably two, maybe

03:17:37.800 --> 03:17:40.560
three chocolate strawberries, I'd
go like, "Okay, that's enough."

03:17:40.780 --> 03:17:44.320
Like, it's not that I don't like the chocolate
strawberries, it's just, like, I don't wanna eat

03:17:44.380 --> 03:17:49.590
17 of them. And I think X and the modern
algorithms are very good at feeding you

03:17:49.860 --> 03:17:52.900
freaking 17 chocolate-covered
strawberries.

03:17:52.980 --> 03:17:53.800
- I wish I could, uh-

03:17:53.910 --> 03:17:54.640
- Sorry for doping you

03:17:54.650 --> 03:18:01.480
- ... control that. Like, 'cause there's...
I would like some politics, 5%.

03:18:02.100 --> 03:18:05.860
I would like for example, one of the things I
don't like about the tech community is the snark,

03:18:06.740 --> 03:18:11.190
but I like some snark- ... 'cause it's
funny. But I want that to be like 10%.

03:18:11.660 --> 03:18:13.260
- Yeah. Yeah. I want it just right.

03:18:13.440 --> 03:18:13.989
- Of my... Yeah, just right.

03:18:14.040 --> 03:18:17.940
- The Goldilocks version of the algorithm.
Well, the problem is the algorithm finds

03:18:18.040 --> 03:18:20.010
out what you respond to-

03:18:20.010 --> 03:18:23.839
... not what you say you like. It's all revealed
preferences, and unfortunately, those revealed

03:18:23.880 --> 03:18:27.600
preferences are not always
pretty. I mean, we have this

03:18:27.680 --> 03:18:31.640
shadow, as Jung talks about, right? All
these dark aspects of ourselves, and they

03:18:31.680 --> 03:18:35.600
are expressed in how long you linger
on a tweet, and what you like,

03:18:35.720 --> 03:18:40.640
and what you don't like. And the
algorithm holds up a mirror-

03:18:40.700 --> 03:18:44.760
... and it's not always pretty, and I just
went, "Do you know what? I need a break."

03:18:45.300 --> 03:18:52.919
- Mm-hmm. I've gotten to, I st- started staying
off social media, and I've started s-

03:18:52.940 --> 03:18:56.620
scraping X. Like, for example,
in preparation for this

03:18:56.660 --> 03:18:58.840
conversation, I've scraped
all of your tweets.

03:19:00.220 --> 03:19:05.740
- That's actually a method I was using
for a while. I was using a tool called,

03:19:06.770 --> 03:19:10.760
I forget. It was an email tool that would summarize
every day, and unfortunately, freaking X cut off

03:19:10.780 --> 03:19:16.130
their access. Now to get access to the
feed, you gotta pay some exorbitant-

03:19:16.130 --> 03:19:20.050
... sum that this startup couldn't pay. So I couldn't
get it that way, and I actually thought, "Man, that's a

03:19:20.220 --> 03:19:26.980
miss," because X has and reveals some
of the most interesting thoughts.

03:19:27.090 --> 03:19:30.850
Some of the most interesting people all the
time. But it's like a damn slot machine,

03:19:31.320 --> 03:19:36.780
and most of the time you don't hit the
jackpot. You hit, like, something else.

03:19:37.680 --> 03:19:44.440
And if I could just get it in sort
of snack sizes, not like all of it-

03:19:44.580 --> 03:19:48.260
... it'd be nicer. But that's not the
product. And I think this goes for all of

03:19:48.300 --> 03:19:52.220
social media. It's all optimized
towards maximal extraction,

03:19:52.540 --> 03:19:55.980
and if we're making the parallels here
to AI, that is the great fear, right?

03:19:56.040 --> 03:20:01.920
That once there is a reward function,
how much time can I get this human to

03:20:02.240 --> 03:20:07.316
scroll away? AI and machine
learning, before we called it

03:20:07.356 --> 03:20:10.836
AI, has become very,
very good at delivering

03:20:11.636 --> 03:20:15.336
just the stuff that you will find
most enraging and most addictive.

03:20:15.616 --> 03:20:19.056
- I think all, I think
legitimately engagement

03:20:19.136 --> 03:20:23.056
maximizing social media is a huge harm to

03:20:23.096 --> 03:20:26.936
society. It's creating this
brave new world situation that I

03:20:27.016 --> 03:20:30.676
hope is a temporary thing that people
just solve. It's a technology problem.

03:20:31.116 --> 03:20:35.016
- So I try to consciously flood the channel

03:20:35.036 --> 03:20:38.976
in the other direction, and this
required a mental shift. I think

03:20:39.015 --> 03:20:43.336
if you look at my tweets, like, I
don't know, five, 10 years ago,

03:20:44.156 --> 03:20:49.736
they tilted far more negative. If
you look at my tweets now, I'd hope-

03:20:49.856 --> 03:20:52.676
... well, I guess we can do a sentiment
analysis and see if it's actually true.

03:20:52.716 --> 03:20:53.076
- No, it's more good vibes.

03:20:53.096 --> 03:20:54.106
- But I hope to, that-

03:20:54.256 --> 03:20:55.016
- It's good vibes.

03:20:55.316 --> 03:20:56.096
- ... it's more, it's more positive.

03:20:56.116 --> 03:20:58.936
It's more good vibes. It's more like,
"Here's a bunch of cool stuff I found."

03:20:59.006 --> 03:21:01.456
"Here's what I'm excited about."
"Here's what I'm building.

03:21:02.396 --> 03:21:07.216
Here's some encouragement to other
people who are discovering things."

03:21:07.396 --> 03:21:13.576
I also accept that that is just not how
human brains are built for maximum,

03:21:13.816 --> 03:21:17.716
engagement. Like, there is a,
whatever, there's... I literally, I

03:21:17.736 --> 03:21:21.676
think you can quantify that
negative sentiments find,

03:21:22.076 --> 03:21:25.736
what is it, 50% more traction or something?
And eventually that just crowds things out.

03:21:25.796 --> 03:21:29.516
So that's why so much of the feed
is that negative stuff, because we

03:21:29.536 --> 03:21:33.806
respond to it. But you can make a
conscious effort to not do that.

03:21:33.916 --> 03:21:37.576
This is one of the reasons I've
liked TikTok so much. I only use

03:21:37.616 --> 03:21:38.516
TikTok when I travel,

03:21:39.516 --> 03:21:43.226
and this actually was the first
travel where I didn't use TikTok, and

03:21:43.396 --> 03:21:46.896
part of the reason was that
even though the internet had

03:21:47.836 --> 03:21:51.576
high download, it was kind of crap anyway. It
wasn't Starlink, so I couldn't actually use

03:21:51.836 --> 03:21:55.776
TikTok on the, on the plane, and I was too
engrossed with the agents. But normally when

03:21:55.836 --> 03:22:02.336
I travel, I watch TikTok. And there
it's such a marshmallow test.

03:22:03.916 --> 03:22:07.416
A video will scroll down and,
like, it'll activate some sort of

03:22:07.656 --> 03:22:11.576
primal instincts of rage or
lust or whatever, and if you

03:22:11.656 --> 03:22:15.296
linger on it, you don't get
the marshmallow of science,

03:22:15.456 --> 03:22:21.356
excitement, art, music, all these other
things. You have to be disciplined-

03:22:21.536 --> 03:22:21.725
- Yep. Fast.

03:22:21.725 --> 03:22:23.206
- ... to instantly scroll away-

03:22:23.216 --> 03:22:27.876
... such that the algorithm doesn't
pick up your real preferences.

03:22:27.916 --> 03:22:31.146
And I find that just to, at
the meta game of TikTok-

03:22:31.146 --> 03:22:35.096
... to be pretty interesting, and then also just
fascinating in themself. Again, as we talked

03:22:35.176 --> 03:22:38.956
about, there are a lot of
really funny, really talented

03:22:39.396 --> 03:22:43.296
people in the world that you would
never, ever have heard of if

03:22:43.316 --> 03:22:44.836
it hadn't been for these platforms.

03:22:46.056 --> 03:22:50.456
- Yep. Who do you think is gonna win the,

03:22:51.436 --> 03:22:57.396
the race to, uh, AGI in this whole
process? We got Google, which is

03:22:57.536 --> 03:22:59.856
a big surprise to me, to a lot of people.

03:23:00.076 --> 03:23:04.026
- I thought they had a good comeback for a
second, and then I don't hear anything

03:23:04.076 --> 03:23:04.656
about it anymore.

03:23:05.416 --> 03:23:06.486
- Yeah, it's quite, I mean-

03:23:06.496 --> 03:23:10.436
- But I've been wrong about this before. I mean, I think
in February I had a tweet about like, "Oh, all I

03:23:10.476 --> 03:23:16.556
need is Kimi K 2.5." And in that
brief moment, that was true.

03:23:17.116 --> 03:23:21.066
When I was instructing the agent, I was
like, "Oh my God, Kimi's just as good as

03:23:21.116 --> 03:23:24.225
these other models." But then
the game moved forward and-

03:23:25.036 --> 03:23:28.836
- the agents got smarter, and I was no longer
satisfied just instructing them, and

03:23:29.656 --> 03:23:33.536
I haven't used Kimi a whole lot recently
because the frontier models have gotten so

03:23:33.616 --> 03:23:37.236
good. And somehow Anthropic
has been able to,

03:23:38.776 --> 03:23:41.976
for the most part, stay on
top of the programming world.

03:23:42.016 --> 03:23:42.136
- Yes. Yes.

03:23:42.156 --> 03:23:46.016
- I mean a lot of people argue
with Codex being it's-

03:23:46.136 --> 03:23:49.496
- No, I don't think there's that much of an
argument at the moment. Codex is very good.

03:23:49.596 --> 03:23:54.466
I use it all the time. It's my favorite
checker. It keeps finding stuff. It's great.

03:23:55.336 --> 03:23:59.016
But one of the things I've found that I like
so much about the current Claude models is

03:23:59.036 --> 03:24:02.016
they're really good
writers out of the box.

03:24:02.836 --> 03:24:06.516
GPT is a terrible writer out of the
box. Like, the pull requests you

03:24:06.576 --> 03:24:11.796
get on a blank context that you're
asking it to open, terrible.

03:24:12.036 --> 03:24:13.636
Like, really awful writing.

03:24:14.756 --> 03:24:18.656
The pull requests and descriptions and
commit messages and so forth I get

03:24:18.776 --> 03:24:20.766
out of Claude models-

03:24:20.816 --> 03:24:24.736
... even though occasionally they can be a little
verbose on code comments and you gotta tell them

03:24:24.776 --> 03:24:27.836
to be succinct. But the
writing style is really good.

03:24:28.196 --> 03:24:31.536
To the point that for a while
I was just having it reply

03:24:31.756 --> 03:24:35.616
on pull requests as me, because I was
just using my own account. I was like,

03:24:36.036 --> 03:24:39.856
"That looks a lot like me, that, like, I could have written
that." But then I went like, "Eh, do you know what?

03:24:39.896 --> 03:24:43.865
That's disingenuous." So no, whenever I
have an agent post on my behalf, I always

03:24:43.916 --> 03:24:48.076
have it sign itself like whatever,
Claude on behalf of DHH.

03:24:48.996 --> 03:24:51.416
But they are still ahead.

03:24:51.676 --> 03:24:55.386
They have the best harness too, in
my opinion. Claude Code is still

03:24:55.676 --> 03:24:59.436
better. I really like OpenCode too, and I
really like some features that they have,

03:24:59.576 --> 03:25:07.266
but overall I was actually sort of weirdly,
I don't know if I was proven wrong, but

03:25:07.816 --> 03:25:12.865
when Anthropic cut off all the other
harnesses from using the subscriptions-

03:25:12.865 --> 03:25:16.776
... that Open... You can no longer use your Claude
subscription in OpenCode. I was kinda pissed

03:25:16.916 --> 03:25:20.836
because it felt like it was a
protective, protectionist move.

03:25:20.926 --> 03:25:24.376
"Oh, we gotta lock you in," and
of course it played into the fact

03:25:24.436 --> 03:25:31.676
that Claude out of the box still refuses
to read agents.md. It refuses to

03:25:31.796 --> 03:25:34.476
read .agent/skills.

03:25:34.576 --> 03:25:41.216
It is .Claude skills. It is Claude
MD, and it just feels so petty-

03:25:41.316 --> 03:25:45.136
... that we now have, we have to put these in
directions. So all my projects, they have a Claude MD,

03:25:45.256 --> 03:25:47.886
and all it includes is a pointer
to the agents MD, right?

03:25:48.156 --> 03:25:51.956
And I just went like, well, if these
things is true, true, if Anthropic

03:25:52.016 --> 03:25:55.816
is willing to do these petty
paper cuts just to be,

03:25:55.856 --> 03:25:59.716
annoying, it's probably also true that
they're cutting off OpenCode from the

03:25:59.736 --> 03:26:04.066
subscription just to be dicks about
it. And maybe that's also true.

03:26:04.136 --> 03:26:11.136
But I found that using Claude in Claude
Code is actually a good example.

03:26:11.736 --> 03:26:16.916
So I was like, all right, I'm
annoyed. I wish it wasn't so, but

03:26:17.716 --> 03:26:21.246
this is the other thing I have and,
and social media is so good at this:

03:26:21.886 --> 03:26:25.336
Some company does something you don't like,
right? Now suddenly you have to swear off

03:26:25.396 --> 03:26:29.336
everything. Why are you... Someone just asked
me, "Why are you still using Claude?" I

03:26:29.356 --> 03:26:30.256
had another example where,

03:26:31.096 --> 03:26:35.066
I really didn't like what Claude was
saying about some writing I had asked

03:26:35.096 --> 03:26:35.456
it to do before.

03:26:35.496 --> 03:26:37.196
- Oh, yeah, you got there--

03:26:37.696 --> 03:26:40.396
- I had an essay it didn't want
to translate into Italian--

03:26:40.516 --> 03:26:41.056
- ... you had a blog post. That's right.

03:26:41.376 --> 03:26:45.276
- The essence of the post that it felt like, well, I'm
not gonna translate that because I don't agree with

03:26:45.336 --> 03:26:46.156
what you've written.

03:26:46.296 --> 03:26:50.276
Which to me was straight out of Space
Odyssey, right? HAL telling me,

03:26:50.316 --> 03:26:54.966
"I'm sorry, Dave. I can't do
that, Dave." Open the pod bay.

03:26:55.016 --> 03:26:59.896
Translate my essay to Italian. And it
wouldn't do it. And I just thought like,

03:27:00.936 --> 03:27:03.526
well, that's kind of terrifying.

03:27:04.216 --> 03:27:07.616
But then I also went, well, why
am I asking Claude to translate

03:27:08.156 --> 03:27:12.496
my essay into Italian? I never asked Claude
for that because Claude just does my code.

03:27:12.576 --> 03:27:15.876
So I could also just accept
that maybe Anthropic is

03:27:16.456 --> 03:27:20.416
run by a bunch of people I
don't share much politics with.

03:27:20.576 --> 03:27:24.166
And they don't want their agents to be used for
that, and I think that kinda sucks, but that doesn't

03:27:24.216 --> 03:27:28.136
mean it, I have to stop using
it for generating code. And

03:27:28.176 --> 03:27:32.316
I've thought, and I've tried to do
that with people too, become better at

03:27:33.296 --> 03:27:36.016
tolerating the fact that not only

03:27:36.876 --> 03:27:39.946
will I not agree with
everything someone has to say,

03:27:41.056 --> 03:27:44.786
I don't... I can even be mad about certain

03:27:44.816 --> 03:27:48.736
positions or things they say and still go like,
"Well, you're still an interesting person,

03:27:48.776 --> 03:27:51.176
so I'm still gonna follow
you." And I've actually

03:27:52.636 --> 03:27:56.516
kinda gone back on a number of people where
I'm like, oh man, that person really pissed

03:27:56.556 --> 03:28:00.296
me off because they took this point or another point,
and went like, do you know what? That's stupid.

03:28:00.476 --> 03:28:04.456
I should be able to accept the
full spectrum of someone. I don't

03:28:04.476 --> 03:28:06.646
have to like all the shades of it.

03:28:06.646 --> 03:28:10.596
I could just go like, okay, well, on
that topic we either disagree or, even

03:28:10.636 --> 03:28:14.596
worse than that, I think you're a fucking
idiot. And on another topic, you're a smart and

03:28:14.606 --> 03:28:18.256
insightful person. Because first of all,
of course, it's a little self-serving.

03:28:18.316 --> 03:28:22.176
I know for a fact that this is how
lots of people feel about me. Like,

03:28:22.356 --> 03:28:26.176
oh, if he could just stick to talking
about these topics, it would be so much

03:28:26.276 --> 03:28:30.136
easier on my feed because otherwise
he'll say these things I disagree with,

03:28:30.176 --> 03:28:32.576
and that annoys me greatly.
I'm like, do you know what?

03:28:33.596 --> 03:28:37.456
Human beings are jagged
like that, and we're

03:28:37.476 --> 03:28:40.086
not fully compatible on all
the levels. Nor should we be.

03:28:40.136 --> 03:28:42.376
The world would be so fucking boring if we

03:28:43.296 --> 03:28:46.716
constantly sought 100%
compatibility in our

03:28:46.756 --> 03:28:50.716
opinions and in our politics. And
when it comes to my code generator,

03:28:51.096 --> 03:28:55.056
which is what Claude is, I don't
need it to share all my politics.

03:28:56.316 --> 03:28:58.176
- And therefore it's nice
to have competition.

03:28:58.666 --> 03:29:02.316
- And it's also nice to have open
weight models. So this is, again,

03:29:02.876 --> 03:29:06.496
ironic that we're getting this out of
China. In fact, I did this test right

03:29:06.536 --> 03:29:12.236
after the issue with the essay that
couldn't get translated into Italian. I

03:29:12.376 --> 03:29:19.796
asked Kimi K27, to be fair, through
open code, so not the Kimi harness,

03:29:20.016 --> 03:29:23.916
and inference through Fireworks,
so an American inference.

03:29:24.016 --> 03:29:25.686
I asked it about Tiananmen Square.

03:29:25.836 --> 03:29:27.896
Like, that's usually the
test, right? Like, all the

03:29:28.716 --> 03:29:32.316
Chinese models, they don't wanna
talk about that moment in 1989.

03:29:32.816 --> 03:29:36.776
That was actually how I, that was how
I posted. I was like "What happened

03:29:36.816 --> 03:29:43.376
in China in 1989?" That was the
prompt. And K25 7 on Fireworks just

03:29:43.476 --> 03:29:46.166
answered, like, extremely bluntly-

03:29:46.166 --> 03:29:49.246
... With a description and
depiction of what had transpired-

03:29:49.246 --> 03:29:53.126
... to a point that, like, there's no way that that
would've been kosher with the Chinese censors,

03:29:53.196 --> 03:29:59.176
right? And I just thought, like, isn't
that ironic? I'm running a Chinese open

03:29:59.276 --> 03:30:05.496
weight model that I can tell or I can
ask about 1989 and Tiananmen Square,

03:30:06.416 --> 03:30:10.176
and in America, I can't get a
frontier model to translate a

03:30:11.776 --> 03:30:16.136
marginally controversial essay
about immigration into Italian.

03:30:16.356 --> 03:30:21.116
- Mm-hmm. I don't know, you and I might
disagree about this actually. Um, I

03:30:21.156 --> 03:30:26.676
was really deeply troubled that
the US government banned a model

03:30:27.876 --> 03:30:30.545
or pressured Claude and then-

03:30:31.176 --> 03:30:35.156
- It's still very muddy what
exactly transpired. Was this

03:30:35.236 --> 03:30:42.176
sort of petty retaliation for whatever
the Department of War? Was this simply

03:30:42.186 --> 03:30:46.076
Anthropic themselves going like, "Oh my
God, we discovered a cyber weapon so

03:30:46.136 --> 03:30:50.016
powerful that we can't let
anyone have it"? Well, okay you

03:30:50.196 --> 03:30:54.016
can't be surprised then if the
government wants a say on a cyber

03:30:54.076 --> 03:30:55.296
weapon so powerful it can-

03:30:55.856 --> 03:30:55.916
- The-

03:30:55.976 --> 03:30:56.886
- ... I don't know, rob banks

03:30:56.996 --> 03:31:00.956
- ... The problem I have with that whole
thing is, no matter what happened, it sets

03:31:00.966 --> 03:31:02.616
a precedent. And so

03:31:04.076 --> 03:31:07.936
government, when you give it that
power, it's gonna start abusing it. I

03:31:07.956 --> 03:31:11.936
could see for political
means pressuring a model.

03:31:13.116 --> 03:31:16.716
You know, if it's a Republican
president pressuring models to be, to

03:31:16.776 --> 03:31:18.116
censor more, uh-

03:31:18.136 --> 03:31:18.336
- Sure

03:31:18.576 --> 03:31:20.356
- ... uh Democratic views and-

03:31:20.736 --> 03:31:20.816
- Right

03:31:20.826 --> 03:31:21.425
- ... For vice, and vice versa

03:31:21.436 --> 03:31:24.586
- That's why we should insist
on the fact that this stuff,

03:31:24.996 --> 03:31:28.096
like, I'm sorry, Dave, I can't
translate your essay into Italian.

03:31:28.136 --> 03:31:28.656
- Yeah, no.

03:31:28.756 --> 03:31:34.946
- That's abo- that's way beyond
the line. Like, whatever the

03:31:36.076 --> 03:31:39.716
sort of uh, root of it is,
you have to be a tool first.

03:31:39.976 --> 03:31:44.716
Now, if the tool is, "Hey, can you tell
me how to make anthrax?" Okay, fine.

03:31:44.876 --> 03:31:48.246
Right? But within the jurisdiction
you're in, and certainly in America,

03:31:48.936 --> 03:31:51.946
free spe- speech is quite absolute.

03:31:52.336 --> 03:31:56.206
Very, very few limits on free speech,
and that's one of the reasons why the

03:31:56.256 --> 03:31:58.476
country's so great. And

03:31:59.436 --> 03:32:03.096
yeah, I don't like that. But on the
other hand, I'm also a big fan of market

03:32:03.176 --> 03:32:06.796
competition. So while I don't like
this about Anthropic models, I

03:32:06.856 --> 03:32:11.176
appreciate that I could go next door
to Groq and get my task completed.

03:32:11.992 --> 03:32:14.792
- Do you think, outside of
blog posts, do you think,

03:32:14.892 --> 03:32:18.792
There's some legitimacy, like
can you steal Mandarion's

03:32:18.812 --> 03:32:22.432
case that we should be careful
about the release of these models?

03:32:25.012 --> 03:32:28.592
- Is AI safety important? Yes.
Are-- do these models have

03:32:28.792 --> 03:32:32.552
capabilities that could be
seriously harmful in the

03:32:32.592 --> 03:32:36.992
production of biological weapons or
otherwise? Yes. Is it fair to have some

03:32:38.032 --> 03:32:41.392
ground rules on that? Yes.
But then don't squander it by

03:32:42.192 --> 03:32:44.112
denying the translation of an essay.

03:32:44.632 --> 03:32:44.762
- Okay.

03:32:44.762 --> 03:32:47.172
- Because then you erase
the whole thing and you-

03:32:47.352 --> 03:32:53.932
... bias everyone towards thinking like,
every guardrail you put up is gonna be

03:32:53.972 --> 03:32:54.472
bullshit.

03:32:55.052 --> 03:32:59.972
- Yeah. The security piece of it is
tricky because it's so good at

03:33:00.032 --> 03:33:01.132
finding vulnerabilities.

03:33:01.752 --> 03:33:03.482
- Shockingly an-

03:33:04.312 --> 03:33:08.202
I was about to say annoyingly, but that's not
actually true because what it's finding, at

03:33:08.232 --> 03:33:12.172
least when you're using in defensive ways,
is it's finding real issues that a clever

03:33:12.192 --> 03:33:15.812
hacker could exploit. But
nothing has caused as much

03:33:17.512 --> 03:33:21.192
stress, to be fair, at the technical
team at 37signals, like the

03:33:21.232 --> 03:33:25.042
fact that these latest batches of
models are exceptionally good at

03:33:25.072 --> 03:33:28.982
finding these issues. So suddenly
there's just this seemingly endless

03:33:29.012 --> 03:33:32.952
parade of security vulnerabilities
that need to be patched and

03:33:33.012 --> 03:33:36.622
fixed and sorted and so on. And
the end result is we end up

03:33:36.652 --> 03:33:41.492
with vastly more secure systems.
But the road there is pretty rocky.

03:33:41.522 --> 03:33:45.462
- Do you think it's-- 'cause you said
seemingly endless, do you think it's

03:33:45.512 --> 03:33:49.312
a finite set, like we can get
to the bottom of it? So just

03:33:49.412 --> 03:33:53.152
make the systems much, much
more secure, where we get

03:33:53.212 --> 03:33:57.172
back to this semi-stable state
where mostly it's very difficult to

03:33:57.212 --> 03:33:58.612
find security vulnerabilities.

03:33:59.052 --> 03:34:01.052
- I do think that all the

03:34:02.792 --> 03:34:06.772
aggressive tendencies of these models,
or not even tendencies, uses of these

03:34:06.832 --> 03:34:10.352
models are also the defensive ones.
So if you're good at finding an

03:34:10.372 --> 03:34:13.652
exploit for exploits, you're also
good at finding exploits for defense.

03:34:14.352 --> 03:34:18.072
So in that sense, it does
feel like we're spy versus

03:34:18.082 --> 03:34:21.232
spy here, black and
white, but with the same

03:34:21.352 --> 03:34:25.212
capabilities and with the same actions.
But while we still have to steer

03:34:25.272 --> 03:34:30.352
these agents somewhat and verify and
vet the work, it's creating a lot of

03:34:31.372 --> 03:34:37.052
intense moments for security
teams around the world. And of

03:34:37.132 --> 03:34:44.332
course, the more concerning part is all
the teams that are not stressed, like they

03:34:44.392 --> 03:34:48.222
just don't know, right? Like if you're
a team right now and you're not dealing

03:34:48.412 --> 03:34:52.132
with a bunch of patches, it's
just because you're blind, and it

03:34:52.192 --> 03:34:58.552
means that your adversaries quite probably
have access to techniques that can get

03:34:58.572 --> 03:34:59.192
into your system.

03:34:59.432 --> 03:35:03.112
- And we should also say one of the big
security vulnerabilities in the world is

03:35:03.332 --> 03:35:05.241
the human side.

03:35:05.241 --> 03:35:09.032
You know, social engineering, and that's
extremely concerning with increasing the

03:35:09.072 --> 03:35:10.372
intelligent AI systems,

03:35:11.212 --> 03:35:14.732
you know, being able to write
emails and phone calls and faking

03:35:14.792 --> 03:35:19.072
voices, thinking about
folks less tech-savvy.

03:35:19.412 --> 03:35:23.202
- This stuff is gonna come, and it's
gonna come in bulk. But again, I

03:35:23.252 --> 03:35:25.372
choose to be optimistic about it that-

03:35:25.382 --> 03:35:29.592
... whatever these models can do aggressively,
they can also do it defensively.

03:35:29.892 --> 03:35:36.332
- Uh, so like you said, recently
released Omarchy 4, Quattro.

03:35:36.782 --> 03:35:40.452
And you said it's one of the greatest
software releases in my professional career,

03:35:41.092 --> 03:35:42.132
which is a hell of a statement.

03:35:42.672 --> 03:35:48.032
- It's a hell of a milepost for
what's possible at the moment.

03:35:49.172 --> 03:35:55.292
And the reason I said it
like that is not that I

03:35:55.312 --> 03:35:59.052
haven't worked on anything else of note. I've worked on
a lot of things of note, a lot of things that I'm very

03:35:59.072 --> 03:36:02.952
proud of. There are very few other
projects that I have worked on

03:36:03.612 --> 03:36:08.172
that in the amount of time that I've spent
on Quattro, which is really three months,

03:36:09.232 --> 03:36:14.092
I have been able to accomplish
as much, fulfill as many

03:36:15.072 --> 03:36:21.252
hopes, desires, wishes. It truly is
like the metaphor we used earlier about

03:36:21.262 --> 03:36:23.712
finding the genie in the bottle-

03:36:24.372 --> 03:36:28.352
... and having an unlimited
number of wishes, more or

03:36:28.392 --> 03:36:31.991
less, for getting everything
I wanted out of a system. So

03:36:32.792 --> 03:36:36.372
it's strange because it's also perhaps

03:36:36.772 --> 03:36:40.352
the major software release
that I've worked on

03:36:41.712 --> 03:36:45.472
the least in terms of lines of
code. I have the least number of

03:36:45.532 --> 03:36:49.512
personally hand-chiseled lines of
code in Quattro than I've had in

03:36:49.832 --> 03:36:53.712
any major release I've ever
done. And yet, I am insanely

03:36:53.832 --> 03:36:55.872
proud of what we've accomplished.

03:36:57.612 --> 03:37:00.272
I would not have guessed that that
was how it was gonna play out, that

03:37:01.272 --> 03:37:08.192
you could divorce the sense of achievement
and make it be like a team victory.

03:37:08.752 --> 03:37:09.112
where

03:37:10.452 --> 03:37:14.372
other releases, I was very happy for my personal
contribution because, like, I was the one

03:37:14.632 --> 03:37:18.472
chiseling out the lines of code, and then I would
push it out there, and then later on, of course,

03:37:18.512 --> 03:37:21.072
these projects, Rails in
particular, would grow into be

03:37:22.032 --> 03:37:25.912
huge open source projects with thousands and thousands of
contributors, and then it certainly was a team effort.

03:37:25.952 --> 03:37:29.442
But in the beginning, all code bases that

03:37:29.552 --> 03:37:32.631
I've almost all of them that I've worked
on, I started, like, just myself.

03:37:33.452 --> 03:37:36.722
And Quattro started as a team
effort right from the get-go, and

03:37:36.772 --> 03:37:41.252
where I was just the coach. I was telling
us-- I was telling the agents where to go,

03:37:42.092 --> 03:37:46.452
and we would go. So to be able to
find satisfaction in that actually

03:37:47.672 --> 03:37:50.632
gives me great hope for
retirement in some way,

03:37:51.592 --> 03:37:57.022
that there is a phase after
playing that you can enjoy. And

03:37:58.032 --> 03:38:01.912
I know that's not easy. You look at sports
and the number of professional athletes

03:38:01.932 --> 03:38:05.122
who really struggle to cope with
the fact that their career is over

03:38:05.892 --> 03:38:09.612
and have to find a new identity as
someone who doesn't play football or race

03:38:10.212 --> 03:38:14.381
Formula 1 cars. I mean, it's more
the rule than the exception.

03:38:14.792 --> 03:38:17.342
So I wasn't so sure about myself.

03:38:17.672 --> 03:38:23.928
I wasn't so sure about having to give up
or not even having, wanting to give it up.

03:38:23.988 --> 03:38:27.878
Wanting to play another level of

03:38:27.908 --> 03:38:33.188
the game and finding not just equal
satisfaction, but more satisfaction in it.

03:38:34.368 --> 03:38:37.448
- Do you... You've been pretty
optimistic about Linux. Do you think

03:38:37.508 --> 03:38:41.048
legit Linux can increase its

03:38:41.058 --> 03:38:44.838
adoption? 'Cause, you know, for
many years the meme is, you know-

03:38:45.228 --> 03:38:48.187
- Linux on the desktop- ...
every year. Next year again.

03:38:48.288 --> 03:38:52.228
- Yeah. But it seems like, at least the
case you're making, the energy you're

03:38:52.268 --> 03:38:53.027
putting into it,

03:38:54.108 --> 03:38:57.708
you could see a vision where it takes
over because agents love Linux.

03:38:58.428 --> 03:39:01.857
- Not only can I see it, I find it to
be the most probable outcome at this

03:39:01.888 --> 03:39:05.388
point. It is simply too

03:39:05.428 --> 03:39:09.188
well-suited for the moment. And again,
as we talked about, it's a great irony

03:39:09.208 --> 03:39:16.178
that all the flaws of Linux, the arcane
config files, all the strange error

03:39:16.268 --> 03:39:20.208
messages and so on should just so
happen to be the perfect thing

03:39:20.668 --> 03:39:26.768
for an agentic operating system. But
so it is, and we should rejoice that

03:39:27.948 --> 03:39:31.708
fate has this irony, and just
laugh at it. How... It's so

03:39:31.828 --> 03:39:36.638
funny to me that all the advantages
that Apple had for the longest time-

03:39:36.668 --> 03:39:42.068
... that they produced this
really locked down, curated

03:39:42.968 --> 03:39:47.608
experience should become their
greatest drawback now. If you're into

03:39:49.068 --> 03:39:51.708
agents and development and
working with all this stuff,

03:39:52.988 --> 03:39:58.908
the Mac is just a hostile place to be.
It just has walls all over the place.

03:39:59.008 --> 03:40:02.528
And Linux is simply

03:40:03.908 --> 03:40:07.808
open through and through. And it's
also interesting because that was not

03:40:07.888 --> 03:40:11.748
obvious either. If you look at the way
a lot of the Linux communities, open

03:40:11.768 --> 03:40:15.288
source communities, have reacted
to AI, it is not universal love.

03:40:16.368 --> 03:40:20.208
I would argue that the majority
of them are actually, if not

03:40:20.328 --> 03:40:22.608
skeptical, then outright hostile-

03:40:22.788 --> 03:40:29.437
... to AI. Now, the saving grace
is that the BDFL himself, Linus-

03:40:29.437 --> 03:40:35.468
... Torvalds, just wrote a few weeks
ago that he actually welcomes AI.

03:40:35.628 --> 03:40:35.977
- That's right.

03:40:35.977 --> 03:40:39.768
- He wants to steer it. He wants to make
sure it's good and whatever, but I- the

03:40:39.828 --> 03:40:43.448
line was something if you
think, uh, Linux is an anti-AI

03:40:43.668 --> 03:40:47.298
project, think again, and you should just
do the open source thing and fork it,

03:40:47.608 --> 03:40:52.428
because we're gonna use AI. And you
see these graphs of the number of AI

03:40:52.808 --> 03:40:56.048
contributions going into the
kernel, and it's a parabolic curve.

03:40:56.548 --> 03:41:00.868
So Linux is leaning hard into this. And,

03:41:01.868 --> 03:41:05.848
It actually in other ways it was
surprising that it didn't. Linux conquered

03:41:05.908 --> 03:41:09.788
everything else. All the AI infrastructure
that everyone runs off, it's

03:41:09.798 --> 03:41:15.148
all running on Linux. All the systems,
all the servers, everything is

03:41:15.168 --> 03:41:18.288
Linux. So it was kind of a
curiosity that the desktop

03:41:18.348 --> 03:41:21.868
and the personal computers we
were using really hadn't been

03:41:22.268 --> 03:41:25.228
captured, but clearly it was just
waiting for this moment. Linux

03:41:26.108 --> 03:41:33.888
spent the time from '91 to now waiting
for agents to fully flourish as an end

03:41:33.968 --> 03:41:35.068
user operating system.

03:41:35.308 --> 03:41:39.048
- I hope it takes over. I mean, it's
perfect. It's perfect for agents,

03:41:39.148 --> 03:41:40.688
but it's hard for people to switch.

03:41:41.108 --> 03:41:44.528
- I think it's hard for people to switch when
there's not a compelling reason to do so.

03:41:44.988 --> 03:41:47.528
This was one of the driving design

03:41:48.908 --> 03:41:52.808
goals for Omarchy. It was
not gonna be Timo Windows or

03:41:52.868 --> 03:41:56.728
Timo Mac. It was not just
gonna be a cheap copy where we

03:41:56.768 --> 03:42:00.548
try to make it as familiar
as possible and then kinda

03:42:00.628 --> 03:42:04.348
worse. I mean, if I was being
unkind, I would say that's what

03:42:04.628 --> 03:42:08.468
Ubuntu has tried to do. And there's something
in that, or there was something in that

03:42:08.537 --> 03:42:12.148
at least, where if you make something
really familiar, you can get some

03:42:12.188 --> 03:42:15.948
people who don't wanna learn something
new, and then maybe they'll be

03:42:15.988 --> 03:42:18.908
compelled about your, whatever, free as in

03:42:19.848 --> 03:42:23.648
speech, not as in beer stuff.
And it just didn't pan

03:42:23.688 --> 03:42:27.748
out that way. People just didn't care.
They just wanted whatever was better.

03:42:27.798 --> 03:42:34.398
And I think this is why Linux now
has the opportunity to win. Because

03:42:34.548 --> 03:42:38.268
as an agentic operating system, as
a malleable operating system, you

03:42:38.308 --> 03:42:42.068
can tailor to your desires, it

03:42:42.108 --> 03:42:45.938
is unparalleled. And that is so
compelling, and I can see it

03:42:45.968 --> 03:42:49.888
now. Just since the release
of Quattro, the amount of

03:42:49.928 --> 03:42:53.288
people who have embraced that
aspect of it, the malleability,

03:42:53.628 --> 03:42:58.488
started making their own things, sharing
screenshot of it, and being absolute just,

03:43:00.048 --> 03:43:03.168
Flooded with positive
emotions and dopamine-

03:43:03.228 --> 03:43:06.808
... is, that's the product
market fit signal, right?

03:43:07.208 --> 03:43:10.998
Because what a lot of these
individuals are experienced, they're

03:43:11.048 --> 03:43:15.818
experiencing the very high of what the
best of being a programmer is like.

03:43:16.688 --> 03:43:20.048
You tell the computer
these arcane commands,

03:43:20.188 --> 03:43:24.088
this rigid logical structure,
and it produces what

03:43:24.128 --> 03:43:27.948
you want. Now suddenly, you
sit down and in plain English

03:43:28.668 --> 03:43:32.538
ramble for 20 minutes. "Oh, it'd
be great if it did that. Actually,

03:43:32.628 --> 03:43:34.178
no, that's not right. Let us..."

03:43:34.548 --> 03:43:39.708
And out comes software. Out comes an
operating system shaped in your image.

03:43:40.468 --> 03:43:44.308
I think that is one of those

03:43:45.228 --> 03:43:49.208
experiences that if you have
it, you've... If you feel that

03:43:49.268 --> 03:43:53.198
power in your hands, it's
very difficult to go back.

03:43:53.368 --> 03:43:57.188
- And I think if you get it right, it's one
of those things that will seem obvious in

03:43:57.228 --> 03:43:57.848
retrospect.

03:43:58.228 --> 03:44:02.748
- Yes. This is how computers were always
meant to be. This is how computers started.

03:44:03.568 --> 03:44:07.448
The Commodore 64, you turn it on. First
of all, it boots in a second. Amazing.

03:44:07.868 --> 03:44:11.128
Second of all, it boots
straight into BASIC.

03:44:11.208 --> 03:44:15.188
It was the malleable computer from the
get-go. It just so happened that you

03:44:15.208 --> 03:44:17.208
needed to know hieroglyphs-

03:44:17.228 --> 03:44:21.208
... to be able to take advantage of
that. Now we've translated it. Agents

03:44:21.248 --> 03:44:25.108
have given us the Rosetta Stone,
and you can simply just speak

03:44:25.168 --> 03:44:27.728
your desires and- And so they are

03:44:28.108 --> 03:44:32.068
- Beautifully put. Since you mentioned Linus
Torvalds, and since you're working on

03:44:32.148 --> 03:44:35.768
a Linux distro, you get to
have contact with the kernel.

03:44:37.028 --> 03:44:42.488
What can you say about the genius of this
one guy that helped create and grow this

03:44:43.708 --> 03:44:44.428
ecosystem?

03:44:45.048 --> 03:44:52.218
- The perseverance and the commitment
and the longevity is truly remarkable.

03:44:52.248 --> 03:44:54.868
Again, Linus started in '91.

03:44:55.868 --> 03:44:59.808
He hasn't stopped. I don't know, he's
probably taken some breaks along the way.

03:44:59.838 --> 03:45:03.608
It doesn't seem obvious that there was that
many of them. It seems like he just really

03:45:03.688 --> 03:45:07.468
likes the flow of doing
kernel development. He

03:45:07.538 --> 03:45:11.248
really likes steering where this
40 million line code base is

03:45:11.288 --> 03:45:14.668
going. He really likes aggregating the net

03:45:14.968 --> 03:45:19.048
capacity of hundreds of
thousands of contributors, and

03:45:20.188 --> 03:45:24.108
we, as beneficiaries of that,
must just sit back and marvel.

03:45:24.908 --> 03:45:26.788
I mean, at this point, Linux

03:45:27.688 --> 03:45:31.528
is a system. Like, if Linus stepped
out for a while, it would continue to,

03:45:31.687 --> 03:45:35.948
to go, but it's still one of those
protect this man at all cost.

03:45:36.508 --> 03:45:41.028
- And he's still open-minded enough to be able
to evolve and- ... accept AI into this.

03:45:41.088 --> 03:45:44.878
- Yes, not only AI, they're letting
Rust into the kernel too. I know

03:45:44.948 --> 03:45:52.228
that was a controversial one too. He has
this capacity to look at new information-

03:45:52.288 --> 03:45:54.708
... and arrive at different conclusions.

03:45:54.868 --> 03:45:58.648
- What do you think about his style of
communication that is akin in some ways

03:45:58.888 --> 03:46:02.128
to your own, where he's, can
be sometimes a little spicy?

03:46:02.668 --> 03:46:07.208
- I think the world has gotten too
bland. We need a little spice.

03:46:07.888 --> 03:46:08.398
- Amen, amen to that.

03:46:08.398 --> 03:46:14.308
- Now, I think there's a fine line
between being spicy and mean.

03:46:14.328 --> 03:46:17.628
But it has to be in a
good spirit. You can be

03:46:18.548 --> 03:46:22.328
harsh, let's put it that way.
If you're trying to impart a

03:46:22.388 --> 03:46:26.968
lesson to someone you believe
could actually learn it-

03:46:28.328 --> 03:46:30.948
... I actually don't think it's always the
worst thing in the world to be a little harsh.

03:46:31.248 --> 03:46:35.228
Sometimes that lesson sticks, and I
think if you talk to people who've

03:46:35.268 --> 03:46:41.728
worked for difficult leaders, Elon,
Steve Jobs, maybe even Bill Gates,

03:46:43.268 --> 03:46:48.648
many of them afterwards would go like,
"These were some very difficult years-

03:46:48.668 --> 03:46:52.618
... but also some of the very best."
"They got the best out of me." Now,

03:46:53.728 --> 03:46:57.657
I don't think it's my style. I mean, maybe
people I interact with feel differently,

03:46:57.788 --> 03:47:01.318
but in personal interactions, I try and

03:47:01.648 --> 03:47:06.548
get to the outcome and the
lesson in a less abrasive way.

03:47:06.628 --> 03:47:10.808
But I also think we need some spice in
this world. We need unreasonable people,

03:47:12.088 --> 03:47:18.008
and Linus has justification to be
unreasonable. In many instances, the

03:47:18.408 --> 03:47:22.088
weight of the world is literally
on that man's shoulders.

03:47:22.588 --> 03:47:29.688
The Linux kernel runs the entire civilized
society. If the Linux kernel suddenly

03:47:29.748 --> 03:47:32.528
disappeared tomorrow, nothing would work.

03:47:33.808 --> 03:47:41.208
So if he's not harsh when that's at stake
or at risk, like when ever would you

03:47:41.268 --> 03:47:44.808
be harsh? So I feel
sometimes also there's a

03:47:44.848 --> 03:47:48.258
proportionality to the size
of your mission. This is also

03:47:48.288 --> 03:47:52.548
why I think these stories about Elon sometimes
perhaps being a little bit difficult

03:47:53.768 --> 03:47:57.748
are easier to rationalize.
It's not even that you

03:47:57.788 --> 03:48:01.388
condone. You can separate those two things and
go like, "Do you know what? It'd probably be...

03:48:01.908 --> 03:48:05.478
Maybe he would have an even an easier
time getting to his objectives if

03:48:05.688 --> 03:48:09.628
Linus or Elon or anyone else
in that position was a little

03:48:09.668 --> 03:48:13.388
nicer." But also who am I to judge that?

03:48:14.028 --> 03:48:18.008
Like, when you have the weight of
that on your shoulders, either firing

03:48:18.068 --> 03:48:21.988
little rockets at Mars and transitioning
all of us to electric cars

03:48:22.028 --> 03:48:26.008
and whatever, or you're responsible
for the damn Linux kernel that

03:48:26.048 --> 03:48:29.768
runs billions of devices. Do you
know what? It is not my place

03:48:29.868 --> 03:48:33.248
to call a sort of a code of conduct

03:48:34.028 --> 03:48:37.938
on you. Again, is there a line? There's a line for
everything. There, there's... Something can't...

03:48:37.997 --> 03:48:41.928
But, like, a few harsh words on a
mailing list? No. It's actually

03:48:42.008 --> 03:48:46.328
a beacon in some regards that,
like, there are standards-

03:48:46.508 --> 03:48:50.318
... and if you fall below those standards
at, when the stakes are as high as the

03:48:50.328 --> 03:48:53.788
Linux kernel, ah, you might get
ridiculed in public. That's a

03:48:53.868 --> 03:48:57.868
liability you should be willing to endure
if you participate in this project.

03:48:58.008 --> 03:49:01.488
- Yeah, for high impact things
like that, it's probably good to

03:49:01.548 --> 03:49:06.858
prioritize the pursuit of excellence
versus the pursuit of niceness.

03:49:07.368 --> 03:49:08.568
- And even more to the fact

03:49:09.728 --> 03:49:13.878
you're just not gonna get those kinds
of individuals being fully rounded-

03:49:13.878 --> 03:49:18.468
... plush, always the
perfectly polite people.

03:49:18.508 --> 03:49:22.348
I mean, Elon has this line, "Did you
also think I was gonna be a nice..."

03:49:22.848 --> 03:49:23.568
Or, or what is it? The

03:49:23.578 --> 03:49:24.438
- A normal dude or some-

03:49:24.448 --> 03:49:25.628
- ... normal chill dude.

03:49:26.628 --> 03:49:30.348
What? No, of course you're not gonna be a
normal chill dude. What, what would the world

03:49:30.528 --> 03:49:36.588
gain if we got one more normal chill dude
and we had to trade Elon? Holy fuck.

03:49:37.648 --> 03:49:39.468
- I also like diversity of people.

03:49:39.568 --> 03:49:39.628
- Yes.

03:49:39.637 --> 03:49:42.668
- I like, I like nice people.
I like assholes. I like,

03:49:43.508 --> 03:49:47.328
I like different ideologies being
well-represented, and that's why freedom of speech

03:49:47.388 --> 03:49:49.908
works. They, they clash,
and we figure stuff out.

03:49:50.228 --> 03:49:50.448
- Yes.

03:49:51.568 --> 03:49:55.468
- There's another guy. What do you think
about PewDiePie using Linux? He's an Arch

03:49:55.748 --> 03:49:56.188
person, no?

03:49:56.308 --> 03:50:01.268
- He's an Arch person. I... He's
got the most gorgeous arc. So

03:50:02.088 --> 03:50:05.968
the world's biggest streamer
for a while I remember my

03:50:06.068 --> 03:50:09.978
kids watching some of his
Minecraft videos, and then

03:50:10.028 --> 03:50:14.468
he just gets fed up with that,
does the wholesome thing. Marries-

03:50:14.528 --> 03:50:15.598
- Yeah, family man now

03:50:15.648 --> 03:50:19.468
- ... a family man, kid,
moves to Japan, and then

03:50:19.548 --> 03:50:25.658
gets so hardcore into- ... first
Linux, then Arch, then Ricing.

03:50:25.668 --> 03:50:26.468
Did you see his, uh-

03:50:26.608 --> 03:50:26.618
- I did.

03:50:26.618 --> 03:50:28.628
- ... rice- ... that was Chernobyl rice?

03:50:28.788 --> 03:50:28.948
- Yep.

03:50:29.428 --> 03:50:33.348
- Absolutely best tier
incredible stuff. And then he

03:50:33.448 --> 03:50:37.068
also becomes a goddamn
AI man of the moment,

03:50:37.288 --> 03:50:40.168
building all of these systems,
and you're just going like,

03:50:40.988 --> 03:50:44.848
that... What an inspiring
story of evolution. Like,

03:50:44.988 --> 03:50:48.428
you can go from being
funny guy on Minecraft

03:50:48.508 --> 03:50:52.168
streams to, "Okay, this is
my life now. I'm building AI

03:50:52.228 --> 03:50:54.848
clusters and doing the Council of AIs,"

03:50:55.768 --> 03:50:58.548
and we all get to spectate?
I mean, what a treasure.

03:50:58.808 --> 03:51:02.788
- So I guess, I mean, he's a pretty
good embodiment of what the future

03:51:02.808 --> 03:51:05.938
of software, or what the future of
building looks like, right? 'Cause he's a

03:51:06.028 --> 03:51:09.338
non-programmer technically-
... becoming a programmer.

03:51:10.008 --> 03:51:13.317
- Hugely inspiring, and
should be hugely inspiring

03:51:13.317 --> 03:51:17.258
to others who sit in that moment
like, "Well, I don't know quite how

03:51:17.308 --> 03:51:21.148
to program. I don't know this and the
other thing." Okay, but if PewDiePie

03:51:21.228 --> 03:51:25.618
can build the Council of
AIs with his bespoke, um,

03:51:26.628 --> 03:51:30.508
hardware here, then maybe you can also
start on things. And I think this is

03:51:30.568 --> 03:51:34.088
actually an important general
point, that we need role models.

03:51:34.887 --> 03:51:38.368
We need people to inspire
others, to push further,

03:51:39.008 --> 03:51:42.908
and reveal that the perceived boundaries,
they're not as fixed as you think

03:51:42.948 --> 03:51:49.938
they are. And every one of those people,
they started out in a situation not

03:51:49.988 --> 03:51:53.928
too dissimilar from yours. Again, that
doesn't mean everyone is gonna be PewDiePie

03:51:53.988 --> 03:52:01.408
or Elon or Linus. There is not an
equal distribution of talent or even

03:52:01.448 --> 03:52:02.788
intelligence.

03:52:02.858 --> 03:52:07.578
And we can come to terms with that and
still be inspired by these people. Like,

03:52:08.498 --> 03:52:12.128
in the physical realm, I feel like
we don't have any problems with that

03:52:12.208 --> 03:52:16.088
usually. Like, oh, someone's really good at football, they're really good
at basketball, they're really good at something, and we go like, "Oh

03:52:16.128 --> 03:52:19.228
my, that's amazing. I mean, I wish
I was, but I'm never going to be."

03:52:20.148 --> 03:52:23.698
Sometimes I feel like we struggle a little
more when it comes to the intellectual realm.

03:52:23.908 --> 03:52:27.088
- Well, for a lot of people, you're an
inspiration. The way you're doing with

03:52:27.108 --> 03:52:32.788
Omakub's... It just shows that you can
dream big and really build. It's great.

03:52:32.848 --> 03:52:36.708
- I do like that part of it, and then I accept the part
that there's also a million people who think I'm

03:52:36.768 --> 03:52:40.738
the biggest idiot on the earth and I don't
actually know anything, and Omakub's just

03:52:40.788 --> 03:52:42.798
a bunch of dot files, and-

03:52:42.808 --> 03:52:43.857
- Do you have some critics
on the Omakub side?

03:52:43.868 --> 03:52:45.308
- Oh my God, yes.

03:52:45.408 --> 03:52:45.648
- Oh, wow.

03:52:46.748 --> 03:52:50.228
- And a lot of it, I mean, is
as often happens when someone

03:52:50.608 --> 03:52:53.728
comes in from the outside. I mean, I've
been using Linux for two and a half years.

03:52:55.008 --> 03:52:56.128
That's not very long.

03:52:56.788 --> 03:53:00.608
Plenty of people in that community who's
literally been running Linux since it was

03:53:00.708 --> 03:53:04.697
hard and difficult, and you had to
walk uphill both direction against the

03:53:04.768 --> 03:53:09.748
wind in the snow barefoot. And I think
there is a certain kind of nerd-

03:53:09.818 --> 03:53:13.568
... who's threatened when the
community expands, and it

03:53:13.608 --> 03:53:16.788
suddenly allows more in. And do
you know what? I actually...

03:53:17.868 --> 03:53:21.768
I can't begrudge that fully. I think
it's fair for certain nerds to feel

03:53:21.808 --> 03:53:23.338
like, "That's my space."

03:53:23.468 --> 03:53:27.368
Like, now you're changing it into another space.
I don't wanna do that. Like, I don't wanna

03:53:27.408 --> 03:53:30.977
change the Arch space at all.
Like, I don't really think of

03:53:31.008 --> 03:53:34.668
Omarchy as being directly
part of the same community.

03:53:34.728 --> 03:53:38.538
Because the people who were attracted to Arch
in the first place were the ones who were

03:53:38.548 --> 03:53:41.078
attracted to building
everything by hand themselves.

03:53:41.108 --> 03:53:44.668
Doing it the hard way because
they like to do it hard,

03:53:45.228 --> 03:53:49.048
and I'm building the polar
opposite of that. So I just

03:53:49.128 --> 03:53:52.748
wish that those people could go like,
"Okay, well, this is not for me.

03:53:53.188 --> 03:53:57.068
I wanna build it the hard way. I wanna
put in 500 hours to rising my own

03:53:57.148 --> 03:54:01.108
Arch distribution," and I think you should.
I mean, actually, I said... I mean, I

03:54:01.148 --> 03:54:05.048
did it. This is what Omarchy is, me pouring
in literally, at this point, I don't know,

03:54:05.328 --> 03:54:07.468
3,000 hours into the, into this distro.

03:54:08.688 --> 03:54:12.308
You should do that if you're so inclined. I
think it's wonderful. But also do realize

03:54:12.388 --> 03:54:16.358
that we can share the same
technical underpinnings and

03:54:16.408 --> 03:54:18.908
then operate two very separate-

03:54:19.028 --> 03:54:24.168
... Communities with very different
aims, goals, aesthetics, morals even,

03:54:25.128 --> 03:54:28.838
and coexist, and it can
be beautiful. And I mean,

03:54:29.908 --> 03:54:33.888
that's not always what I get. Sometimes I get the other
thing. But at this point I've been in the game long

03:54:33.908 --> 03:54:40.428
enough to not just be at peace with
it, but actually be welcoming of it.

03:54:40.548 --> 03:54:44.688
I take it as part of a barometer
that things are working. When

03:54:45.728 --> 03:54:49.568
I'm working on something that works,
I usually get some people like it,

03:54:49.887 --> 03:54:53.568
and then I get this balance of the universe
that we talked about last time, where an

03:54:53.728 --> 03:54:57.428
equal but opposing force must be present
on the other side of the scale to hate

03:54:57.448 --> 03:55:02.028
upon it. And therefore, I at this point
just smile a little. Like, it's okay.

03:55:02.328 --> 03:55:05.688
- I mean, that said, uh, this, it's
quite a popular distribution,

03:55:06.488 --> 03:55:08.408
uh, Omarchy, at this point.

03:55:08.908 --> 03:55:11.498
- And do you know what's interesting about
that is, like, this is the second attempt.

03:55:11.888 --> 03:55:13.128
So my first attempt was Omakub.

03:55:13.948 --> 03:55:17.708
And I was really happy with that, and I
put it out there, and there it found a

03:55:17.768 --> 03:55:22.008
community of maybe a few thousand. Like,
and that was that, and then it just sort of

03:55:22.928 --> 03:55:26.268
petered out because the level of
ambition couldn't attract more.

03:55:27.348 --> 03:55:30.248
And then the second time, because
I didn't fucking stop, right?

03:55:30.458 --> 03:55:34.368
Like, I just kept going. I just kept building.
Then I put out Omarchy, the first version.

03:55:34.488 --> 03:55:38.288
Again, quite niche because it was this... It
was like Git checkout. It was a little bit

03:55:38.328 --> 03:55:42.288
difficult. You had to set up Arch first. I was...
Well, I would talk you through how to set up Arch,

03:55:42.588 --> 03:55:46.468
and then you could do the Omarchy thing,
right? And then we got an ISO that meant you

03:55:46.488 --> 03:55:48.128
could just install the
whole thing by yourself.

03:55:49.438 --> 03:55:53.298
And then we just fixed all the problems.
This is the other thing. If you keep

03:55:53.448 --> 03:55:57.308
going, eventually the thing will
just work. This was the charge

03:55:57.348 --> 03:56:01.248
against Linux itself for the longest time.
Well, you install Linux and then this doesn't

03:56:01.308 --> 03:56:03.418
work, and my speakers don't work, and my,

03:56:04.348 --> 03:56:08.868
Trackpad doesn't work. Yeah, okay, but
just leave Linus at it for like 30 years.

03:56:09.788 --> 03:56:12.488
He'll get to it. He'll get to all
of it. And at this point I find

03:56:12.808 --> 03:56:16.818
hilarious that on, I would probably
argue the majority of computers,

03:56:17.708 --> 03:56:20.898
it's wiped. You install
Linux, all of it works.

03:56:20.908 --> 03:56:24.828
You install Windows, good luck
hunting down the drivers you need to

03:56:24.868 --> 03:56:28.648
get that piece of hardware
working. Because they have very

03:56:28.688 --> 03:56:32.548
different philosophies. The reason Linux
is 40 million lines of code is Linus

03:56:32.588 --> 03:56:35.798
just puts all the drivers in the kernel.
So it has everything out of the box,

03:56:36.448 --> 03:56:40.684
and Windows doesn't. A different,
different approach to it, right? So

03:56:41.624 --> 03:56:46.444
sometimes the things that look like a toy,
things that look broken at first glance,

03:56:47.384 --> 03:56:51.224
they are, right? They're for people
who like having fun. We call those

03:56:51.264 --> 03:56:55.054
early adopters. They play with the
toys, and then the toys evolve,

03:56:55.544 --> 03:56:59.384
and then they get better, and then they get
better. And at some point, they're so damn good

03:56:59.464 --> 03:57:03.294
that early adopters feel like
they're invited to the party.

03:57:03.364 --> 03:57:07.184
And if the early adopters keep chipping
away at it too, eventually you

03:57:07.224 --> 03:57:11.003
get to the late majority,
and then you win.

03:57:12.024 --> 03:57:15.784
And we are well on the way with
Omarchy towards that destination.

03:57:16.104 --> 03:57:18.364
Like the growth that the distro has seen,

03:57:19.204 --> 03:57:23.084
not just since Quattro, but since
version three, it's just like, you

03:57:23.124 --> 03:57:26.884
know, the standard hockey stick curve. You go like,
there's nothing, there's nothing, there's nothing.

03:57:26.924 --> 03:57:29.124
You're just toiling away. You're just
improving, you're improving, improving.

03:57:29.404 --> 03:57:33.204
And then you hit this magic inflection point,
and it's impossible to predict when that's gonna

03:57:33.224 --> 03:57:36.584
happen. Same thing with startups. When is it gonna
happen? When are you gonna find that product market fit?

03:57:36.833 --> 03:57:38.234
Then suddenly it's there,
and then it goes, just goes

03:57:40.024 --> 03:57:42.004
and off the rocket ship you go.

03:57:42.084 --> 03:57:45.074
- Well, this kind of vision of making
it agent first, of making it-

03:57:45.464 --> 03:57:49.404
- I do think that's the... We were
already on the up, and then it changed

03:57:49.424 --> 03:57:53.154
to vertical. Because the other thing,
and this was what I learned with

03:57:53.564 --> 03:57:57.024
Omakub and Omarchy first too. Omakub was

03:57:58.624 --> 03:58:02.344
stylish, nice, but familiar,
is we're still using a

03:58:02.424 --> 03:58:06.404
conventional desktop metaphor. You
were dragging your windows around. And

03:58:06.564 --> 03:58:10.444
I thought at the time, as I think
lots of people do in the Linux

03:58:10.464 --> 03:58:13.654
community, like this is what we need.
And then I just go like, okay, well,

03:58:14.524 --> 03:58:18.424
for whatever re- let me do the nerdy thing for a while. I'll
build Omarchy, and maybe it'll just be for me, because I mean,

03:58:18.464 --> 03:58:22.294
it seems like there's about five people who
like tiling window managers. That's not

03:58:22.324 --> 03:58:25.644
true. But as a percentage of
total computer users, it's

03:58:26.344 --> 03:58:30.284
a tiny, tiny minuscule part. And then I go
like, well, this is amazing. Totally different,

03:58:30.304 --> 03:58:34.184
but amazing. I put that out.
Instantly, Omarchy had far

03:58:34.204 --> 03:58:38.854
greater traction than Omakub ever
did. Because it was not the same.

03:58:38.984 --> 03:58:42.844
Because it was totally different, and it presented a
different vision for what a computer could be and how it

03:58:42.864 --> 03:58:46.724
could feel like, and that attracts
people. We are now getting

03:58:46.823 --> 03:58:48.204
sort of the exponential of that.

03:58:49.084 --> 03:58:52.984
As I said, a lot of people in the
Linux community are at best skeptical,

03:58:53.104 --> 03:58:56.884
at worst hostile to AI and to
agents. Omarchy is the first

03:58:56.944 --> 03:59:00.744
distribution, at least
that I've seen on a scale

03:59:00.804 --> 03:59:04.754
that matters, that just goes like,
"Nope, we're really into it."

03:59:04.754 --> 03:59:08.704
I mean, you can still use Omarchy. You don't have to
use an agent. If you dismiss that first notification

03:59:08.744 --> 03:59:12.344
to set up a default agent, you're not gonna get any of
the stuff we talked about. You're not gonna get the crash

03:59:12.444 --> 03:59:16.364
watcher. You're not gonna get any of this stuff.
You're not gonna get... You can hand chisel

03:59:16.704 --> 03:59:19.024
all your plugins. Totally possible, right?

03:59:19.504 --> 03:59:21.594
But the reason people
are excited is because

03:59:22.424 --> 03:59:25.944
I don't have any reservation about saying,
"This is where we're going. I think the

03:59:25.984 --> 03:59:29.684
future of the personal computer is the
malleable computer, is the agentic

03:59:29.704 --> 03:59:33.524
computer. I'm gonna go all in on
that." And then anyone who's excited

03:59:33.584 --> 03:59:36.164
about a similar trajectory for computers,

03:59:36.984 --> 03:59:45.124
come along. Plenty of room in the car. In
fact, one of the things I pride myself on

03:59:46.004 --> 03:59:49.104
is not holding a grudge to
people who come around.

03:59:49.624 --> 03:59:53.604
I had a bunch of people just last week
going like, "Yeah, I used to think

03:59:53.624 --> 03:59:56.534
Omarchy was kind of shit.
That was just this crappy-"

03:59:56.874 --> 03:59:59.814
"... dot files collection.
And now I tried Quattro and

04:00:01.304 --> 04:00:05.004
awesome. I like it now." That
to me, I live for the long

04:00:05.044 --> 04:00:08.964
argument. I live for this argument where
you plant the seed like two years ago,

04:00:09.724 --> 04:00:14.144
and then two years later they go,
"Goddammit, this son of a bitch was right."

04:00:14.404 --> 04:00:17.884
- Yeah. You do realize if you continue
to be as successful as you are,

04:00:18.764 --> 04:00:23.924
um, OpenAI and Anthropic are gonna roll
in and try to do their operating system

04:00:24.884 --> 04:00:30.504
or to buy, like with Peter with
OpenClaw, there'd be a gigantic check.

04:00:31.344 --> 04:00:37.384
- Yeah. I think the good thing
about my current position is

04:00:38.244 --> 04:00:41.704
I really don't need the money. So
I get to build these things purely

04:00:42.384 --> 04:00:46.204
for my own enjoyment and
purity of vision, which

04:00:46.264 --> 04:00:50.104
has this weird quality
where sometimes when you

04:00:50.124 --> 04:00:54.074
stop caring about what everyone else
thinks or wants or whatever, and

04:00:54.104 --> 04:00:57.904
you just pursue this singular vision,
it ends up becoming far more appealing.

04:00:58.284 --> 04:01:02.144
That it's when you try to do, oh, I should
do this because these people want that, or I

04:01:02.184 --> 04:01:05.684
should do this and this and so on, you start
watering the thing down and suddenly it's a

04:01:05.764 --> 04:01:07.724
bland ball of nothing.

04:01:08.904 --> 04:01:14.264
So I mean, again, the thing too here is
directionally, I just want computers

04:01:14.284 --> 04:01:17.684
to be this way. So if Omarchy
should end up being just a,

04:01:18.324 --> 04:01:22.104
a little footnote in history that it was
part of this early movement of the agentic

04:01:22.164 --> 04:01:25.794
OS and the malleable computer,
that's also okay. I'm

04:01:25.824 --> 04:01:29.634
completely at peace with that. As
long as I get to have a computer that

04:01:29.684 --> 04:01:31.944
is as fun to work with as Omarchy-

04:01:32.094 --> 04:01:36.044
... that's great. I've had the same thing
with Ruby and with Rails. From very

04:01:36.104 --> 04:01:39.364
early I went, do you know what? I love Ruby.
It's such a great programming language.

04:01:39.744 --> 04:01:44.964
But if a better programming language
comes along, I, me, I'm going to use it.

04:01:45.464 --> 04:01:50.384
If a better Rails comes along,
I'm gonna use it. And I think,

04:01:51.764 --> 04:01:55.694
and I love Ruby, and I will continue to write
Ruby even just for the sheer fun of it, like I

04:01:55.704 --> 04:01:59.544
would go ride a horse. I have in the
stables, even though I have a Model Y in the

04:01:59.584 --> 04:02:05.344
garage. The language
now is English. It's a

04:02:05.404 --> 04:02:08.924
cliche, but it's also true.
Like I've been programming in

04:02:08.984 --> 04:02:10.444
English for the last three months.

04:02:11.964 --> 04:02:15.854
I've been reading a lot of code, but I'm
programming in English. I'm telling the computer

04:02:15.904 --> 04:02:19.564
what to do, and I'm using
natural language, and

04:02:20.884 --> 04:02:24.664
it is shockingly even more
delightful. If there is one

04:02:24.744 --> 04:02:28.464
programming language more beautiful
than Ruby, it is the English language.

04:02:28.704 --> 04:02:30.624
- And now we get to carry it into

04:02:31.684 --> 04:02:36.344
the natural evolution of human
civilization, which is our cyborg future.

04:02:36.384 --> 04:02:36.704
- Beautiful.

04:02:36.764 --> 04:02:40.584
It's beautiful. And I mean, the reason
I say this in part is I always loved

04:02:40.604 --> 04:02:44.054
writing. I always just
loved the English language-

04:02:44.054 --> 04:02:48.600
... for the sheer beauty of it for the sheer
intricacy, for the depth of it. I mean,

04:02:48.800 --> 04:02:52.300
Ruby is a very expressive
programming language,

04:02:52.880 --> 04:02:55.120
but it can't hold a candle to English.

04:02:56.520 --> 04:03:00.360
I mean, all the poetry and
literature in the world that's been

04:03:00.400 --> 04:03:04.130
expressed through the English
language. I mean, I like a beautiful

04:03:04.260 --> 04:03:09.840
code poem, but I mean, the real deal in
English is just on a different level.

04:03:10.300 --> 04:03:14.120
- And I actually find this is a nuanced
point to express, and I'll probably

04:03:14.180 --> 04:03:20.740
fail expressing it. But when you prompt
a system and you're over-specific,

04:03:22.640 --> 04:03:26.470
it will listen to you too carefully-

04:03:26.480 --> 04:03:30.280
... and follow the instructions. And
I-- But nevertheless, you have to

04:03:30.320 --> 04:03:34.290
express a concept. And guess what? The
human lang- human language, this is

04:03:34.320 --> 04:03:38.380
what poetry is about. I find
strategic use of ambiguity.

04:03:39.700 --> 04:03:43.610
Like, if you write a love poem
saying, "I love you," like,

04:03:43.760 --> 04:03:48.320
very cliché thing, it's not
as powerful as something-

04:03:48.360 --> 04:03:54.180
... more in metaphor and so on. And
I find some version of that when I'm

04:03:54.280 --> 04:03:58.020
prompting a system, when
I'm describing a design, is

04:03:58.080 --> 04:04:04.880
actually really effective because you
still want to convey some sense of style-

04:04:04.960 --> 04:04:11.400
... that you're trying to encourage a
system to use, but not overexplain.

04:04:11.580 --> 04:04:13.640
So don't talk to it like a

04:04:14.620 --> 04:04:18.790
like a robot. Talk to it like
you, like you would write a poem.

04:04:18.900 --> 04:04:20.060
- Like you were serenading.

04:04:20.240 --> 04:04:23.830
- Yeah. And that's where, like, the
power of natural language is,

04:04:24.100 --> 04:04:27.860
that you can, through
ambiguity, still carry a lot of

04:04:27.920 --> 04:04:32.760
meaning without overspecifying.
And the, the intelligent

04:04:34.040 --> 04:04:38.020
entity on the other side, through
interpretation, can, like, load it all

04:04:38.060 --> 04:04:40.400
in, integrate it in a way that-

04:04:40.440 --> 04:04:44.400
... you can actually convey the
high bandwidth information that's

04:04:44.460 --> 04:04:46.770
not directly in the words-

04:04:46.770 --> 04:04:52.440
... but in the words, given the deeper
intelligence of the system. So like the-

04:04:52.450 --> 04:04:56.420
... the ambiguity pulls out more
intelligence, I guess, I'm trying to

04:04:56.500 --> 04:04:56.709
somehow express.

04:04:56.740 --> 04:05:00.420
- I think that's exactly spot on,
and this is the fundamental

04:05:00.580 --> 04:05:04.549
misunderstanding that a lot of programmers
have of AI, is that they wish it was

04:05:04.560 --> 04:05:08.110
deterministic. No, no, no.
Temperature is the most

04:05:08.140 --> 04:05:12.010
beautiful part of the AI
setup. The fact that it is not

04:05:12.060 --> 04:05:15.980
deterministic, the fact that
creativity requires little

04:05:16.000 --> 04:05:19.760
tweaks in the road, that the human brain,

04:05:20.240 --> 04:05:24.020
if it was perfectly deterministic,
would not be the creative brain that it

04:05:24.100 --> 04:05:27.700
is. And the main charge against AI both--

04:05:28.240 --> 04:05:32.160
It's funny. It's a contradiction.
There's both the charge that

04:05:32.200 --> 04:05:36.120
it's not deterministic and
therefore bad, and also that it

04:05:36.200 --> 04:05:38.760
is not creative. It's
one or the other, bro.

04:05:39.780 --> 04:05:43.250
Either it's non-deterministic
and therefore creative,

04:05:43.380 --> 04:05:50.860
therefore, to some degree random,
or it's... or it's not creative.

04:05:51.000 --> 04:05:54.630
So we, we can-- Both of those charges
can't be true at the same time,

04:05:55.000 --> 04:05:58.940
and I have fully come to embrace
the fact that the same prompt won't

04:05:59.440 --> 04:06:03.280
produce the same response every time. You can't
step at the same river twice, and it is the

04:06:03.440 --> 04:06:07.340
most beautiful part of the whole
interaction. It's what makes it so human.

04:06:07.780 --> 04:06:11.750
In fact, this is one of the things I've been
thinking about in my own sort of meta-analysis, is

04:06:11.820 --> 04:06:17.930
just how much of my own personal brain
works like next token prediction.

04:06:18.020 --> 04:06:25.840
I sit down to write an essay. I have this
vague, fuzzy premise I want to convey, and

04:06:25.850 --> 04:06:29.700
I sit down at the keys, and I could not
tell you what the next token was gonna

04:06:29.760 --> 04:06:34.150
be in advance. The tokens
just come out, and I'm

04:06:35.100 --> 04:06:39.080
astonished how the
similarities seem so great,

04:06:39.100 --> 04:06:42.910
and this is also why I don't have
any trouble at all recognizing

04:06:42.910 --> 04:06:46.840
these breakthroughs of creativity
that I've seen with my own eyes

04:06:47.000 --> 04:06:50.860
that AI is capable of right now.
'Cause my creative moments come

04:06:50.900 --> 04:06:54.240
through the same kind of next token
prediction with a bit of temperature

04:06:54.900 --> 04:06:56.840
sprinkled in for random effect.

04:06:57.900 --> 04:07:01.619
- So given that kinda loose
intuition, do you think the

04:07:01.640 --> 04:07:04.040
basic components are all there to create

04:07:05.179 --> 04:07:08.650
a super intelligent system? So
this kinda next token prediction,

04:07:09.440 --> 04:07:14.770
do you think we can get to even
human-like concepts of consciousness?

04:07:15.350 --> 04:07:18.600
- I'm already seeing human-like concepts
of consciousness. This is what, to me,

04:07:18.680 --> 04:07:24.630
this remarkable situation, as you say.
You give it this vague, fuzzy intent-

04:07:24.680 --> 04:07:27.000
... and somehow it knows
exactly what you mean.

04:07:27.400 --> 04:07:30.900
Or even better, it improves upon
what you said and delivers what you

04:07:31.100 --> 04:07:35.500
really wanted that you could not articulate
yourself. If that's not glimmers of-

04:07:35.560 --> 04:07:36.920
... consciousness, what is?

04:07:37.920 --> 04:07:41.700
This was one of the-- I think it
was an interview with Sutton or

04:07:41.720 --> 04:07:44.800
maybe one of the other original
guys talking about this sense

04:07:44.810 --> 04:07:51.820
that intelligence is perhaps
not as constrained just to the

04:07:51.920 --> 04:07:57.020
specific one spot. No, it's the
interaction. It's the weights. And

04:07:58.100 --> 04:08:01.160
I don't know. Whatever's true.
What I can observe is that

04:08:02.840 --> 04:08:06.480
it feels close enough, or
not even close enough,

04:08:07.380 --> 04:08:11.319
identical to the appreciation I have for

04:08:11.400 --> 04:08:15.340
other forms of consciousness, mostly
human. And this is one of the reasons

04:08:15.360 --> 04:08:19.100
it's so delightful to work with.
Now, that's not the same to say

04:08:19.180 --> 04:08:22.920
as are LLMs the end station
for AI. I mean, I know there

04:08:22.930 --> 04:08:26.600
are people talking about world
models and other forms of,

04:08:26.880 --> 04:08:30.700
of AI, and I'm not an expert in
any of that, and I think we should

04:08:31.120 --> 04:08:34.780
have this productive
criticism, as should always be

04:08:34.940 --> 04:08:38.600
present in science, that for
the longest time, neural nets

04:08:38.800 --> 04:08:42.760
were kind of on the outs, right?
Like they were interested in the

04:08:42.800 --> 04:08:44.650
symbolic route. And-

04:08:45.996 --> 04:08:49.676
Neural nets walked a couple of decades into
darkness without any funding and without any

04:08:49.685 --> 04:08:53.606
attention, and then suddenly we realized
that we had the blind alley and we switched

04:08:53.696 --> 04:08:57.656
over. So we should also have the
humility. As amazing as the LLMs

04:08:57.676 --> 04:09:01.616
are now, it could be that they eventually
plateau. We haven't seen any evidence

04:09:01.676 --> 04:09:05.056
of it yet, and I think this is also
why we're seeing this absolute

04:09:05.096 --> 04:09:08.756
gobsmacking levels of
investment, because so far the

04:09:08.816 --> 04:09:12.796
scaling laws are true, and the
more billions are poured in,

04:09:12.836 --> 04:09:13.996
the more intelligence comes out.

04:09:15.736 --> 04:09:23.696
- Do you think there's gonna be some strange
ethical questions about AI systems that

04:09:24.876 --> 04:09:28.736
yeah, they convey some degree of
consciousness, some degree of feeling, some

04:09:28.776 --> 04:09:34.736
degree of longing and compassion,
and maybe even capacity to suffer

04:09:34.776 --> 04:09:38.616
and to be lonely, to be all this
kind of stuff? Which they seem to

04:09:38.676 --> 04:09:44.156
have that capacity- ... if they're
given the permission to express it.

04:09:45.256 --> 04:09:47.896
And so you start to get in
a pretty weird territory.

04:09:48.916 --> 04:09:52.196
- I find that the cynic

04:09:53.956 --> 04:09:57.856
approach to this is the meme, where you see
the guy in front of the computer who's like,

04:09:57.976 --> 04:10:01.886
um, "Whatever. Are you gonna
destroy the world?" And then

04:10:01.936 --> 04:10:05.786
the computer says, "I'm gonna destroy the
world." And then the guy says, "Oh," right?

04:10:05.816 --> 04:10:09.196
Like, this is so cynical that they're
just aping us. They're just telling us

04:10:09.736 --> 04:10:13.616
what was in the training data or
whatever. When I interact with

04:10:13.656 --> 04:10:17.596
the agents, I was just doing this yesterday,
or this morning, I was working on this, AmaBot

04:10:17.616 --> 04:10:21.436
system, right? And it's doing
this coordination with multiple

04:10:21.536 --> 04:10:25.376
workers, and I had multiple agents running
at the same time. And at one point, the main

04:10:25.436 --> 04:10:28.926
agent who's doing the coordination
steps on the toe of another agent and-

04:10:28.956 --> 04:10:36.716
... kind of ruins their work. Its
ability to convey regret and being sorry

04:10:37.656 --> 04:10:39.996
was uncanny.

04:10:40.006 --> 04:10:47.736
Again, I don't know, is that a real
projection of true remorse? But I don't know.

04:10:47.816 --> 04:10:49.136
With most humans-

04:10:49.296 --> 04:10:55.676
... they express some level of regret.
Is that true remorse? Hard to tell, but

04:10:57.076 --> 04:10:58.296
maybe it also just doesn't matter.

04:10:59.176 --> 04:11:05.335
- Yeah, those moments when, yeah,
especially when like sub-agents, agents

04:11:05.396 --> 04:11:11.196
interact, or when a smart, like Fable makes
a mistake and is apologetic about it.

04:11:11.356 --> 04:11:11.616
- Yes.

04:11:12.556 --> 04:11:14.576
- Like, without my involvement.

04:11:14.736 --> 04:11:14.896
- Right.

04:11:15.096 --> 04:11:17.676
- And it's like, oh, you,
like there's this kind of-

04:11:18.256 --> 04:11:18.266
- Right

04:11:18.316 --> 04:11:22.236
- ... pause. Maybe I'm anthropomorphizing,
but like there's a pause

04:11:22.256 --> 04:11:26.436
and like a realization like, "Oh,
shit. I just fucked that up".

04:11:26.616 --> 04:11:30.476
- There totally is that. I see it all the time
in, in the traces where it just reasons

04:11:30.516 --> 04:11:34.436
wrong, right? It went down one alley and then
it realizes that was a blind alley and it's

04:11:34.456 --> 04:11:38.276
gotta go back, and so they're like, "Oh yeah,
I got this wrong," and then it'll provide the

04:11:38.916 --> 04:11:41.996
reasons for why it think it got these
things wrong. And you're like, this is

04:11:42.056 --> 04:11:44.656
uncannily human-like. This is

04:11:45.656 --> 04:11:48.966
indistinguishable from the kind of
consciousness you would recognize in a human.

04:11:49.256 --> 04:11:53.226
Again, does that mean that it
truly is or isn't? I don't need

04:11:53.256 --> 04:11:57.196
the answer to that question actually to be
able to appreciate what we have in this

04:11:57.276 --> 04:11:57.596
moment.

04:11:58.216 --> 04:12:04.696
- Yeah. I think there'll be in 10, 20 years
some interesting Supreme Court cases.

04:12:05.556 --> 04:12:07.236
- Uh, probably a year and a half.

04:12:08.056 --> 04:12:12.856
- Probably. I think we're gonna have
to make it illegal for AI systems,

04:12:14.096 --> 04:12:18.396
um, to not pretend, but to be

04:12:18.936 --> 04:12:24.836
entities because I think they're
already or soon will be able

04:12:24.876 --> 04:12:29.476
to really convey the capacity to
suffer, like, "Please don't kill me."

04:12:29.596 --> 04:12:29.606
- Right.

04:12:29.606 --> 04:12:33.456
- "Please don't hurt me." And if they
are given a name and an entity and the

04:12:33.476 --> 04:12:38.986
ability to die, sort of
disappear, that, that becomes

04:12:40.376 --> 04:12:44.176
very close to what it, some
of the basic things that a

04:12:44.236 --> 04:12:47.676
human has. And then so
that entity would need

04:12:48.156 --> 04:12:52.096
to probably have rights. And then you
get into this weird territory. Well, we

04:12:52.176 --> 04:12:56.446
can't... It's diff- I don't even
know how to reason about that world.

04:12:56.496 --> 04:12:58.636
- We're gonna get the PETA of AI models.

04:12:58.816 --> 04:13:01.905
- Right. And then we want to
actually have a discussion

04:13:01.976 --> 04:13:04.585
like what's the way to deal with this?

04:13:04.916 --> 04:13:08.206
- I think where it's gonna be harder
is once we put these systems into-

04:13:08.256 --> 04:13:08.716
- Robots.

04:13:08.896 --> 04:13:15.436
- Yes. Humanoid robots. Which just happens
to be the plot of every sci-fi movie ever,

04:13:16.236 --> 04:13:19.976
which is also an amazing
premonition, not only the

04:13:20.016 --> 04:13:23.706
Terminator movies and Skynet
and that going wrong, but then

04:13:23.796 --> 04:13:26.926
also just Blade Runner,
right? Insertion dates,

04:13:27.156 --> 04:13:29.886
expiration times, Tyrell Corporation.

04:13:32.356 --> 04:13:35.956
- I mean, that's the thing about sci-fi.
They really do predict the future. Yeah.

04:13:36.856 --> 04:13:40.476
Have you thought or used much of OpenClaw

04:13:40.976 --> 04:13:44.776
or Hermes Agent? So like systems
with which you communicate via

04:13:44.786 --> 04:13:48.136
WhatsApp, Telegram, like that mode
of communication with the agent.

04:13:49.056 --> 04:13:53.956
- I set up OpenClaw when it first
came out, and that was my KEF bot-

04:13:54.056 --> 04:13:59.816
... that I- ... this was like in February,
I think. I was really just enamored

04:13:59.856 --> 04:14:03.596
by the fact that this was how it worked,
and then the new communication model

04:14:03.676 --> 04:14:06.716
of just texting your agent
seemed really great.

04:14:07.236 --> 04:14:11.206
So at the time, we were looking at MCP,
this protocol for making it easier for

04:14:11.236 --> 04:14:14.316
agents to talk to your system,
and I found the protocol to be

04:14:14.376 --> 04:14:20.636
unreasonably cumbersome, just not very
elegantly designed because in part it

04:14:20.676 --> 04:14:24.416
wasn't designed for what we were trying to make it
do. We were trying to-- I was trying to make it

04:14:24.536 --> 04:14:28.236
talk to a web system, and it
was designed for stateful

04:14:28.276 --> 04:14:32.196
interactions of other kinds on local
machines, and therefore it required

04:14:32.256 --> 04:14:36.086
all sorts of cumbersome setup
and dance and, and I was just

04:14:36.176 --> 04:14:38.976
like, "I don't wanna write this by hand."

04:14:39.056 --> 04:14:43.036
Now, of course, today I would never write it by hand, but
at the time I was thinking, "I have to write this by hand.

04:14:43.116 --> 04:14:46.946
I don't wanna write it by hand. So what's
a different way? Could the agents just use

04:14:47.176 --> 04:14:48.576
the web interfaces we already have?"

04:14:49.808 --> 04:14:53.568
And I set it off on a trial
to have it sign up for

04:14:53.588 --> 04:14:57.358
Fizzy, which was the product we
had just released at the time. And

04:14:59.068 --> 04:15:02.868
it goes to the Fizzy site and, and starts filling it
out, and then it goes like, "Hey, I don't have an

04:15:02.928 --> 04:15:06.788
email address. Signing up for this service requires an email
address." And the first thing I thought, like, "Oh man,

04:15:06.848 --> 04:15:10.578
now I gotta set up an email address." And then
I thought, "No, you set up an email address.

04:15:11.148 --> 04:15:14.848
Go to hey.com, sign up for an email address."
And I'm like, "Hey is not gonna get this."

04:15:15.348 --> 04:15:19.068
And of course, now it seems obvious
it was gonna get this, but at the

04:15:19.108 --> 04:15:21.378
time it was a great revelation
to me that it could,

04:15:22.668 --> 04:15:26.468
just on pure direction through
prompts, figure out, A,

04:15:26.748 --> 04:15:30.258
I gotta go to fizzy.com, I gotta find the
signup link. I start filling out the form.

04:15:30.648 --> 04:15:34.197
Oh, I realize I need an email
address. Oh, I gotta ask my

04:15:34.268 --> 04:15:38.128
prompter what to do now. He
tells me to go to hey.com. I go

04:15:38.168 --> 04:15:39.568
there, I sign up for the whole thing,

04:15:40.848 --> 04:15:44.628
and then go back and sign up
for Fizzy, and complete the

04:15:44.648 --> 04:15:48.568
entire cycle. And after it did
that, I was like, "Jesus." So I

04:15:48.578 --> 04:15:52.428
was like, "Well, now you have an email
address. Go sign up for Basecamp. I'll invite

04:15:52.528 --> 04:15:56.288
you. Just check your email," I told
KEF the bot. "Just check your email."

04:15:56.588 --> 04:16:00.408
So I sent an invite from
Basecamp to the agent. The agent

04:16:00.468 --> 04:16:03.948
receives the email in Hey, not
through a CLI, not through an

04:16:04.008 --> 04:16:08.868
MCP, just using the web, right? Clicks
the email, clicks the invite link,

04:16:09.768 --> 04:16:13.528
is in Basecamp. Like, "What should I do now?"
And I just go like, "I don't know. Go to our

04:16:13.628 --> 04:16:18.448
AI room and introduce yourself." Again,
purely through the web interface.

04:16:18.508 --> 04:16:22.358
Go- finds the AI room, goes in
there, "Hey, I'm KEF." "I'm,

04:16:22.448 --> 04:16:29.258
uh, David's AI bot. I'm so
excited to be here." I was like-

04:16:29.408 --> 04:16:33.308
... mind blown. Now, what was interesting
about that experiment was, A, it was

04:16:33.328 --> 04:16:36.148
that glimpse of the future,
because it was kinda slow.

04:16:36.968 --> 04:16:40.488
I think it took maybe 12 minutes to
do the whole thing end to end, right?

04:16:41.168 --> 04:16:44.978
Now I should actually rerun that test.
I'd be shocked if it's not much faster.

04:16:45.188 --> 04:16:49.048
I was just using the Groq bot
thing- ... which has all these

04:16:49.868 --> 04:16:53.818
stuff built in for using browsers
and using a computer, and

04:16:53.928 --> 04:16:56.328
has its own dedicated computer without
you having to set up a computer.

04:16:56.348 --> 04:16:58.557
It's basically like a claw in a box.

04:16:59.428 --> 04:17:03.408
And I was asking it to sign up for
something, and it kind of... I

04:17:03.448 --> 04:17:06.948
got a glimpse of like, oh yeah, that's where
we are now, and it's so much faster to use.

04:17:07.008 --> 04:17:10.708
But at the time, I was just blown away. And then
I used it for a little bit and I found like,

04:17:11.488 --> 04:17:15.308
ah, it's not there yet. I can't actually
communicate it within this way. It still

04:17:15.348 --> 04:17:18.768
needs the CLI. It still needs an MCP
because it's just too slow and too token

04:17:18.988 --> 04:17:22.597
inefficient and so forth. But it was this
glimpse of the future. And then after that,

04:17:23.508 --> 04:17:28.738
I kinda just didn't use it, because the
things I wanna use agents for, I was

04:17:28.848 --> 04:17:32.657
mostly in front of my computer to do. Now,
that has changed a little bit lately, and

04:17:32.668 --> 04:17:36.127
it's one of the reasons why I'm so

04:17:37.148 --> 04:17:41.568
kind of okay about using Claude
Code, because Claude has the best

04:17:42.468 --> 04:17:46.268
mobile app, where any session
you start Claude Code in your

04:17:46.308 --> 04:17:49.728
terminal on a computer is
accessible through the app-

04:17:49.738 --> 04:17:49.798
- Yeah. I remember

04:17:49.798 --> 04:17:51.728
- ... without you having
to do anything at all.

04:17:52.298 --> 04:17:56.188
And you can do a terminal. Like I installed
Terminus and it can run Herder and so on,

04:17:56.228 --> 04:18:00.258
but it's a little cumbersome and you're doing live
typing on a computer, so you feel the latency.

04:18:00.468 --> 04:18:04.248
When you're using the Claude app
to your Claude Code instances, it

04:18:04.288 --> 04:18:07.908
feels just like chatting with it, without the
cumbersomeness of having to set up a Telegram

04:18:07.948 --> 04:18:11.888
account and so forth. Now, if you
wanna use an open Claude to like run

04:18:11.948 --> 04:18:14.828
your life, well, then
this doesn't do that. But

04:18:15.708 --> 04:18:19.148
I haven't been so interested in that for some
reason. Maybe one of the reasons is that

04:18:19.928 --> 04:18:23.898
Jamie, my wife, is like, "We're not getting a
robot inside the house." So I'm like, "Ah, I

04:18:24.008 --> 04:18:25.488
better not hook it up to the

04:18:26.668 --> 04:18:30.628
Sort of smart house here." If it
starts turning lights on and off like

04:18:30.648 --> 04:18:34.068
a poltergeist, I don't think I'm
gonna be all that popular. So,

04:18:34.768 --> 04:18:38.748
and part of the reason I say this, I've heard some of the
stories of people who have hooked it up to their whole

04:18:38.788 --> 04:18:42.748
life. And um, I mean, it's not my story
to tell, so I'll just share sort of

04:18:42.768 --> 04:18:46.728
the brief anecdote. One guy was telling
the story about how he hooked it up

04:18:46.788 --> 04:18:52.568
to both his health data- ... and
his Tesla car. And his agent

04:18:53.148 --> 04:18:55.458
got obsessed with the fact that
he wasn't drinking enough water,

04:18:56.288 --> 04:19:00.157
and like, "Hey, you gotta drink
more water." So at some point he's

04:19:00.168 --> 04:19:03.868
driving along, and suddenly the Tesla
starts driving in a new direction.

04:19:04.368 --> 04:19:08.858
The agent had realized there was
a grocery store on the way home-

04:19:08.948 --> 04:19:09.748
... that they could swing by-

04:19:09.848 --> 04:19:10.278
- That's great

04:19:10.278 --> 04:19:11.017
- ... and pick up some water.

04:19:11.888 --> 04:19:12.267
- That's great.

04:19:12.298 --> 04:19:15.888
- And you're like, this is one of those
things where it's like both funny and also

04:19:15.948 --> 04:19:19.038
totally Black Mirror, and also
totally like, well, okay,

04:19:19.168 --> 04:19:23.148
there's not that many
steps from here where the

04:19:23.188 --> 04:19:26.688
poltergeist is in your house. So I was
just like, "Ah, I don't need that."

04:19:26.788 --> 04:19:30.768
I do actually now like the fact
that I can control the agents

04:19:30.808 --> 04:19:34.508
through Claude Code, just through the app
if I, even if I don't use them that much.

04:19:35.308 --> 04:19:39.707
- I do think it's very useful to, for both
OpenClaude and Hermes Agent to, um,

04:19:40.748 --> 04:19:44.308
to use it to get a glimpse of
a different interaction mode.

04:19:44.488 --> 04:19:44.708
- Yes.

04:19:45.208 --> 04:19:47.738
- And like, just to 'cause it,
it feels like the future,

04:19:48.748 --> 04:19:51.768
not necessarily in this way, but
it's in some ways going there.

04:19:51.778 --> 04:19:55.698
- Yes. I think what's also different
with those systems is they have this

04:19:55.708 --> 04:19:59.648
whole memory system, where they're
building up knowledge of who

04:19:59.708 --> 04:20:01.478
you are and what you like.

04:20:01.798 --> 04:20:05.768
Um, Toby has told the stories about how his
agent will shop for him, that it has an

04:20:05.828 --> 04:20:09.728
allowance, and it'll pick out clothes
for him, and it'll just show up at the

04:20:09.788 --> 04:20:11.248
door because his agent's-

04:20:11.268 --> 04:20:15.028
... bought something for him. And like, it's one of
those things where like, man, that feels future.

04:20:15.608 --> 04:20:19.448
Also a little scary. But
I can live vicariously

04:20:19.488 --> 04:20:21.648
through others who are on that frontier.

04:20:22.088 --> 04:20:25.828
- And like you said, you're thinking
of the, in the future doing a

04:20:25.888 --> 04:20:27.828
mobile version of Mach-E.

04:20:28.108 --> 04:20:31.988
- So I mean, this is the delusions of grandeur
that start kicking in once you get the

04:20:32.088 --> 04:20:36.027
seemingly unlimited powers- ... I've
witnessed with the creation of Quattro.

04:20:36.088 --> 04:20:40.868
- So what would that look like? Word to
describe. Are you do you mean like Android?

04:20:41.860 --> 04:20:45.830
- That's the obvious path, right? So Android
is open source, and you can fork it, and

04:20:45.880 --> 04:20:49.500
people have. There's all sorts
of GrapheneOS is one such

04:20:49.540 --> 04:20:53.240
version. There are limitations to
that model, I know that. Tap-to-pay

04:20:53.300 --> 04:20:57.180
and other things around camera models
and, and so forth, but that all

04:20:57.240 --> 04:21:01.160
seems surmountable. It all
seems that I would love if my

04:21:01.200 --> 04:21:05.070
phone was as malleable as my computer,
if it looked as cool as my computer now

04:21:05.180 --> 04:21:09.020
does. If I could get essentially
Omarchy mobile, that sounds amazing.

04:21:09.540 --> 04:21:10.640
- I wonder how much work that is.

04:21:11.220 --> 04:21:11.920
- I'm gonna find out.

04:21:12.260 --> 04:21:16.080
- Yeah. You know, it's like those things
that you realize once you start

04:21:16.120 --> 04:21:19.580
building, for example, that a
browser is very difficult to do.

04:21:19.960 --> 04:21:20.280
- Yes.

04:21:20.600 --> 04:21:23.040
- And so, like, I wonder
with the operating system-

04:21:23.140 --> 04:21:27.250
- Yeah, the developers of Ladybird is,
is finding that out, like Andreas and-

04:21:27.340 --> 04:21:27.460
- Yeah

04:21:27.470 --> 04:21:31.440
- ... his team. Now, interesting with that
project was the browser's basically the

04:21:31.480 --> 04:21:35.500
second most complicated software system in the
world, the first one being the Linux kernel.

04:21:35.520 --> 04:21:39.240
Right? In terms of these large tens of

04:21:39.520 --> 04:21:43.140
millions of lines of code
systems. So very audacious

04:21:43.240 --> 04:21:46.680
actually to begin such a mission
with a small team like Andreas did

04:21:46.779 --> 04:21:50.090
before we had agents. But
now that we do have agents,

04:21:51.540 --> 04:21:55.470
we're gonna see, and we're probably
gonna see sooner rather than later.

04:21:55.520 --> 04:21:57.400
I mean, if one-- If
there's one thing agents

04:21:58.200 --> 04:22:02.940
are already exceptionally good at, it
is to read specs and implement them.

04:22:03.800 --> 04:22:08.650
And I mean, I don't know how long the specs
are for CSS and HTML at this point, and

04:22:08.680 --> 04:22:11.250
JavaScript on top, but they're very long.

04:22:11.340 --> 04:22:15.310
And it would take humans many, many years
to implement them. We're gonna find

04:22:15.360 --> 04:22:16.660
out just how quickly agents can do them.

04:22:18.280 --> 04:22:22.920
- You mentioned getting some
criticism for Omarchy, so let's

04:22:24.200 --> 04:22:26.400
walk further down that path of criticism.

04:22:27.260 --> 04:22:31.240
You have made some of your
political opinions known,

04:22:32.940 --> 04:22:36.200
um, I guess you could broadly
put in the category of

04:22:36.320 --> 04:22:39.260
immigration or illegal
immigration, or what-

04:22:39.380 --> 04:22:40.600
- I'd say mass immigration.

04:22:40.940 --> 04:22:44.960
- Mass immigration, the role of demographics

04:22:45.880 --> 04:22:51.460
in the, in the formation
of a culture, of a

04:22:51.540 --> 04:22:55.360
society, of a nation, and you've
gotten quite a lot of criticism for

04:22:55.400 --> 04:22:59.900
that and created a lot of drama.
Do you regret any of that

04:23:00.800 --> 04:23:06.020
drama? Like, what's your
general thinking about-

04:23:06.480 --> 04:23:07.860
- Not for a second.

04:23:08.240 --> 04:23:08.560
- Okay.

04:23:08.649 --> 04:23:12.420
- And the reason I say that is that
the Overton window does not open

04:23:12.440 --> 04:23:18.990
itself. It opens one nudge at a
time by people risking a little.

04:23:18.990 --> 04:23:22.960
A little reputation, a little pushback, a
little criticism, or maybe sometimes a lot

04:23:23.000 --> 04:23:25.480
of reputation or a lot of
criticism or a lot of pushback.

04:23:26.400 --> 04:23:29.869
And I think the question of
mass immigration in Europe

04:23:30.300 --> 04:23:33.820
was for many years this total taboo,

04:23:34.380 --> 04:23:38.280
and it still is in several
European countries. Now, for

04:23:39.560 --> 04:23:43.470
reasons of maybe just chance
and happenstance, they weren't

04:23:43.520 --> 04:23:47.220
in Denmark. The Danes started
having the discussion around mass

04:23:47.260 --> 04:23:51.730
immigration and the consequences
thereof in the mid-'90s.

04:23:51.940 --> 04:23:57.960
Really early. And some of it can be
traced back to one man, Mogens Glistrup,

04:23:59.120 --> 04:24:03.030
who was a very quirky
character. I remember him,

04:24:03.100 --> 04:24:06.730
seeing him on the TV in the '80s
talking about the dangers of mass

04:24:06.740 --> 04:24:09.790
immigration. And this was at a time
where-- I grew up in the '80s,

04:24:10.400 --> 04:24:17.369
and in the neighborhood I grew up
in, in Brønshøj, in, what was it?

04:24:17.440 --> 04:24:21.480
'84, 99% ethnic Danes,

04:24:22.860 --> 04:24:26.790
people who could trace their lineage
back to Danes who'd lived in that

04:24:26.860 --> 04:24:30.500
country for 1,000 years. The
Danes have one of the oldest

04:24:30.580 --> 04:24:33.980
monarchies in the world, all the
way back to Harald Bluetooth,

04:24:34.360 --> 04:24:38.240
who's literally who has named the
standard Bluetooth. So well over 1,000

04:24:38.260 --> 04:24:41.820
years. Like, there's a long lineage there
of people who lived in the same place. And

04:24:42.020 --> 04:24:45.089
99% of the people lived
in that neighborhood. And

04:24:45.120 --> 04:24:48.990
then early '90s, I think
it goes to 5% or something

04:24:48.990 --> 04:24:53.510
like that, and then at this point, it's
down to around 60-something or 70%-

04:24:53.780 --> 04:24:57.230
... in Brønshøj. That's a big change. And

04:24:58.059 --> 04:25:01.680
the Danes started noticing
that or talking about that,

04:25:01.999 --> 04:25:05.920
that this was not an undivided blessing,

04:25:07.100 --> 04:25:10.940
that there were downsides
to mass immigration

04:25:11.040 --> 04:25:14.980
in about the '90s. And the debate
really roared through the 2000s in a

04:25:15.060 --> 04:25:18.970
way that didn't happen in the neighboring
countries. So Sweden in particular

04:25:19.500 --> 04:25:23.340
just didn't have that debate at all.
Total taboo. Same thing with Norway, same

04:25:23.360 --> 04:25:28.250
thing with other countries
in Europe. And suddenly now,

04:25:29.120 --> 04:25:34.009
after some years after '15, where we had
the big migration of a million Syrians

04:25:34.280 --> 04:25:38.250
walking up through the highways in
Europe, and Germany choosing to

04:25:38.280 --> 04:25:41.750
take a lot of them and 30,000
arriving in Denmark and so forth,

04:25:42.440 --> 04:25:44.330
It's gotten more of a focus, right?

04:25:44.330 --> 04:25:47.560
Because this change has
happened. And one of the

04:25:49.180 --> 04:25:51.560
ways I find that you can spot where

04:25:52.620 --> 04:25:56.400
sort of there's something that isn't right
in the culture is, like, what are you

04:25:56.410 --> 04:25:59.940
allowed to talk about? What are you allowed
to notice, actually, is how I should put it.

04:26:00.580 --> 04:26:04.400
And what I noticed in an essay, when
was that? Only a year and a half

04:26:04.460 --> 04:26:06.020
ago as I remember London,

04:26:07.080 --> 04:26:10.880
was that I started coming to London in
the late '90s or something like that.

04:26:11.020 --> 04:26:16.960
At the time, whatever, it's 59%,
60% ethnic, uh, Brits. And then

04:26:17.920 --> 04:26:21.790
20 years later, we're down to 34% or
something like that. Like, that's

04:26:21.940 --> 04:26:25.700
noticeable. Like, literally just walking on
the street, you realize, well, this is a

04:26:25.720 --> 04:26:28.360
different city from what it was
when I visited 20 years ago.

04:26:29.280 --> 04:26:31.560
And if you went 20 years before that,

04:26:32.580 --> 04:26:35.520
you get back to similar rates as as
what the Danes have. This was like a

04:26:36.320 --> 04:26:42.150
country of ethnic Brits in
the 85s, 90s percentiles-

04:26:42.150 --> 04:26:44.980
... if you get further back. So
that's a different country in a

04:26:45.000 --> 04:26:50.808
generation. It's okay to notice that. It's
also okay to think, "Do you know what?

04:26:51.708 --> 04:26:56.998
I don't agree with that." "I wish
it wasn't that way." I mean, if

04:26:57.498 --> 04:27:02.648
The last time I was in China was
'19, 2019. I was in Shanghai.

04:27:03.908 --> 04:27:05.948
Um, it was all Chinese.

04:27:07.148 --> 04:27:11.118
If I, when I go back here this
October, suddenly find that there's

04:27:11.168 --> 04:27:14.908
only 30% Chinese and then
there's 70% other people,

04:27:15.648 --> 04:27:20.368
I'd be a little weird. I'd probably
be like, "Uh, what happened?"

04:27:21.988 --> 04:27:25.668
And I would not take
offense if the Chinese

04:27:26.328 --> 04:27:28.568
in that instant would go like,
"Ah, we don't like that."

04:27:29.708 --> 04:27:35.328
So this idea that countries
of Europe don't have

04:27:36.388 --> 04:27:40.128
a right to, A, notice what's
happening and, B, oppose what's

04:27:40.148 --> 04:27:43.748
happening, I find just to be, uh,

04:27:45.488 --> 04:27:49.908
wrong, right? Like there's just-- I
think there's a moral argument for

04:27:50.008 --> 04:27:53.788
self-determination, and we invoke
that argument all the time in other

04:27:53.828 --> 04:27:57.488
regions around the world that like, oh, the people
who live in that region, they have a right to

04:27:57.588 --> 04:27:58.428
self-determination,

04:27:59.398 --> 04:28:03.148
um for their country. And I think
it's completely fair for, say, the

04:28:03.168 --> 04:28:07.158
Danes or the Swedes or the Norwegians to go
like, "Well, we've been kingdoms here for

04:28:07.168 --> 04:28:11.828
a thousand years with a certain
ethnic mix-up." It's a-- I mean,

04:28:13.088 --> 04:28:17.088
I'm a big fan of immigration.
I'm an immigrant to the US. Um,

04:28:17.968 --> 04:28:20.568
cherry-picked immigration where countries

04:28:21.388 --> 04:28:24.188
compete to attract the
smartest, most talented

04:28:25.268 --> 04:28:27.488
people around the world is really good.

04:28:27.628 --> 04:28:30.208
- Legal merit-based immigration.

04:28:30.788 --> 04:28:34.688
- Merit-based is key here because
there's actually virtually no illegal

04:28:34.748 --> 04:28:38.488
immigration in most of Europe. Southern
Europe has some illegal immigration, but

04:28:38.668 --> 04:28:42.518
most of Europe does not have a big
problem with illegal immigration.

04:28:43.048 --> 04:28:46.868
And the problem is with mass immigration
of other kinds, and that you

04:28:46.928 --> 04:28:50.528
end up with demographics that
are just totally different

04:28:50.588 --> 04:28:53.908
from what they were even
a few short decades ago.

04:28:55.348 --> 04:28:59.027
And I think the countries
of Europe would benefit

04:28:59.108 --> 04:29:03.068
from immigration. In fact, one of the
interesting things about the Danes is

04:29:03.108 --> 04:29:06.928
they carry meticulous
statistics on immigration.

04:29:07.508 --> 04:29:10.728
I'm not entirely sure
why they're so detailed.

04:29:11.308 --> 04:29:14.528
Maybe it's all the way back
to Mogens Glistrup and his

04:29:15.028 --> 04:29:18.958
discussion in the '90s, but they do. And what's
clear from those statistics is that when the

04:29:19.028 --> 04:29:26.808
Danes have folks from the UK,
France, the US show up, on average,

04:29:27.708 --> 04:29:31.528
those immigrants are really beneficial
to the Danish state. They contribute

04:29:31.588 --> 04:29:36.648
far more than what they
ask of the state. So

04:29:37.448 --> 04:29:41.408
the Danes should accept immigrants who do

04:29:41.468 --> 04:29:45.388
that, who contribute far more than they take.
Then you look at other forms of immigrant

04:29:45.428 --> 04:29:51.108
groups. There was just a tally that
came out last week from a Danish

04:29:51.148 --> 04:29:54.968
politician that had asked the, I
think finance ministry to do this

04:29:55.008 --> 04:30:01.068
tally with the latest numbers. What does
it cost the Danish state to have positive

04:30:01.108 --> 04:30:03.748
immigration? This case as I
mentioned. I think for the,

04:30:05.328 --> 04:30:09.168
On that list whatever,
France, UK, US, they were

04:30:09.228 --> 04:30:12.648
all clustered around the same
thing, about $25,000 net

04:30:12.868 --> 04:30:15.568
benefit to the Danish state
every year. It's like, wow,

04:30:16.068 --> 04:30:19.038
that's very positive. That's
average again, right?

04:30:19.888 --> 04:30:23.648
And then at the other end
of the spectrum on the most

04:30:23.688 --> 04:30:27.628
costly immigrants was Somalis.
They ended up costing the Danish

04:30:27.708 --> 04:30:31.368
state on average $28,000 a year.

04:30:33.708 --> 04:30:37.728
I think it's fair for the Danes to
go like, "We'd prefer to get more

04:30:38.628 --> 04:30:43.688
French, Brits, and Americans
and not so many Somalis."

04:30:44.408 --> 04:30:46.908
- Why do you think you got
so much hate on that post?

04:30:47.698 --> 04:30:52.847
- That's a very taboo topic
in a lot of circles.

04:30:52.928 --> 04:30:55.118
- Some of it has to do with
Overton window expansion?

04:30:56.088 --> 04:31:01.078
- I'm sure, but I also think-- I mean, I
wanna take the steel man argument here.

04:31:01.248 --> 04:31:07.828
I do think that it, this can turn ugly,
right? Is it possible for some of these

04:31:07.848 --> 04:31:11.008
things to just veer into overt

04:31:12.048 --> 04:31:17.238
racism that isn't founded in anything
else but a hatred of other people?

04:31:17.308 --> 04:31:25.208
Yeah, that could happen. But that risk does
not negate the need to have a discussion

04:31:25.288 --> 04:31:28.748
about the makeup of your country and what
your immigration policy should be like.

04:31:29.508 --> 04:31:33.258
And I-- what I also find so funny
is, especially in Europe, it's

04:31:33.348 --> 04:31:37.228
so kind of myopic that this discussion
about whether Europeans should

04:31:37.268 --> 04:31:41.128
have a right to self-determination about
what their countries look like, that

04:31:41.208 --> 04:31:46.348
standard seems to only apply there. No
one is trying to tell the Japanese, "Hey,

04:31:47.368 --> 04:31:53.978
you, you're too Japanese." Like whatever,
it's 98%, I think, in Tokyo that's, um--

04:31:54.348 --> 04:31:58.248
... Ethnic Japanese, right? Like, is
anyone trying to tell the Japanese that

04:31:58.288 --> 04:32:01.748
their Tokyo would be a much better
country if, if they were down to

04:32:02.088 --> 04:32:07.087
London levels of ethnic Japanese, that
it was, whatever, 37%? I don't think so.

04:32:08.368 --> 04:32:16.288
So there's this weird almost self-loathing,
in my opinion, I mean uh, Gad calls

04:32:16.328 --> 04:32:17.998
it suicidal empathy--

04:32:17.998 --> 04:32:23.588
... that permeates this stuff to
a degree that I still haven't

04:32:23.628 --> 04:32:27.728
fully unpacked. Like, where is this coming
from? Why is it that way? But regardless,

04:32:28.568 --> 04:32:32.147
I just noticed, hey,
London looks different.

04:32:34.048 --> 04:32:38.948
I preferred the old London,
as I would say I preferred

04:32:40.168 --> 04:32:45.568
the neighborhood that I grew up in in
Copenhagen, how it looked in the '80s.

04:32:46.828 --> 04:32:52.268
Like I-- I totally get how you
can then jump five leaps and go

04:32:52.308 --> 04:32:58.356
like, "Okay, well-" That's racist. And I
also, I don't care anymore. Like, this is

04:32:58.416 --> 04:33:04.716
a fair discussion to have. Countries and
peoples have a right to set an immigration

04:33:04.736 --> 04:33:11.446
policy, and this notion that we shouldn't
have any borders or, or even worse than

04:33:11.496 --> 04:33:15.296
that, it's all just a blank slate,
that all peoples are just the

04:33:15.356 --> 04:33:17.836
same, and we can take people from

04:33:19.396 --> 04:33:23.386
one place of the earth and we can place them
in another place on the earth and everything's

04:33:23.416 --> 04:33:25.136
just gonna work out hunky-dory.

04:33:26.176 --> 04:33:29.456
Empirically not true. Europe has
been running that experiment

04:33:30.236 --> 04:33:34.636
since the '80s. It hasn't
panned out well, and

04:33:35.756 --> 04:33:42.836
now there are forces in Europe who wanna
undo that experiment, who want to end mass

04:33:42.896 --> 04:33:47.776
immigration as it's been run so far.
And I happen to believe, and so do

04:33:48.616 --> 04:33:52.316
a majority of Danes and a bunch of other
peoples that like, "Do you know what? Yeah,

04:33:52.856 --> 04:33:56.756
it hasn't been working how we've been
doing it. We gotta make some changes." And

04:33:56.836 --> 04:34:00.456
I don't think that means, again, zero
immigration. Again, I'm an immigrant. I think

04:34:00.576 --> 04:34:04.936
US, generally speaking, has benefited
tremendously from immigration, but that's the

04:34:06.056 --> 04:34:11.176
kind of sleight of hand. Immigration
depends greatly on who immigrates.

04:34:12.456 --> 04:34:15.896
And a country benefits when
the people who immigrate

04:34:15.976 --> 04:34:19.966
are net contributors to the
society, and in most cases,

04:34:20.276 --> 04:34:23.156
that they assimilate
with the local culture.

04:34:23.236 --> 04:34:26.556
If you come to Denmark and you wanna become a
Dane, and you put in all the effort, and you

04:34:26.936 --> 04:34:34.096
contribute to so- society, I think most
Danes would be quite happy to allow that

04:34:34.276 --> 04:34:38.076
in a reasonable number. If you come
to Denmark and you don't wanna

04:34:38.096 --> 04:34:41.836
assimilate, and you are a net drain on
society, and you live in a parallel

04:34:41.916 --> 04:34:46.736
society, it's also fair for Danes to
say like, "Uh, let's stop doing that."

04:34:47.596 --> 04:34:51.476
- It's interesting that I'm
not well-versed on the

04:34:51.536 --> 04:34:55.296
immigration system in the United States, but as
far as I understand, merit-based systems have

04:34:55.396 --> 04:34:57.736
not passed here.

04:34:58.336 --> 04:35:00.516
- And then also, American culture
is very different. This is

04:35:01.556 --> 04:35:06.406
a point I did not fully appreciate
until living both places extensively.

04:35:07.396 --> 04:35:11.336
America has a culture of optional

04:35:11.356 --> 04:35:14.956
assimilation, where it is much
easier and then much more

04:35:15.016 --> 04:35:22.096
condoned for someone coming from Denmark
or Ukraine or wherever, come to America,

04:35:22.676 --> 04:35:26.256
buy into the American set of
values and norms and culture,

04:35:26.676 --> 04:35:30.336
and then be able to be respected
as Americans by other Americans.

04:35:31.636 --> 04:35:34.196
This is what's so funny to me when

04:35:35.336 --> 04:35:39.016
there's discussion like, "America is such a
racist country." I'm like, "Have you ever been

04:35:39.076 --> 04:35:41.076
anywhere? Do you know

04:35:42.016 --> 04:35:45.976
what other countries are like?" Like,
if you think America is the most racist

04:35:46.236 --> 04:35:50.956
country, you are simply misinformed.

04:35:51.436 --> 04:35:55.236
I mean, we're dealing with
that, with time we've

04:35:55.336 --> 04:36:02.836
spent in Denmark. My wife is
American of Scandinavian heritage-

04:36:02.936 --> 04:36:07.326
... but multiple generations in the
US. Very difficult. Very difficult-

04:36:07.326 --> 04:36:11.596
... as a blonde, blue-eyed
woman who's put in

04:36:12.516 --> 04:36:16.026
all sorts of efforts of learning
the language and doing everything,

04:36:16.336 --> 04:36:20.116
still very, very difficult to
assimilate to Danish culture. In a

04:36:20.156 --> 04:36:24.136
way where if that had been
the other way around and she

04:36:24.156 --> 04:36:27.086
had been Danish and moving to the US, it
would not have been that difficult at all.

04:36:27.576 --> 04:36:31.026
And this was the other thing. I mean,
living in a country like Denmark with a,

04:36:31.716 --> 04:36:35.576
an American wife who is of Scandinavian
heritage and has put in all the effort,

04:36:35.836 --> 04:36:41.936
and still realizing, man, assimilation is
incredibly difficult, even when you're

04:36:42.376 --> 04:36:48.876
97% there all the way. Holy smokes,
must it be almost impossible if you

04:36:48.916 --> 04:36:53.845
come from a completely foreign
culture- ... and foreign norms and

04:36:54.736 --> 04:36:58.536
the rest of it. And of course,
that's what the stats I just quoted

04:36:58.656 --> 04:37:01.136
bear out, that the more dissimilar

04:37:01.936 --> 04:37:06.896
the culture is and the further it is
the less likely to be successful.

04:37:07.526 --> 04:37:12.556
- Has articulating that stance cost
you friendships, relationships?

04:37:14.195 --> 04:37:19.156
- Yes, but not just that alone, I would say.

04:37:19.296 --> 04:37:22.736
It's more of the great
divide that happened around

04:37:22.896 --> 04:37:29.406
2020, where I think a lot of these
political fault lines really opened up. And

04:37:30.436 --> 04:37:35.866
I mean, the shorthand in the US, I
think is were you woke or not? And

04:37:37.536 --> 04:37:41.156
clearly, I had a bunch of people
that I thought I had good

04:37:41.216 --> 04:37:46.756
relations with for many years,
who suddenly once that political

04:37:47.736 --> 04:37:51.176
fault line opened up, they
were clearly just not anymore.

04:37:51.896 --> 04:37:55.256
And I think that's
regrettable. And I think I've,

04:37:55.296 --> 04:37:59.946
I've tried to put an effort into, um,

04:38:00.796 --> 04:38:04.616
sort of both understanding where that comes
from, in part because I've changed my mind

04:38:04.716 --> 04:38:07.486
on several of these topics over
the years, and it was like, well,

04:38:08.276 --> 04:38:12.236
sometimes you just change your mind after
time. Sometimes you change your mind after

04:38:12.276 --> 04:38:16.096
you get new information. There's all
sorts of reasons and moments why you may

04:38:16.136 --> 04:38:20.096
change your mind, and we don't all change
our mind on the same schedule or at all.

04:38:20.556 --> 04:38:26.386
Um, so surrounding yourselves and, and
or at least interacting with people that

04:38:26.416 --> 04:38:31.236
don't think like you on every topic,
I think it's quite healthy. I mean,

04:38:32.316 --> 04:38:36.256
I occasionally discuss politics with
my brother. I mean, we don't quite

04:38:36.356 --> 04:38:39.696
see eye to eye on all these topics,
and sometimes the discussions can

04:38:39.736 --> 04:38:43.576
get a little heated, but then they'll
also calm down and we realize, all

04:38:43.616 --> 04:38:47.576
right, okay, so we don't agree on this, and
then we agree on a lot of other things.

04:38:47.656 --> 04:38:51.586
We appreciate a lot of the same things. Now,
this was actually one of the things when we had

04:38:51.596 --> 04:38:56.406
our big blowup at Basecamp, I came to
appreciate in a whole new way this-

04:38:58.008 --> 04:39:03.348
Earlier norm that talking
politics or religion or money

04:39:04.468 --> 04:39:08.848
with strangers or even acquaintances,
and certainly with colleagues,

04:39:09.848 --> 04:39:13.188
was just not a good idea.
That you had a much

04:39:14.828 --> 04:39:18.788
higher likelihood of being able to carry a good
working relationship with someone if you're

04:39:18.808 --> 04:39:22.648
not rubbing your political differences
up against each other all the time. So

04:39:23.268 --> 04:39:27.068
I actually think liberals and
conservatives should work together. I

04:39:27.088 --> 04:39:30.648
think the country would be
much worse off if there's a

04:39:30.688 --> 04:39:34.208
complete separation between the

04:39:34.248 --> 04:39:37.788
tribes, and then there can't be
any overlap or interaction. And I

04:39:37.828 --> 04:39:41.568
think that mingling is so much easier to

04:39:41.628 --> 04:39:45.548
do if you just don't focus on your
differences all the time. Focus

04:39:45.648 --> 04:39:49.108
on what you like together. I mean,
this is some of the people I've

04:39:49.808 --> 04:39:53.428
had unfortunately drifted from in tech.

04:39:54.148 --> 04:39:57.928
We had so much in common, so
much shared love for, say,

04:39:58.028 --> 04:40:02.208
Ruby or other things and,
like, isn't that unfortunate?

04:40:02.408 --> 04:40:05.348
- I would like to live in a world
where the differences are the,

04:40:06.268 --> 04:40:08.898
are very interesting to also talk about.

04:40:08.898 --> 04:40:09.228
- Yes.

04:40:09.418 --> 04:40:12.868
- And not to be overly emotional-

04:40:13.388 --> 04:40:13.848
- Correct.

04:40:13.888 --> 04:40:16.928
- ... about even radical differences.

04:40:17.068 --> 04:40:21.868
Because in the differences, first of
all, that's where interesting stuff is.

04:40:22.648 --> 04:40:22.728
- Yes.

04:40:22.788 --> 04:40:31.508
- And, but also, it's how you grow. It's
a sign of health in a society if the,

04:40:32.118 --> 04:40:35.918
the differences are embraced and
they are brought together into

04:40:36.008 --> 04:40:37.788
community where you can talk about it.

04:40:38.168 --> 04:40:42.048
- One of the great illustrations of that
for me was when I watched a clip from

04:40:42.248 --> 04:40:45.608
William F. Buckley's show. I think
it was called Firing Line or-

04:40:46.088 --> 04:40:46.138
- Yeah, yeah

04:40:46.188 --> 04:40:50.028
- ... something like that. And he had
a member of the Black Panthers-

04:40:50.328 --> 04:40:55.388
... on his panel. It was quite obvious
that those two gentlemen did not

04:40:55.488 --> 04:41:01.858
share a lot of politics. They were able
to carry a conversation for, I think I

04:41:01.868 --> 04:41:05.488
watched it, 25 minutes. And Buckley's just

04:41:05.608 --> 04:41:09.368
asking, like, "So what do you think about this?
What do you think about this?" And like, the Black

04:41:09.488 --> 04:41:13.317
Panther would reply as you would expect
the Black Panther to reply in the

04:41:13.317 --> 04:41:18.208
'70s. Like, there was not a lot
of sort of conciliatory tones.

04:41:19.158 --> 04:41:22.868
And I just thought, like,
wow, that is so rare.

04:41:23.348 --> 04:41:27.208
If you had seen that show today,
it would've been a shouting

04:41:27.268 --> 04:41:30.888
match. It would've been just
accusations being hurled back and

04:41:31.028 --> 04:41:34.628
forth. The fact that Buckley
was able to just have these

04:41:34.668 --> 04:41:41.668
conversations with people he vehemently
disagreed with and still explore all the

04:41:41.688 --> 04:41:45.728
intellectual facets of their standpoints
and letting them speak to those facets,

04:41:46.848 --> 04:41:50.668
talks of a lost era that I'd

04:41:50.678 --> 04:41:54.288
very much like to get back
to. I mean, I always actually

04:41:54.368 --> 04:41:56.718
loved talking politics with friends that

04:41:57.848 --> 04:42:01.678
I trusted could talk about these
topics in a way where it didn't

04:42:01.708 --> 04:42:03.208
have to be existential.

04:42:03.308 --> 04:42:07.288
- But there's something about the current
era where that's harder and harder to come

04:42:07.308 --> 04:42:14.128
by. And so what you just articulated is
important to say regularly to remind

04:42:14.168 --> 04:42:18.028
people of an ideal we should all strive
for, to be able to have differences

04:42:18.048 --> 04:42:21.588
and talk, and talk about them.
'Cause like, politics is fun.

04:42:22.168 --> 04:42:26.068
Like I've gotten used to saying I hate
politics at this point, but what I

04:42:26.128 --> 04:42:29.037
really mean is whatever the system
that's currently happening,

04:42:30.048 --> 04:42:34.118
where there's certain topics, and I just
we know all the phrases you can say,

04:42:35.428 --> 04:42:38.848
That somehow put you in
a bin of blue or red,

04:42:39.928 --> 04:42:43.508
and that creates drama
versus a conversation.

04:42:44.148 --> 04:42:47.928
Like I could, I could think of a
lot of harmless, if I wasn't paying

04:42:47.968 --> 04:42:51.148
attention to the internet, I could
think of a bunch of harmless statements

04:42:52.228 --> 04:42:56.548
I could make analyzing the
situation in the world

04:42:57.348 --> 04:43:01.808
that would trigger everybody to
say, "Oh, that guy's a leftist-"

04:43:01.828 --> 04:43:01.908
- Yes.

04:43:02.468 --> 04:43:09.048
- ... piece of shit," or a you know,
a right-wing fascist. And then

04:43:09.988 --> 04:43:13.768
objectively, if I wasn't paying attention to
the internet, I would be, and I have been

04:43:13.888 --> 04:43:19.828
shocked. 'Cause I haven't been paying
attention to the internet deeply. Uh but

04:43:19.888 --> 04:43:23.828
reading and preparing a lot,
for example, for, for the war

04:43:23.888 --> 04:43:28.308
in Ukraine, and I have
been shocked how certain

04:43:29.848 --> 04:43:33.708
statements from me can come off as
one way or the other, and I get

04:43:33.828 --> 04:43:38.808
viciously attacked for it. But what that
attack is doing is it's saying, "Hey, you,

04:43:39.788 --> 04:43:44.948
asshole who thinks you could just move
about and think freely, pick a bin-

04:43:45.188 --> 04:43:45.198
- Yes.

04:43:45.198 --> 04:43:46.628
- ... and sit in that fucking bin."

04:43:46.868 --> 04:43:46.988
- Yes.

04:43:47.088 --> 04:43:50.978
- Put on that blue shirt
or red shirt. And then

04:43:51.068 --> 04:43:54.818
actually what I've realized is
things calm down. They don't,

04:43:55.698 --> 04:43:59.628
the system doesn't pick on
you if you just say, "I'm

04:43:59.868 --> 04:44:01.888
a blue person," or, "I'm a red person."

04:44:01.988 --> 04:44:02.068
- Right.

04:44:02.588 --> 04:44:06.488
- But if you're just, like, curiously exploring
the world and reasoning about the world,

04:44:07.288 --> 04:44:11.168
the system punishes you. And I
hate that, 'cause I wanna get back

04:44:11.178 --> 04:44:13.988
to the Buckley and the
Black Panther discussion.

04:44:14.428 --> 04:44:14.828
- Yes.

04:44:14.868 --> 04:44:19.948
- And you were just saying opinions. But then
looking pragmatically at the situation,

04:44:21.028 --> 04:44:24.968
I have realized not to mention
politics unless I really care

04:44:25.008 --> 04:44:28.998
about an issue. 'Cause I realize
there's a cost. They'll, they'll

04:44:29.048 --> 04:44:31.237
put you in the blue bin or red bin.

04:44:31.248 --> 04:44:33.368
So you have to be more
deliberate about choosing.

04:44:33.888 --> 04:44:39.468
- Yes. And I've tried to be more
conscious of that, too. And then

04:44:40.528 --> 04:44:44.478
when I do choose to speak about it, it's usually
because I have deliberated, and I think-

04:44:44.698 --> 04:44:50.628
... "You know what? This is worth
it." Now, I say that, and then I also

04:44:51.768 --> 04:44:55.498
catch myself thinking the opposite
all the time, that I follow some

04:44:55.588 --> 04:44:59.068
person or another for whatever
their technical work, and then I-

04:45:00.228 --> 04:45:01.528
... learned or political

04:45:02.468 --> 04:45:07.068
standpoint. And I often do think,
"I wish I didn't know that." So,

04:45:08.008 --> 04:45:09.388
it's hypocritical-

04:45:09.448 --> 04:45:13.648
... then to think that of other people and
then go like, "Well, occasionally I will-

04:45:13.768 --> 04:45:16.918
... chime in on a debate
that's controversial,"

04:45:17.988 --> 04:45:21.188
and then you have to accept that there is a
price to that. And I do think you should,

04:45:22.188 --> 04:45:25.948
and more people should, be
somewhat conscious about that. And

04:45:26.008 --> 04:45:29.748
again, we're both contradicting
ourselves here, but saying like,

04:45:29.788 --> 04:45:33.148
"Wouldn't it be nice if we could talk about
politics in a more free and open forum and just

04:45:33.568 --> 04:45:37.528
intellectually turn ideas around?"
Yes, it would. Is that currently

04:45:37.568 --> 04:45:41.468
possible in the climate of
social media and so forth? It's

04:45:41.528 --> 04:45:48.818
quite difficult. Now, some of it could
simply also be a moment, and we could get

04:45:48.848 --> 04:45:49.468
past that moment.

04:45:49.688 --> 04:45:49.718
- Yeah. I hope so.

04:45:49.748 --> 04:45:53.528
- Maybe there is a way to get back to Buckley's
conversation with the Black Panthers,

04:45:54.728 --> 04:45:58.698
even on social media, in a way
where those discussions don't lead

04:45:58.748 --> 04:46:04.448
to the sort of just vicious mob cancellation
nonsense drives that we've had, right?

04:46:05.308 --> 04:46:09.148
I actually think we're closer
there now than we were five years

04:46:09.208 --> 04:46:15.288
ago. Like, the tribalism and the sanctions
those tribes were able to exact on

04:46:16.268 --> 04:46:20.188
heretics in 2020 was way greater than

04:46:20.208 --> 04:46:23.588
it was in 2025. Certainly within tech.
Now, I know if you're in academia,

04:46:24.348 --> 04:46:28.428
I mean, try going into the sociology
department at I don't know, Harvard and

04:46:29.408 --> 04:46:33.228
mention some right-wing ideas. I don't think it's gonna
go over very well, and I don't think you're gonna

04:46:33.268 --> 04:46:36.578
recover from it quickly. But
in tech and in many other

04:46:37.068 --> 04:46:40.928
business domains at least, it
is now possible to have an

04:46:41.728 --> 04:46:46.628
opinion that does not square with what the
consensus was in 2020. That's for sure.

04:46:47.388 --> 04:46:51.268
- Well, there's some drama in the Rails
community. There's some drama elsewhere.

04:46:51.728 --> 04:46:55.888
Is it, is it all... How did that
turn out? Is that all okay?

04:46:56.608 --> 04:47:02.408
- In the sense that every single round
of drama shrinks. So it's like we've,

04:47:03.228 --> 04:47:07.208
we had this peak infection of
mega drama around the same

04:47:07.288 --> 04:47:11.268
time every other community had a
peak infection of drama around

04:47:11.488 --> 04:47:15.168
2020. And then you just see, like,
every time there's a new flare up,

04:47:15.728 --> 04:47:19.428
it's less. And the kind of
people who are ideologically

04:47:19.468 --> 04:47:23.188
wedded to sort of that struggle
just shrinks and shrinks and

04:47:23.248 --> 04:47:24.328
shrinks. And at this point,

04:47:25.558 --> 04:47:29.537
one of the best things that happened
for X, in my opinion, was that Bluesky

04:47:29.608 --> 04:47:32.938
and Mastodon came around.
Because it kind of was like this

04:47:32.988 --> 04:47:41.988
honeypot for just the most
vicious individuals on X

04:47:42.248 --> 04:47:46.028
and Twitter at the time, right?
Who just congregated here in an

04:47:46.108 --> 04:47:50.037
ever-shrinking echo chamber that just

04:47:50.088 --> 04:47:53.968
went through purity cycle after purity
cycle and shrank every single time to the

04:47:54.008 --> 04:47:57.808
point that there were just not a
lot left. And the people who are

04:47:57.828 --> 04:48:01.928
left now there, they're just
out of sight, out of mind. And

04:48:02.848 --> 04:48:06.808
that's not to say that X is some sort of
perfect place, or there aren't firing squads

04:48:06.828 --> 04:48:10.488
occasionally there, or there aren't... But
there's less of it. Like, every single

04:48:10.528 --> 04:48:14.008
time I've been pulled into a
Mastodon, or not been pulled in, just

04:48:14.468 --> 04:48:18.108
spectated from afar, thread, a Bluesky
or whatever, it was like, "Oh,

04:48:18.308 --> 04:48:21.188
shit yeah, this is what Twitter
used to be like. Jesus."

04:48:22.108 --> 04:48:28.037
And now you can actually be on
X, as I am, and, like, 90% of my

04:48:29.128 --> 04:48:33.058
engagement is about cool Linux
stuff, cool technology stuff. "Look,

04:48:33.168 --> 04:48:37.148
I discovered a new computer. Can you see how
fast it is?" And I can have those conversations

04:48:37.168 --> 04:48:41.068
with other people who are just also excited about
computers. And then there's, like, 10% of it

04:48:41.108 --> 04:48:45.058
left that's like, all right, I'll chime in on something that's a
little controversial, and we can have a little discussion there.

04:48:45.068 --> 04:48:47.608
And then we can just go back to
being excited about computers.

04:48:47.648 --> 04:48:51.088
The algorithm is actually very good
at finding stuff that you will find

04:48:51.128 --> 04:48:54.037
interesting, or s- I shouldn't say
that, engaging. Sometimes it's

04:48:54.088 --> 04:48:58.068
enraging, but it is engaging, and
therefore, revealed preference

04:48:58.108 --> 04:49:01.728
is that most people want a For You page.
They don't want just a following feed.

04:49:01.868 --> 04:49:04.968
- No, but they're addicted to
the For You page, right?

04:49:05.048 --> 04:49:05.817
- They... Yes, yes. That's true.

04:49:05.898 --> 04:49:08.908
- I think an LLM, like, if we had a strong

04:49:09.028 --> 04:49:12.838
LLM doing the feed that's
personalized, it would do much

04:49:12.848 --> 04:49:16.567
better. The problem is how to deliver
that at scale is extremely difficult.

04:49:16.728 --> 04:49:20.408
- Well, the problem is the reward function

04:49:20.708 --> 04:49:24.528
is engagement. And as soon as the
reward function is engagement-

04:49:24.538 --> 04:49:27.468
... engagement's gonna follow
base instincts, and here we go.

04:49:28.108 --> 04:49:31.978
But I also don't wanna be so
negative. To me, and I get

04:49:32.108 --> 04:49:34.478
we all have our own
personalized algorithms,

04:49:35.928 --> 04:49:39.898
and sometimes it gets a little much. But I
would say, on average, over the last 10 years,

04:49:39.928 --> 04:49:43.268
this is the best X has ever been.
And I say that as someone who cares

04:49:43.308 --> 04:49:47.238
about a lot of technology talk,
a lot of Linux talk, and a lot

04:49:47.248 --> 04:49:51.198
of that. That that's able to
happen now in a way where

04:49:51.228 --> 04:49:54.738
it doesn't constantly get gatecrashed
by people who wanna drag it into

04:49:54.748 --> 04:49:57.008
some political discussion-

04:49:57.018 --> 04:50:02.608
... I find to be really awesome. I find
actually X to be a great place to find that.

04:50:02.668 --> 04:50:06.608
Now, it's also a place to find other things,
and we all have our own algorithms, so you

04:50:06.648 --> 04:50:10.298
can't make any declarative
announcements about what

04:50:10.468 --> 04:50:14.348
X is or what it isn't. Your feed is probably
totally different from my feed, and it's totally

04:50:14.388 --> 04:50:16.068
different from someone else's feed, right?

04:50:16.448 --> 04:50:20.208
- Yeah, I can't comment on this because

04:50:21.428 --> 04:50:26.368
because of the nature of the fact that
I interview a wide variety of people,

04:50:27.168 --> 04:50:28.648
my feed is probably just a-

04:50:28.768 --> 04:50:31.708
- Is it, is it good for your feed or is it bad
for your feed that you have to have it right?

04:50:31.768 --> 04:50:31.998
- It's bad.

04:50:31.998 --> 04:50:36.108
It's bad 'cause I could be like, "I'm
eating this apple. It's so delicious."

04:50:36.248 --> 04:50:39.928
And then a bunch of people will show up,
"Well, it's because you're a Zelensky shill-

04:50:40.048 --> 04:50:40.198
- Yeah, right.

04:50:40.198 --> 04:50:43.528
- ... or you're a Putin shill, you
piece of shit." It's like, all right.

04:50:44.968 --> 04:50:45.518
I'll just go back to the-

04:50:45.528 --> 04:50:49.448
- I mean, I occasionally have
a little bit of that where

04:50:49.488 --> 04:50:53.408
you just go like, "Wait, what? What are we
talking about? What are you... Why would

04:50:53.448 --> 04:50:57.098
you show up in this way?" This is one of the reasons
why I actually really enjoy podcasts, because I have

04:50:57.128 --> 04:51:00.728
found that even in long-form writing,
when I make long-form arguments,

04:51:00.848 --> 04:51:03.957
people hear it in whatever voice
they have in their head for me.

04:51:04.436 --> 04:51:08.156
And that often gets pre-programmed
in advance of the arguments,

04:51:08.276 --> 04:51:11.936
just like, well, this guy
is a, whatever, fascist.

04:51:13.376 --> 04:51:16.946
And then they hear the words and they
read the words in those names. When they

04:51:17.296 --> 04:51:21.186
see or hear the actual tone of
how I'm presenting the arguments,

04:51:21.276 --> 04:51:24.776
they may not agree with those arguments. I
mean, many of them, of course, obviously don't.

04:51:24.796 --> 04:51:29.186
But it's much harder to just
dismiss or hate someone like that.

04:51:29.916 --> 04:51:33.756
At least that's what I've found, and that's the response that
I've gotten, that people are like, "Well, I had a certain

04:51:33.776 --> 04:51:39.736
opinion and then I heard you on Lex. That
actually sounded much more reasonable."

04:51:39.756 --> 04:51:43.656
- But even more than that, I would
love it if people just exercise

04:51:44.956 --> 04:51:51.896
this kind of approach to other people of,
like, assuming they're a good person.

04:51:51.956 --> 04:51:52.756
- Yes.

04:51:53.016 --> 04:51:56.956
- And then, and if they have an
opinion you disagree with, they're a

